      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>CRUEL TEMPTATION.</head>
        <p>CRUEL TEMPTATION.</p>
        <p>Florence tlodgkinson, -</p>
        <p>Author of " TIN3TL AND GOLD," Ex., &amp;a,</p>
        <p>..lIAPTER IV,</p>
        <p>THE rubicon was crossed, the die. was cast;</p>
        <p>'Eldred Jasper was gone for ever, supposed be sleepins fatho:ns deep beneath the sea. wr )Ie Heren—ja.r,,Ezz plane-sing to Yorkshire under the care and escort of her second cousin, the Earl of Alandale. From the moment of seeing on Plymouth platform The man she feared more than aught else on earth the girl's decision was taken; for weal or woe she was Helen Jardine, she had taken her dead friend's name for good and all, she would not, must not look back. Lord Alandale was quite a new experience to Helen (she must henceforth be Helen in this story as well as•to her own little world) ;</p>
        <p>cradled in poverty, with a sad childhood spent - - - - - -</p>
        <p>With a father who was one of life's failuses, it happened that she had seen few specimens of the high-bred English gentleman, and known none intimately. This tall, fair-hairN Earl, with his dark brown eyes and broad open forehead was a type of which she knew abslintely nothing. She had read Messrs. Melville and Brett's letter, she had been present at the subsequent iterview between them and the heiress, but 2 le knew painfully little of the Jardine family h story and she was vaguely afraid of betray- ing her ignorance if she entered into con- versation with Lord Alandale, so she was strangely silent the first part of that 10r..,; journey to London. After an hour had; passed her companion dropped the newspaper he had been readily, and moved to a seat opposite Helen's. He put her silence down entirely to shyness and the shock she had lately undergone, and he wanted if possible to make) her feel at her ease and at home with him before she met others</p>
        <p>of the family. "I think, if vrei are not too tired to listen," he began kindly, "that I had better tell you a little about ,our mutual relations before you reach London. I fear in any case you will feel very strange and lonely after leaving your adopted country, and of course the shock you have gone through and the loss of your friend must make you very downhearted." "I am quite well enough to listen," said Helen gravely; "perhaps I ought to tell you I know nothing of my owr story except what the Melbourne lawyers told me. My-my father never spoke of his English home. I did not even know he was the son of a noble- man, he only told me his family had cast him off on account of his marriage with my mother and that when he knew that he was dying he wrote to my grandfather-his letter was never answered." "It never reached my uncle," said Lord Alandale; "it was suppressed by his daughter•- in-law, Mrs. Charles Jardine." "I thought my uncles never married." WI "Viscount Jardine, the heir, never took a wife, but both his brothers were married, William, who came next to him in age, had no children ; but Charles, the youngest of the family, left one daughter, who, but for your existence, would now be mistress of Alandale: At the time your father wrote home every- one believed lie had been dead for years, and Mrs. Charles Jardine had persuaded herself that her daughter must be the Earl's heiressJ I am not saying one word in her defence, for it was a cruel and dastardly act ; she stole your father's letter, and hoped that, his first appeal having been disregarded, he would make no future attempt at a reconciliation with his family."- " And how was Mrs. Jardine's perfidy found out ? " asked Helen anxiously. "By a singular chance. She did not destroy the letter she had stolen, she only concealed it. Later on she admitted what she had done to her daughter Magdalen, with instructions to keep it a secret from everyone ; but Magdalen is a Jardine through and through, and her answer Was to take the letter to her grandfather and beg him to pardon her mother. "Lord Alandale was furious'; he banished his daughter-in-law from the Hall and would never see her again. There is no doubt he would have instituted a search for you at °nee, but he was in very bad health at the time and so delayed it. In the end he never recovered sufficiently to make the effort, and it devolved on his lawyers." "I hope he provided for Magdalen," said Helen as a sort of lump rose in her throat at the thought of how she was robbing Magdalen of her rights. . "He left her ten thousand pounds, and she lives with her mother in one of the mpre rural suburbs. I need not say that we would all of us have offered her a home, but Magdalen is very staunch ; she fully admits her mother's sin, but she is beautifully loyal to her. She insisted on making confession and atonement, but that once dove she cast in her lot with her mother's. The chances are that you and Magdalen will never meet, but you must not imagine she grudges you your inheritance : on the contrary, when, the news of the wreck of the 'Cygnet' first came, and we thought for a little de that you had gone down with the burning ship, Magdalen told me she still hoped you might have been rescued, not only for your* own sake, but because she had no wish to be mistress of Alandale. She said she was not cut out for a grand lady and that she much preferred her quiet life at Kok." " Is she engaged to be married? " • Lord Alandale smiled. "What a strange question I No, Mag- dalen is twenty-four and I never heard of her having even a fancy for any man. In the old days, when most people thought she would be her grandfather's heiress, she had plenty of suitors, but she never favoured any , of them ; she's a nice girl, and I do believe a , good one, but I don't think a thought of love or marriage has ever entered her mind." "I am glad she does not live near Alandale," broke out Helen. "I should not like people to be always comparing me to her and hating me just because I was not Magdalen." "You need not fear that. Your aunt, Lady Mary Jardine, is appointed your guar- dian until you marry or reach the age of twenty-one, and she will make a home for you at Alandale Hall. I have a house in the neighbourhood, but, except myself and Lady Mary, you are not likely to meet any of your relations; so you will be the only Miss Jar- dine-in Yorkshire, and you will find your aunt most anxious to make you happy for your father's sake.". "Do you remember him ? " asked Helen impulsively. She was fully conscious that if Lady Mary Jardine wanted to talk about her brother Aubrey and hear the story of his Australian life her own position would be trying. "1 do not," replied Kenneth. " You see, he was never much at Alandalu alter his marriage, twenty odd years ago, and I am only 'thirty; but I suppose a child's_ memory is capricious for I remember your mother perfectly, she was my nursery governess ; my another was very fond of her, and could never unslerstand why Lord Alandale was so angry at the marriage ; Miss Grey was a lady by birth and education. I have heard my mother say tune after time that site was fully Aubrey's equal in all but wealth and station.- " Does your mother live with you ? " asked -11elen. " Shall I see her ? "</p>
        <p>lie slic,ok his hJ.• ".She as been dead some years,: she was</p>
        <p>the wife. of Abe Earl's youngest brother, and so she was really very little older than his children; she and your aunt, Lady Mary, were great friends." 'lt WIS -oe quite ntt ~ vi-llen we get to London, I suppose?" said Helen. "Shall we go on to Yorkshirl to-night? " ` No ; Lady Mary instructed me to take you to the quiet family hotel where she always stays when she is in London. I am going down to Kew, but I will come and fetch you to-morrow in good time for the morning express." _ _ _</p>
        <p>Are you going to see Magdalen ? "Yes." he said cheerfully. "She and I are very good friends. lam the only member of the family Mrs. Charles Jardine has never tried to quarrel with, and she doesn't object to my visits. Between ourselves, Helen, I think Magdalen has a very dreary life, and I like to try and brighten it a little.' The ; quiet family hotel" was near the Strand ; it was past seven when they reached it. Lord Alandale confided his cousin to the special care of the manageress and then went back to the waiting cab. A strange sense of loneliness seized on Helen as he drove away. "If I had had/ a brother like that there might have been a chance for me. Oh, what • would Lord Alandale think of me if he knew the truth ? lam ten times more wicked than Mrs. Charles Jardine ; she sinned to benefit her child. I have stolen a dead girl's name and robbed a living one of wealth for my own sake, and that only." The Earl was punctual to his promise, and by half-past ten the supposed cousins left King's Cross for the North. Helen was still wearing the plain, untrimmed serge skirt in which she had left the "Cygnet," and the coat and hat purchased for her at Marseilles ; there was not the least attempt at smartness or fashion in her appearance, and yet Lord Alandale, himself a most fastidious man, decided that she looked a worthy mistress of Alandale Hall, and that her aunt would •be proud of her. _ _</p>
        <p>It was a long journey before they reached the Attie wayside station which was the near- est lib Alandale Hail; even then they had before them a five-mile drive; Lori. Alandale felt conscious that a fresh fit of shyness had seized upon Helen, and did his best to cheer I her.</p>
        <p>"You °must be very tired and cold," he said quietly, "but we shall soon be home now, and you may be sure of a warm welcome. Of course," he went on in a lower tone, " every- thing will seem new and strange to you at first, but I hope that you will soon grow used to England and English ways and not feel homesick for Australia."</p>
        <p>" I feel frightened," said the girl franklyd "Suppose Lady Mary does not like me I Think of all the thousands of miles I have come to be among strangers " "I hope we shall not seem strangers to you long," was the kindly answer. "Now this is Alandale village; that white gate you can see over there leads to Woodlands, my little homestead, and the Hall is not 'two miles further on."</p>
        <p>They were there at last. Helen was only conscious of a long rambling mansion of grey stone with a vista of light auil warmth visible from an open door. Lord Alandale gave her his arm, and led her through a broad oak hall lined on either side with a long file of waiting servants to the place where an elderly lady, dressed in thick silk and crepe, 'stood ready to receive her.</p>
        <p>"This is Helen, Aunt Mary," said Lord Alandale, who generally addressed his cousin in this manner. "I am afraid she is very tired and cold."</p>
        <p>Lady Mary put her arms round the trembling *girl and kissed her. No words could tell how she dreaded the arrival of the Australian niece, whom she expected to find (such is insular prejudice) ill-bred, conceited and vulgar, but one sight of the beautiful wistful face dispelled her fears ; from that hour Helen had her own place in Lady Mary's heart. ,</p>
        <p>"Welcome to Alandale, my dear," she said quietly; "welcome to your father's homes You do indeed look tired, you must come straight upstairs to your own room ; tea shall be brought to us there. Kenneth," to the young Earl, "we shall expect you in to dinner at half-past seven." Lord Alandale never quite knew what passed at that first talk between the stranger aunt and niece, but when he returned as bidden to dinner two hours later he found only Lady Mary in the drawing-room. "Helen has gone to bed fairly worn out," she told him. "I am afraid all she has] gone through has told on her terribly." "Don't you want to be looking after her? demanded Kenneth, "instead of dining with me? Say the word and I'll go home. My old housekeeper is used to my little ways and won't think it wonderful my changing my mind and being in to dinner after all.' "Nonsense," said Lady Mary. "Helen is far better quiet and alone; besides, Kenneth, I really want to talk to you"' But yet she waited to do so, if the subject was Helen Jardine, for Lady Mary hardly mentioned her name till the dessert was on the table and the liveried servants had retired ; then she looked at her kinsman with a troubled expression in her frank, kindly I brown eyes. "Well," demanded the Earl, "are you disappointed? What do you want to ask me about Helen? There are half a dozen questions in your glance."d I am not disappointed," said Lacy Mary quietly, "but I am very much surprised. She looks years older than twenty and she might be English, she seems so 'used to our</p>
        <p>ways." " I fancy girls develop early in the colonies," said Lord Alandale thoughtfully, for it had struck him, too, that Helen seemed very womanly for her years, "As for the rest, Aunt Mary, I assure you Australia is very like England—only bigger. Lady Mary laughed, and went on with her subject. " Helen is not in the least like 'Aubrey, Kenneth, but she is much better looking than I expected. When two handsome people marry their children are generally plain." " She is more than good-looking, she is beautiful; but, Aunt Mary, Helen's is the saddest face I ever saw.' When I looked at her and thought of all sill must have suffered to bring that wistful expression to her eyes I felt more angry with Mrs. Charles Jardine than I ever did before." - - -</p>
        <p>" Yes, but for her, Helen Would have come straight to us on her father's death instead of having to work for hen l living. Miss Murray (our old acquaintance), who knew • Aubrey and his daughter in Melbourne, wrote to me that I should find Helen one of the brightest, merriest girls in the world, but I can't .say, Kenneth, that I agree with her." "Think of all Helen has gone through," he answered. " She has been face to face with death. and seen the friend she loved taken from her side. Captain Maitland told me Helen was the bravest woman who had ever sailed with him, she actually gave up her place in the last boat that left the 'Cygnet' in order that a husband and wife might not be separated, and she was the only woman on board when, just before the, wreck went</p>
        <p>vo. Laa., srly. Curi,ell. quite a remote epoch in her life and even the memory of the kindly faces of those who had been so good to her ,on board the i" Stella" was fast fading into the distance. Was she happy at Alandale ? Did she regret the one false step taken to secure a home, a name, and safety for herself ? For the life of her Helen could not have answered those questions, often as they wre in her mind.</p>
        <p>She had learned to love the old grey Hall almost like a living thing. To the girl who from early childhood never had a real and lasting home there was a never-ending delight in looking at all the treasures which dead and gone Jardines had amassed and feeling that they were hers and hers only, that so long as she lived no reverses of fortune could rob her of them, while at her death they would go to her unknown cousin Magdalen. That was the one drop of comfort in Helen's lot when remorse seized on her ; she was only robbing Magdalen for a little while. At her death all would be restored to their real owner, and when she first came to Yorkshire the girl felt so weary and heart- broken that she verily believed death could not be far distant. But Lady Mary's -kindness and affeition, the perfect freedom from worry, the intense rest and peace of her new home did so much for Helen that it came on her with a shock on one of those fair April days, when she saw her full length reflection in one of the long pier glasses, that she looked ten years younger, and so much better in health that she must surely be going to get quite well after all. Lady Mary 'caught her gazing at her own fair, face with half-frightened eyes. " Well," asked the kindly aunt, " do you feel satisfied, Helen ? Won't you agree 'with me that Yorkshire has made you a new creature ? " " I don't think I ever had so much colour," replied Helen, " and I am getting positively fat."</p>
        <p>Lady Mary laughed. " Nothing of the sort ; you are thin still, though not the walking skeleton Kenneth brought me home six weeks. ago. Why, Helen, when I first saw you I thought you looked more like thirty than twenty ; now you are a girl again, and what is more, my dear, a very beautiful girl, too." Helen blushed crimson. There was nothing she desired less than to be beautiful for beauty brought offers of love, proposals u;</p>
        <p>that, Aunt Mary. I am quite con- IntZtijilisi I am ; I have the strongest possible aversion to the thought of being married." " Your mother married at eighteen," said Lady Mary, "and worshipped her husband. Your father gave up home, prospects, country. for love, and I never heard that he regretted it ; yet you, the child of these two, appear to detest the thought of love and marriage." "Not for other- people," said Helen, "only foil myself. I have had a good deal of trouble in my life, Aunt Mary, and now I seem to have drifted into a peaceful harbour ; can't you understand that I want to stay and rest and enjoy the quiet and freedom from worry instead of rushing back to the world and its cares."</p>
        <p>"I never in all my life heard a girl of twenty talk as you do,"" poor Lady Mary declared in amazement. "Do you mean to tell me that you have had a love affair and it ended badly? " " Oh, no--but I have seen a good deal of unhappy marriages, and I am afraid." Lady Mary sighed. " There have been Tardines at Alandale for centuries," she said dolefully, " but it look to me as if the old race would die out. You are twenty. your cousin Magdalen is four years older. Alandale himself is just thirty ; you might all have married long Ago, yet not one of the three seems to think of it."</p>
        <p>_ • " Is not Lord Alandale engaged ? " " No." Lady Mary spoke quite sharply. " I used to think he would marry Magdalen because, he was so sorry for her when her mother's crime was found out ; I even asked him if she was to be the next Countess Of Mandate. and what do you think he told me ? That he looked on Magdalen as a sister ! " Helen went away to get A-eady for her drive. She was 'going into Thornby,* the nearest town, to change some books at the library and buy a little new music. As, she fastened her bat and coat there was a 'strange thrill of pleasure in her thoughts for which she could not in the least account ; she had not heard any good news, Why should she feel glad ? And then it broke on her with a shock that all the world looked glad because Lord Alan- dale was not going to marry Magdalen. " I must be the most selfish creature in the Arorld," Helen told licreif. After robbing Niagdalen as I have done I ought not to grudge her a husband, but all the same I'm thonkful she's not noble to be Alandale's wife.</p>
        <p>She was close to the water's edge, and looked out upon the wild expanse of ocean t, what if she had been laid to rest beneath those waves instead of her friend, surely, surely it would have been better. Then she remembered Alandale and the happy, peaceful home she had there; perhaps life was best after all. She turned to climb the uphill path ,which led back to the town, arid then, quite suddenly, without a moment's warning, a man's hand clutched her arm, and a voice she had hoped never to hear again, spoke her name. " You ! "</p>
        <p>If ever loathing, horror and dismay were all, concentrated in one word they met in that brief monosyllable. Helen Jardine stood as one fascinated, her eyes in spite of herself gazing into those of the man upon whose face she would gladly, thankfully have never looked again. And in truth he was not an inviting object for the eyes of a refined and beautiful woman, as she saw him now, a man between thirty and forty, with close-cropped hair, bullet- shaped :o.ead and thin, cruel lips which by trick or habit he had, compressed so con- tinually that they now appeared as little more than a seam across his face.</p>
        <p>" Yes, it's me," he returned. " I daresay you hoped I was dead and , that you were free to math the world as you pleased, but I'm very nmeh alive, my lady, and so you'll find." " Why have you come here ? " she asked, and her voice trembled. " Was it--to look for me " I heard you'd gone to be a school teacher out in AnStralia," he answered, " and it was too far to go after you, even if you were likely to be worth following ; but it seems I made a big mistake ; no school teacher's salary paid for the clothes you're wearing. What have you been up to? Found a hus- band with plenty of yellowboys, perhaps ? " " Would you insult me? " she asked hoarsely. "Do you think it likely I would marry i " " Oh 1 I don't know. IPalways went by facts, old girl, and I can see pretty plain you've fallen on your feet. Of course, I must stand to share the profits of your little game, whatever it is."</p>
        <p>Oh, that she had gone down with the loomed " Cygnet :' ! Oh, that she had neve :aken her friend's name and place ! Better poverty and starvation; better by far l.th__.__thn this:</p>
        <p>"I'm waiting for An answer," he said sut lenly, finding she did not speak, "and I meat to have it."</p>
        <p>" Tell me one thing first—why did pay come here ? "</p>
        <p>" Well, I don't know that I mind youi knowing. A friend of mine told me this district was a likely place for the business I'm best at ; I;n1 keeping quiet ,at present till get back my good looks a little, then he will come down and help me and we shall be at our old work again."</p>
        <p>"But," he went on, not heeding that one briefs word, "now I find you in clover things are different. Of 'course, what's yours is mine, and--" She interrupted him. " Richard,' she said in the low, musical voice which Lady Mary thought her niece's greatest charm, "you don't understand. I'm not the great lady, the rich woman you suppose; I'm living on the brink of a preci- pice, any day may see me fall over into the abyss beneath; a word from you would send me there, but to speak it would not benefit yourself." The man she called Richard (he passed as Mr. Norton in Thornby) looked at her in surprise before he said : "I suppose you mean you are playing a game of your own? Well, I didn't think you were that , sort; but if you are, at any rate, you need not look down on me. hold my tongue right enough, for a considera- tion." _</p>
        <p>" Won't.you leave me ? " "No, I won't, and that's flat. What's more, I'll follow you home, and enlighten the, people of the house as to your history ,up to four years ago, unless you do two things. " What are they ? " . "Tell me where you are staying and the name you are known by, that's the first. The next is that you meet me to-night after the household have gone to bed and bring me twenty pounds." The amount was a random sum; he did not guess what she was doing or how much money she might have at command.</p>
        <p>"nr bring you ten pounds," she said bitterly ; " I haven't twenty in the: world. Do you know a house called Alandale Hall ? " " Rather ; it's one of the show places of these parts. Are you living there ? What are you up to ? " . " I will tell you to-night. Be under the big cedar tree near the West Lodge at eleven. I will come there then with the ten pounds."</p>
        <p>"If you don't, l'llgo sttaight- up to the house to-morrow and tell Lady Mary Jardine what sort of person she's engaged to wait on her neice ; that's your billet, I expect ; maid to the heiress."</p>
        <p>Helen answered nothing. She turned back to meet the pony-cart with a ,leaden weight at her heart ; surely of human creatures she was the most miserable. (To be continued.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <p>down, tne survivors were taxen on by the ' Stella.'"</p>
        <p>"You need not defend her, Kenneth," said Lady Mary, a little irritably. "I am not speaking against her, but she is not in the least like what I expected. Aubrey was a: weak, yielding sort of man, given to put off his burdenskon anyone who would carry them, and his wife' was nothing but a merry, light- hearted child; one would not have expected their daughter to be so grave and careworn." "That will pass," said Lord Alandale. "You will be proud of Helen yet,. I can tell you. Everyone on the ' Stella ' 'looked on her as a heroine." "I shall be fond of her," agreed Lady Mary, "but I shall have a hard task to make her happy. She looks to mite as if she had never known what it is to be gay and light-hearted." " Captain Maitland told me she was pas- sionately attached to her companion, that when she came to herself on board the 'Stella,' and they had to tell her Miss Jasper had perished, she was so overcome that the doctor feared she would not get over the shock." "Miss Murray mentioned in her letter that they were very much attached," said Lady Mary; "they had lived together in. the same flat for nearly a year and were like sisters. Of course, Kenneth, I don't wish to seem heart- less, but I think it just as well for us that something occurred to part them. Helen would never have grown really one ,of our- selves, never have felt quite at home with us if she had had Miss Jasper always at hand to ' confide in, and besides, Miss Murray expressly said she knew nothing of the young lady's own people ; they might have proved most ,• objectionable." "Objectionable or not. I think they ought to have news of her fate," said Lord Alandale. "Captain Maitland told me that he had not the name or address of any of her friends so had not been able to communicate with them." " I will ask Helen to write to them as soon ; ' as she seems a little better," was Lady Mary's answer. "As she and Miss Jasper lived together for more than a year she must know what relations the dead girl had."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>CHAPTER V.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER V.</p>
        <p>IT was March when Helen Jardine came to ! the old grey Hall, and by .the time that the April sunshine was waking the early spring ; flowers to life in the beautiful gardens of . Alandale it seemed to her that she must have br n Yorkshire at least a year. for the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <p>marriage, aria tor love ana marriage there f'd be delighted to hear she was going to marry was no place in her life. , 1, duke, but 1 should hate any woman who "It was, very kind of you to be troubled aad a nearer claim on Kenneth than I have." with such a miserable creature as I was," she "He has been so good to me that I can't told Lady Marv. " I shall never forget your give up. his friendship. It's bad enough to goodness, never, while I live."— • =low that he was friends with Magdalen "It wasn't much to do for my own brother's years before I knew him. I couldn't bear to child, and We have a good many arrears of think he was going to be her lover." kindness to make up to yon, Helen. I only Very fair and beautiful looked the ,youtig mistress of Alandale when she cathe down- wish I could lirve foretold, you would recover your health and good lobics ki quickly." stairs. Lady Mary had bought an extensive "\Vhy," asked Helen. " does it matter ? " outfit for her niece scion after the girl arrived, " You strange child I You ask such plain and the garments in it were far more costly . 'business-like questions. It matters in this and numerous than those which had gone way; ii ,I had foreseen how soon you would be down in the ill-fated "Cygnet." It was near- the beautiful Miss Jardine' I should have ly six months now since the Earl's death and arranged to spend at least part of the season the days of lengthy mourning were out of in London that you might be presented, fashion, so already Helen was allowed soft at one of the June drawing-rooms." greys and white to relieve her black dresses. T For it was still in the clays of Victoria the To-day she had a black coat and skirt of rich Good, and as yet evening courts had not faced cloth embroidered in white, a white tie succeeded the time-honoured afternoon . draw- at her throat, and a snowy wing in her soft ing-rooms. felt hat, a simple costume enough, yet on( "Then I ant very glad you did not foresee which had cost close on twenty pounds, foi it, Aunt Mary," answered Helen. " I think the material was expensive and the styli to go to London would make me wretched, and first-rate. Helen thought of the shabb3 as for being presented I can't bear the very little teacher of St. Monica's awl sighed. thought of it ; I should 'be sure to do some- " Sometimes it still seems lust a dream from thing stupid and disgrace you. which I must wake up presently to the old "My dear, I am not afraid of that; you are days of poverty." -' one of the most graceful girls I ever met, and I I Miss Jardine was considered by her aunt expect You will make a great success when far too precious to be allowed to drive by your debut comes. As things are, it must herself, though she had been taking lessons be put off till next spring; by that time you in the art since coming to Alandale. A groom will have grown used to the idea, I hope, but was always at her disposal ; he had the reim . , I wish you would tell me now why you dislike i now, and would take his young mistress to th( it so much." 4 I town of Thornby, going on .to the station tc</p>
        <p>And the answer was a strange one to come from a girl of twenty. " I hate the thought of the publicity; besides, being presented means going to balls and parties. I have never been even to a carpet dance in my life, and I simply dread beginning." " You are the strangest girl I ever met," said Lady Mary. " If you 'never go to balls or parties, if you keep aloof from all society. as your words imply you would like to do, how are you ever to be married.? " There was no pretty blush of girlish modesty ; Helen's colour never changed, and. she answered her aunt as frankly as though she had been asked her opinion on a point of millinery : " Never at all. I hope, Aunt Mary. not the least desire for a - husband.' " Helen I " said poor Lady Mary, horrified at the confession : ' what in the world do vot,</p>
        <p>enquire for a parcel Laily Mary expected frorr London while she did her shopping, ane returning for her later. Either the shopping took less time that Helen expected, or the man had mistaken hei instructions, for there was no sign of the pony. cart when she had finished; accordingly leaving a Message that it should wait for hei at the Sun, the hotel to Which her purchase: were to be sent, she took the winding path that led down to the sands, to her lasting regret, since the trouble she had dreaded ever since her return ,to England was, alas I brought about by that one step. Helen loved the sea, not to sail on it for days and nights, but to stand on the shore and watch the waves as they came in breaking over each other in their haste, their soft monotonous music always seeming to bring a strange peace and rest to her throbbing heart</p>
        <p>I have</p>
