      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>LANCASHIRE CONGREGATIONAL UNION.</head>
        <p>LANCASHIRE CONGREGATIONAL UNION.</p>
        <p>Annual Meeting of the Liverpool District.</p>
        <p>PROCEEDINGS AT WYCLIFFE CHURCH.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the Liverpool District of the Lancashire Congregational Union was held at Wycliffe Church on Tuesday, Mr. T. S. Eccles, district treasurer presiding over an excellent attendance.</p>
        <p>FINANCIAL STATEMENT. DECREASE IN INCOME.</p>
        <p>Mr. ECCLES said he was very pleased to meet them again at the annual district meeting: They had been engaged in some exciting work during the last few weeks, and he thought they might congratulate them- selves,whatever their views on the fiscal question might be, that they had now a Parliament which was more in sympathy with' their aims and ideals than the last Parliament bad been. (Hear, hear.) They were very glad to see Mr. Albert Spicer in the House of Corn. moos, and to see that thete were also a good many of his character and stamp there. They could naturally look for greater sympathy and might congratulate themselves upon the result of the election. (Hear, hear.) He was very sorry he was not able to congratu- late himself or them upon the result of their work during the year so far as contributions to the Union were concerned. He came there with quite a bowed head, and felt very depressed in regard to the state of their finances. Tney had done badly all round. There were very few churches which had given as much as last year,and a good many had given less, while the grants that had been made to aided churches had been larger than lalt year, and that afternoon they would be asked to give still larger grants. The contributions for , the last year were £557, against £592 the year before, £589 the year previously, awl £845 the year before that. The grants were £585, as against £567 last year, £5OO the year before, and £550 the year before that. While they were increasing their grants they were decreasing their contributions to the Union. He did not think it was creditable to the Liverpoool District, and he thought they should at any rate pay_their expenses and keep up their grants to the general Union. The decrease was not confined to one church, but ran throushout almost the whole Union. There were several churches which were heavily burdened with debts, but with 'a good deal of beating up they could get morepaoney than at present. One church which had improved its contribution was the one to which be belonged, and that had been got entirely through subscriptions. That must be the basis of their work in all churches. The subscribers in many churches were falling off. He did feel that their position was a very sad one, and they ought to be doing better. He woski like to impress upon all the importance of their work. Surely if they supported missions abroad they ought to support their missions at home and if things wont on as they were at present they would not be able to keep up their missions and it would be cruel to have to withdraw their subport. (Hear, hear.) He hoped they would do their utmost to make their accounts tat the end of the year look better. (Hear, hear.) Mr. Chamberlain said they had been going through." a dreadful time," but he thought they might have done a great deal better. They had some small churches who were doing very well, and were raising money by vari- ous means and if they all did their utmost he would be able next year to present a better balance sheet. (Hear, hear.)</p>
        <p>ELECTION OF OFFICERS. On the motion of Mr. J. B. LEACH, seconded by the Rev. J. E. SE WELL, of Liverpool, Mr. T. S. Eccles, of Huyton, was re-appointed distriet treasurer.—Mr. Eccles said he was pleased to accept office again. The CHAIRMAN said he would like to ask the Rev. John Yonge to accept the secretaryship for another year. Last year, he told them he would like to resign, but as he was in good health again, he did not think they could get the work done any better than he could do it. He proposed that Mr. Yonge be re-elected.— The Rev. J. Simon, of Liverpool, seconded, and the motion was heartily carried. The following were elected members of the Executive for the district : - Revs. W. L. Roberts, Liverpool; C. B. Tinker, Wigan; J. E. Sewell, Liverpool • A. Scott, Southport; G. E. Cheeseman, Huytein • 'Messrs. G. Oliver Jones, Waterloo, and J. B. Leach, guyton. Upon a vote being taken the Rey. John Yonge was elected as the first chairman of the district. The Rev. JOHN YONGE said that nothing was further from his desire that he should be appointed chairman. but having been elected he would do his best. (Hear, hear.) GRANTS. The following grants to the respective-churches were agreed to :—Berkeley-street, Liverpool, £5O; Rice-lane, Liverpool, £35; Woolton, Liverpool, £35; Howe Bridge, Leigh, £5O ; Orrell, £5O; Skelmersdale, £6O; Hampton-road, Southport, £6O; Cockhedge, War- rington £5O; Widnes, £45: Abram, Wigan, £10; Pemberton, Wigan, £3O. In respect to Ainsdale (Southport), Canning-road (Southport), and Gidlow (Wigan), thegrants asked for are to be considered by the Executive Committee. The returns for Cockhedge Mission showed that the average attendance at public worship was 114 on a Sunday, and 29 on a week day. The church members last year were 53, while the pre- sent number is 59. The teachers now number 17, scholars 340, the average attendance being 286. THE NEXT MEETING. It was decided that the next annual meetitk be hel at Leigh.</p>
        <p>VOTES OF THANKS. At the conclusion of the meeting an adjournment was made to the Wydliffe Memorial Buildings where a sub- stantial tea awaited the visitors. After tea Mr. Eccues proposed that a hearty vote of thanks be given to Mr. and Mrs. Y enge and the Wycliffe friends and others for the way in which they had been entertained. Be did not think they could have been better, entertained. (Applause). Dr. KARFOOT seconded, and the 'motion was heartily sppoved. , The Rev. .7. YONGB returned thanks. He said that all their friends in Warrington had felt it a great pleasure and honour to entertain the visitors that day. He considered that the visitors had done them a great honour by the way in which they had done their work,and he was sureby their coming there that day,they had done them a poer of good. He hoped the time would not he long when they would again welcome the Liverpool district to Warrington,and they would have the pleasure of seeing a larger number of people present. (Applause).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>DO YOU FM WINTER? How to Avoid its Perils.</head>
        <p>DO YOU FM WINTER? How to Avoid its Perils.</p>
        <p>Why is winter more deadly than the milder seasons of the ye tr, and more unhealthy? And why do people catch cold sooner and sanr more frori colds in winter than at other times ? The simple retson is that winter weather pats a heavier strain on the system. H3n3a if your vitality is lowered, if you are run I down, an-emio, or just convalescent sfter influenza or fever, let that familiar chilly feeling, those frozen hands and feet, that slight hoarseness, and that cutting pain in your back and chest be your danger signals. By keeping the stomach healthy and the liver active, bile beans enable you to defy winter's grim attack on your system. The secret of robust health is rich red blood, the supply of which is only limited by impaired digestion. When your food is doing you no good, how can strength and bodily heat be maintained? Bile Beans practically transform the food into warm red blood, which then circulates on its mission of life and heat to all parts of the body. Concerning influenza, Mr. Albert Facer, of Long Buckby, North Hants, says :—" I had influenza seven. times—used to have an attack every year. I was pulled through my last attack, four years ago, by bile beans, which so built up my health that I have never had influenza since. Solely by the use of bile beans, which are a splendid preventive tonic, I have been able to check the recurrence of the malady."</p>
        <p>Nurse Wiseman, of 36, King-street, Eccleshill, Brad- ford. who suffered terribly for many months from neuralgia in the head, says :—" The pains increased in winter until they were unbearable and they affected my health seriously. Tonics were quite useless ' • but the neuralgia was cured long before I had finished a single box of bile beans,and I have never had neuralgia since." Mr. Thomas Ikrady, of Mansfield-street, Liverpool, contracted a severe liver-chill. Months of medical treatment left him weak, dibilitated, and still suffering as much as ever. "A friend advised bile beans," says Mr. Brady," and in a short time they rid me of all pain brought back my appetite, and renewed my strength."' Mrs. Marshall, of 52, Haydon-street, Swindon, says : After suffering for twenty years from rheumatism in my arms and ankles, I was quite cured by taking a course of bile beans. My hands now straighten out quite naturally, and loan get about anywhere. That is three years ago, and I have never had a return of the • old trouble."</p>
        <p>Such living testimony as. the above is final proof that bile beans are an ideal winter tonic, and the safest family medicine. But nute that the four cases just cited were cured by the genuine bile beans, and not by a spurious imitation or cheap substitute.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>WRETCHED WARRINGTON HOME.</head>
        <p>A WRETCHED WARRINGTON HOME.</p>
        <p>Child's Death Inquired Into.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Police Station on Saturday Coroner Brighouse held an inquiry touching the death of Annie Ryan, the eight-months-old child of Patrick and Catherine Ryan, Warburton's-yard, who were in custody on the charge of neglecting deceased and other children. Mr. A. Browne watched the proceedings on behalf of the N.S.P.C.C. The, Coroner read the police report which stated that the parents of the child had been under the obser- vation of the N.S.P.C.C. for several months previous to Pridaywjanuary 19th. On that data Inspector Cox visited the house in company with Dr. Peacock and a police constable. The deceased child was found in a very emaciated condition and on the recommendation of Dr. Peacock it was removed, along with two other children, to the Workhouse where it died four days afterwards. If the jury considered that death had been brought about by neglect they had to decide whether it was such gross neglect as to justify them sending the parents for trial for manslaughter. On the other hand there might be neglect which was punishable under the Act of Parliament, but which would not justify commital for manslaughter. Elizabeth Ward, wife of Patrick Ward, general labourer, 7, Stoney-yard, was called, and said she last saw the child alive a fortnight ago, when it appeared very weakly. She had known the Ryan's for the past four months' and had never seen any act of neglect towards the deceased.</p>
        <p>Inspector Cox stated that on Friday, January 19th, be visited Ryan's house. The parents were both out. A girl aged ten years had the deceased child in her arms. It was dressed in a ragged night-gown, was in a filthy state, and was very emaciated. There was a little fire in the grate but not a particle of food was to be found in the house, neither was there any furniture. In the bedroom was a beep of strawand a filthy chaff bed. The 'deceased child was examined by Dr. Peacock and after- wards taken to the Workhouse.</p>
        <p>Dr. Peacock said he had made a post-mortem examina- tion of the deceased. The body was very thin, being almost devoid of fat. In his opinion, death was due to consumption of the bowels. He could not state definitely that the child would not have died of that complaint but for the wronful act or omission of some person. •Dr Smith corroborated. _. _</p>
        <p>The Coroner, while expressing the opinion that there was a case for the N.S.P.C.C., said the evidence would not justify commital for manslaughter. A verdict in accordance with the medical testimony was returned.</p>
        <p>PARENTS SENT TO GAOL. , At the Borough Police Court op Tuesday Patrick and Catherine Ryan were again brought up on the charge of neglecting their three children.—Mr. A. Browne, who prosecuted on behalf of the National Society for the Prevention of Cruelty to Children, said it was an extremely bad case, and the fault was not through poverty. The man could earn regularly Rl. a week as a carter, but V would not work. On the 19th of January Inspector Cox visited the house, and in consequence of the condition of the children called in Dr. Peacocke, who ordered their removal to the Workhouse Infirmary. On Tuesday week the prisoners were brought before the magistrates, and were remanded, it being then intimated that the youngest child, aged eight reotths, was in a dangerous condition. The child, died on Wednesday last, and at the inquest on Saturday Drs. Smith and Peacocks reported that it died from consumption of the bowels, but they were not prepared to say that death was eaused by neglect. The jury returned a verdict in accordance with the medical evi- dence. The case had been under the supervision of the society for twelve months, and prisoners had been warned several times. On the occasion of the last visit the Ouse was in a deplorable condition. Dr. Peacocke, in reply to Mr. Browne, said that in his opinion the neglect contrilpted to the death of the, child.—The magistrates sent the husband to gaol for six months and the wife for six weekk</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>ULCERS FOR 60 YEARS!</head>
        <p>ULCERS FOR 60 YEARS!</p>
        <p>Terrible Results of a Dog-Bite. ---- ENGLISH AND AMERICAN DOCTORS HELPLESS.</p>
        <p>Miraculous Cure by Zam-Buk.</p>
        <p>An extraordinary triumph for Zam-Buk has just been investigated by a reporter of the " Birmingham Sunday Echo." _</p>
        <p>It appears that Mrs. Minett, of 21, Sloane Street, Parade' Birmingham, suffered with chronic ulceration of the leg for more than sixty years, and she recently experienced a miraculous healing by Zam-Buk. She- says : "When a child of eight, I was bitten on the leg by a dog. A doctor cauterised the place, and a piece of flesh taken tom my shoulder was grafted Into the wound but it never healed up soundly. " When a Young woman I was being continually attended by the doctors. Sometimes my leg would appear better for a few days, hut only to break out into running sores worse than ever. I went into service. but had to leave for a Suffolk hospital, and for over three years I was an in-patient and out-pe.ti ent by turn I received no benefit. After I married, my leg continued to trouble me. At times the pain was almost unbear- able, and I could never get proper rest at nights. tried all sorts of remedies from the chemists, but could find no relief. „." This sort of thing went on year after year. I was always in pain, and the sore discharged continually. Twelve months ago I west to America to see my daughter, and during the voyage I had to keep my bed. Die ship's doctor gave me a plaster, which I had to take eff again, as it made the pain so intense. In Providence several medical men said nothing could ever do it any good. When I returned to Birmingham I went to the Dispensary for three months, but all to no purpose. " One day my youngest daughter brought home a sample box of Zam-Buk, and induced me to try it. With the first application I seemed to find ease, and further treatment with Zam-Buk did me so much good that I sent for a proper supply. One full box completed the cure, and my leg has not troubled me since. The curs is absolutely complete, and my general health is better than it ever was, too." Zam-Buk is unfailing in cases of ulcers, bad legs, inflammations rashes, eruptions, diseased ankles, sore backs, poisoned or festering gores, cold-sores, piles, eczema, scalp diseases, pimples,ringworm, running sores chapped hands, chilblains, colds and chills, chafed skin, cuts, burns and all injuries to, or diseases of the skin. All chemists sell Zam-Buk in is. 10. and 2s. 9d boxes (3i. tld, size contains nearly four times as muck as the Is. 1W.); or post free from the Zam-Buk Co.,Red Cross- street, London, E.C.</p>
