      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, February 3, 1906.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, February 3, 1906.</p>
        <p>WOMAN'S REALM.</p>
        <p>DRESS-FASHION-11031E.</p>
        <p>THE NEW PARIS HATS.</p>
        <p>White lace hats, trimmed with cocks' feathers, and large rosettes of jet resting on the hair, are among the fancies of the hour in Paris, writes "Lorna." Tiny black feather toques, perched on the top of the head, are adorned with long white plumes. Black and coloured velvet hats, decked with wreaths of feather trimming, look warm and cosy for January. Gowns for the Riviera are made with bands of fur, jet, or em- broidery placed around the hem.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>SHORT-SKIRTED EMPIRE GOWNS.</head>
        <p>SHORT-SKIRTED EMPIRE GOWNS.</p>
        <p>Empire and Princess frocks are first cousins, Both avoid a waistline, both depend on their beauty of line, and both need a good carriage and figure before they can be seen to advantage. They divide the reale' of fashion nearly equally at present, but there is no fear of monotony, says a Globe writer. The Empire frocks alone, within certain limits, are very varied, but it is a pity that we should not copy more closely the Empire gowns in the length of the skirt, which was in those days so often quite short, even above the ankle. This applied, not to walk- ing-skirts, which, curiously enough, appear to have been almost, or quite, touching the ground. but to visiting and ball-dresses. The ease and comfort must have been very great, and have conduced in no small degree to gracefulness of posture and carriage. DAINTY FLANNEL BLOUSE.</p>
        <p>In the accompanying sketch we have por- trayed a dainty, little blouse of soft,. thin French crepe flannel. It fastens down the front t-cder a narrow box-pleat, which is finished on one side with a frill of lace. A deep-pointed yoke forms the upper part of the blouse, and this is deco- rated with alternate rows of velvet straps and velvet-covered buttons, the " skirts " being quite</p>
        <p>plain. The sleeves are full round the shoulder, and gradually tighten in to fit the arm, being finished at the wrist with lace ruffles and velvet- covered buttons. For other occasions this model might be copied in silk or velvet, and in any case it would be advisable to mount it on a pro- perly fitted lining.</p>
