      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>DINNER AND DANCING GOWNS.</head>
        <p>DINNER AND DANCING GOWNS.</p>
        <p>The young girls are wearing charming even- ing gowns this season, says the Queen. Seeing , how many good dressmakers there are, and how determined all are to evolve something original out of .her inner consciousness, it is not so easy even in the case of the most charming debutante to evolve a, really suitable dress; tho great point to consider is that she should be be- comingly gowned. Net gauze and chiffon are the leading fabrics. These sound as if they were not very extravagant, but when they come, as they mostly do, to be embroidered, spangled, or trimmed with lace, it is a very different matter. The young people are invading the matrons' domain as far as richness of fabiics goes. It seems impossible to have a skirt too wide at the feet. There are any amount of narrow flouncings and ruffles inside the edge. The waists ore all made to look small by the cut and arrangement. All the bodices are pointed, or have the appearance of being so, by means of a band. Girls never look so well as in white. Some of the waistbands are of flowers, while bouquets are carried across the bodice to the waist, and so to the feet. Even garlands of flowers are in- troduced on to the lace hoods of evening cloaks, which are essentially graceful. ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>SMART INDOOR DRESS.</head>
        <p>SMART INDOOR DRESS.</p>
        <p>Our illustration shews an indoor dress that could be successfully achieved in any delicately- tinted cloth or cashmere. The draped bodice follows the rounded lines of the figure, and finishes at the bend with a couple of velvet bows. A V-shaped lace front, merging into a collar. is used for the neck, atid the draped part of the</p>
        <p>bodice falls over a tightly The double-puffed sleeves arranged in box-pleats, which and pressed between the two gathered skirt has two small adornment, both of which headed with narrow bands of WOMEN AND HOME PRODI7CTS.</p>
        <p>athered silk belt. have the fulness are only stitched puffs. The full- frills by way of are hemmed and velvet.</p>
        <p>'A writer in the Queen remarks that while men may theorise and wax excited over this contro- versy or that, and may vote for this party or that, women have a practical power left almost entirely in their hands by men, and that is the power of the purse. Without knowing much of tariffs and "Retaliation " or "Protection," women can, set, as the caterers for the house and family, exercise a very marked influence. Most English-made articles are better of their class than those of foreign make, always except- ing such things as are dependent on climatic conditions and peculiar or local skill in labour; and if English women would always prefer the British-made article to the cheaper (and gene- rally poorer) foreign-made stuff, they would con- fer a boon on our labouring, manufacturing, and commercial classes which would outweigh the utmost effects of Parliamentary enactions.</p>
