ntinue the welding process only divides it int* more numerous filaments than a bar of common iron. Ho recounts how the head of a great Yorkshire firm, famed among bar-iron makers, who called at his works, scouted the idea of disintegration of any bar-iron made by his firm by simply working it at a temperature below welling heat, but on one of his bars being hammered he was utterly astonished at it dividing for about a font of its length into a mass of fibres "forming a veritable birch. broom." Sir Henry' Bessemer also mentions that a bar of Bessemer mild steel, similar to the two bars of lin. square iron referred to above, were heated at the same temperature uncle!, the same hammer bet it simply became ex- tended into a flat undivided surface, without a crack or rift in the material. He commentE that these examples of forging below a welding heat serve to shew the imperfection inevitable 'to all puddled or, welded iron; while the steel ..yairmle also shows the continuity of narts re suiting from tile Bessemer steel or nomogeneoue ipe2 being formed into an ingot while the metal is in a fluid state, hence producing an un- divided and indivisible mass, however much it may be hammered. hot or cold. _._</p>
