      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>Prize Distribution.</p>
        <p>The annual prize distribution in connection with the Baptist Sunday school, Golborne-street, took place on Sunday afternoon when over a hundred prizes were dis- tributed by the Pastor to the successful scholars. Addres- sing the scholars the Rev. H. Dunnico spoke of the im- pottance of Sunday school cork and the good work the Bible class was doing. Mr. Freeman (superintendent), Mr. Elliott (secretary), and Mr. Pepper (secretary of the Bible Class), also addressed the scholars on the work of the Sunday school. The latter was the recipient of a cruet stand for the good work he had undertaken. Mr. Walker and Miss Carrie Hastings contributed solos their efforts being highly appreciated.</p>
        <p>Wyclife P.S.A. There was a good attendance at the Wycliffe P.S.A. meeting on Sunday. Mr. C. Eccles presided, and spoke upon "Acceptable service to God ; humility and sincerity of service." Mr. J. R. Wolstencroft, of Swin- ton, Wag the speaker, and his subject was " Jesu's power to save." Mr. A. Downes also took part in the meet- ing. The Secretary referred to the deaths that had taken place during the past week in two families which had been good friends to the society. Mr. E. W. Bind- loss had lost his wife, and Mr. E. Pickavance his sister, the late Mr. Pickavance, senior, being one of the founders of the movement in Warrington 13 years ago. To the families of the deceased, the brethren *sent their deepest sympathy and condolence.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>St. Paul's Church.</head>
        <p>St. Paul's Church.</p>
        <p>_ Special sermons were preached in St. Paul's Church, on Sunday by the Rev. Hawley Sharpe, of Liverpool,on behalf of the Waifs and Strays Homes of the Church of England of which he is the local secretary. Mr. Sharpe made forcible appeals for help, giving a large number of instances in which homeless children had been taken out of the streets' 'educated and had been given a good start in life. In the afternoon a "pound service was held, pounds of rice, sugar, tea, coffee, &amp;c., being brought by the congregation and placed by the clergy inside the altar rails, altogether no fewer than 250 packets being contributed. These were afterwards dis- tributed at the home in Brick-street, The collections amounted to £ll.</p>
        <p>Latchford Wesleyan Church. A choral service was held in the above Church on Sunday afternoon, presided over by Mr. William ' Roberts. Solos were rendered by Miss Lily Fairhurst, Miss Alice Woodland, Mr. E. Pearce and Mr. J. Willett. Messrs. E. Pickers and Fred Podmore accompanied on the organ. The Chairman, in his remarks, said that the afternoon's service was to aid the fund for a new Sunday school. In his opinion Sunday schools were very valuable assets in the training of the young people. He hoped the new school that was contemplated would be a moral and spiritual centre of blessing to many a youthful mind. A collection was taken at the close in aid of the Sunday school building fund.</p>
        <p>Rev. J. S. Hughes and Labour Representation.</p>
        <p>Preaching at Hill Cliffe Baptist Chapel on Sunday evening the Rev. J. S. Hughes referred to the question of labour representation in the House of Commons. In the sudden rise inthe representation of Labour, he said some people found cause for disquietude, and beheld in it an element of danger. It was, said they, the corning of Socialism of the worst type, which would bring in its train rebellion and anarchy, but such persons were needlessly alarmed, and the remarks were not compli- mentary to the intelligence and power of the working classes who formed four-fifths of the population, and were the muscle and bone of the Constitution. Such talk was sheer nonsence or hysterical screaming. The present upheaval had evolved the man with as hammer. Doubtless he looked formidable and his hammer was a nasty looking instrument, but they had no right to suppose that he was going to smash the Constituton with it, or look upon him as a dangerous character. While the labour man with his hammer would help to smash some cherished idols, such as class dis- tinction, privileges, &amp;c., he would be a powerful factor in keeping the ship of State in sound condition, and fastening down the planks, which might be loosed by stress or storm. There was nothing to fear but every- thing to be thankful for in the fact that we had the handy man of Labour on board the vesssel of State. Poverty and hard work were often a stimulus to the brain—there was a connection between plain living and high thinking. Property was not a proof of wisdom nor poverty a sign of mental degeneracy. The inequalities of life were real things, and if we removed them to-day they would be here to-morrow. These inequalities might ho summed up in four words—strength, talent, riches and rank. Nothing was more common than that riches and rank should be preferred to talent, and yet there was nothing more absurd—they could strip a man of riches and rank, but talent was indestructible and indeprivable. Thus it seemed to him that there was nothing to fear but much to reioice at in the coming of the Labour man into the councils of the nation, and he should be welcomed with open arms. Keep him out, and they stored dynamite for future anarchy and a Russian revolution, but give him a place in their councils and he would realize the responsibility and share in the development and defence.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>„</p>
        <p>THE LICENSING ACT, 1904.1 LITTLE ONE ONE SOLID SORE</p>
        <p>A year's working of this Act has probably convinced most people that this piece of Tory legislation is rightly dubbed" The Brewers' Endownent Act." It represents the most successful attempt to" grease the fatteth hog the more with lard " that any band of log-rolling poli- ticians has ever essayed. Luckily the Government that perpetrated it is defunct, and its party routed at the polls. But the Act is inscribed on the Statute Book of the country, and until repaaled or amended will continue its unwarrantable benefits to brewers. Since the famous Sharp v. Wakefield decision, the tenure of a public- house has been regarded as annual only. The Act of 1904 converted this annual tenure,no doubt with intent, into freehold, the aggregate value of which is com- puted at some hundreds of millions of pounds sterling. Can it be ilnagined that legislation which enhanced values fifty or a hundredfold was seriously intended as a temperance measure for the reduction of the number of licensed houses? If the Tory Government who passed it genuinely thought it was, then the country are well rid of such clumsy administrators. The Act creates a barrier against its own supposed object. In a large community like Liverpool its impotence is not so apparent as in small towns, although nobody will contend that the experience of the past year's working of the Act in Liverpool was satisfactory. In many places this " measure of temperance reform " is an absolutely dead letter, because the amount of levy is not sufficient for necessary compensation. Liverpool licensing justices, alarmed and warned by recent events, have this year decided to call for the full compensation levy, and a greater suppression of undesirable licensed houses may be expected. Experience has proved thatto call the Act a "measure of Temperance reform "is sheer nonsense ; it is an Act to enrich brewers and slowly, very slowly, to reduce licences. The brewers benefit in three ways: They get more money from their holmes, they escape lightly in contributing the amount of levy, and they pocket most and in many instances all the compensation. All through the chapter they play "heads I win, tails you lose." Even the trade organs complain that the levy falls disproportionately heavy on the legitimate licensed victualler —those who conduct their business on inde- pendent lines, and whose premises are colloquially termed "free houses." If anybody, these men aught to be encouraged ; but now they are suffering from posi- tive penalties which may squeeze some of them out of existence. Probably the brewer would not regret this result if he saw in it a possibility of extending the abominable tied house system. The tied- house tenant is in a pitiable position. To cover the amount of levy, the brewer increases his rent or by I adding to the price of the goods supplied. In one of these ways, sometimes both, he extorts from the tenant what the Act compels him to allow. The tenant is helpless ; he must submit or relinquish his means of livelihood. When it comes to a division of the com- pensation money these tenants—we speak, of course,not absolutely of every tenant of a tied house, but the vast majority—whose names are over the house as licence- holders, are again the victims of the greed of the brewer. They must accept whatever paltry amount the brewer chooses to give them, just as they must take to sell glucose and maize beer, vile " silent spirit whiskey," or" patent still brandy," as the brewer thinks fit to palm off upon them as high-class goods at firs.-class prices. The disproportion betwern the amount of compensa- tion the brewer receives and the tenant gets is in many cases startling. Here are a few instances :</p>
        <p>Total Tenant's Award. Share. Sailors' Return, Middlesbrough 1,900 ... 0 Waterloo Hotel, Sunderland B9O ... 0 18, Kempston-street, Liverpool Spinners' Arms, Bolton Telegraph Inn, Derby BOO ... 10 George and Dragon, Chester 450 ... IS 87, Overbury-street, Liverpool 31, Prince Edwin-st., Liverpool 29, Walnut-street, Liverpool 520 ... 20 23, Cardwell-street, Liverpool 3OO ... 23 96, Boundary-street, Liverpool 86, Scarle-street, Liverpool 450 ... 25 10, Newhall-street, Liverpool Cornwall Arms, Chester Three Tuns, Chester 214 ... 30 89, Thirlmere-road, Liverpool Robin Hood, Chester Dee Vaults, Chester 34, Knowsley-street, Liverpool 405 ... 40 George Hotel, Warrington 2,280 ... 70 Britannia Sunderland These are a sample of 128 licences dealt with. The- local cases, which are not the worst in the list, are given on account of the interest naturally felt in them. One point abundantly proved by the working of the i Act s the ridiculous inadequacy of the compensation fund to effect its professed object of suppressing exces- sive licences at a reasonable rate. As a consequence, half the number of authorities in the country have been powerless to put the Act effectively into operation. No other explanation is apparent. For instruice, Warrington last year was unable to extinguish more than one licence, and that one alone swallowed up more than the total amount of levy. Halifax had at its disposal only £l,BOO to meet £7,000 compensation. Like Liverpool Halifax called for only half the levy, but they found the whole would have been insufficient to deal with the most urgent cases. Plenty of other authorities have experienced similar difficulty. It is estimated that if the amount of levy remains as at present it will take 120 years to do what a member of the Council of the Victuallers' National Defence League declared was a reasonable reduction for oneyear in the whole country when,some years ago, he published a scheme for the immediate reduction of 64,000 ,licences.—" Liverpool Post and Mercury."</p>
