        <p>PRINTERS, BOOKBINDERS, PAPER RULERS, LITHOGRAPHERS, EXAMINER OFFICE, WARRINGTON</p>
        <p>The revolt against the con- THE UNIONIST tinned Leadership of the LEADERSHIP. Unionist party by Mr.</p>
        <p>BALFOUR grows daily. Angered by their smashing defeat at the polls, the rank and file are in search of a scapegoat. The failure of the ex• Prime Minister to hold his own seat at Manchester, and the subsequent attempts to find him a seat elsewhere, have set the friends of Mr. CHAMBERLAIN discussing the question of the Leadership of the party, with the result that Mr. BALFoun's qualifications for the office are being discussed in a by no means friendly way. We are being told that it is for the party itself to elect a Leader; and elaborate analyses of the opisions of the Unionist Members who have secured election are being published, with a view to showing that Mr. CHAMBERLAIN has a bigger following in the new Parliament than has Mr. BALFOUR. It is further pointed out that the choice of a Leader for a small Opposition is an exceedingly grave matter and that it; is only Mr. CHAMBERLAIN who can secure, that unity which is so necessary if effective work is to be done. Well, we may have been wrong, but we have had an impression that the Conserva- tive party was fairly united until Mr. CHAMBER- LAIN started his heretical propaganda, and that all the disunion had been caused because the "unauthorised programme " put forward by him failed to attract many of those who believe with the late Lord BEACONSFIELD that " Protection is not only dead, put damned." No doubt, of course, Unionists know better what has caused their defeat than we do ; but we should have said that the greatest factor was Mr. CHAMBERLAIN'S policy, and that those who wish to build up the party again on stable basis would have done well to recognise that fact, and revert to the former, and more sane economic doctrines held by the party, and still held by every living ex-Chancellor of the Excequer except Mr. ATJSTEN CHAMBERLAIN. But DO we are learning now, in the best approved style of the Tariff Reform League, that the real reason for the disaster which has overtaken Conservatives is to be found in the fact that Mr. BALFOUR refused to become a " whole-hogger,' and that unless he will now consent to join hands with Mr. CHAMBERLAIN he must not again be allowed to lead the party.</p>
        <p>All this is just what we expected would happen. It will be remembered that a fortnight ago we expressed the opinion that the events then happen- ing were doing a good deal to throw the remnant of the Conservative party into the hands of the apostle of Tariff Reform. We knew that, even if Mr. CHAMBERLAIN were "delighted at the best possible solution," he and his immediate followers would leave no stone unturned to prevent the party from being lei anywhere except in the direction of Protection. The bulk of the Conserva- tive party has always been Protectionist at heart, nod has only in the past refrained from adopting the policy because of a wholesome fear of the electorate. But when a leader of Mr. CHAMBERLAIN'S standing took up the cause there was great rejoicing among the old-fashioned Protectionists, and they are now persuading themselves that the recent defeat would have come about anyhow, that, in fact,they expected it, but that the pendulum will swing in their direction next time. Hence their great anxiety to commit the whole party now to Mr. OHAMBELRAIVEI keeping rather than that of Mr. BAtvouß, so that if by any chance they should get a majority at the next election they will be able to say that the country knew what their policy was, and has decided in its favour. The fear that Mr. BALFoUR is really anxious to rescue tho party fromlits Protectionist heresies is at the bottom of the intrigue to remove him from the leader- ship. All Mr. CHAMBERLAIN'S organs in the Press are doing their best to make it impos- sible for Mr. BALFOUR to remain at the head of the party. He is being alternately threatened and cajoled. One day ha is told that his nature is too delicate for the rougii and tumble of political life, and on another that his own good sense will toll him that he cannot hope to continue to lead a party which contains such a majority of ",whole-hoggers." Mr. Cnemaantaix is no doubt a very good electioneerer, he is no doubt pushful, but his diplomacy, whether in statecraft or else- where, fails to impress us. It is all too trans parent. He means, if possible, not only to load the Opposition—that he will do in any case—bu be means to be its elected and recognised Leader, with the reversion of the Premiership if ever hia party comes back again.</p>
        <p>These are joyous days for Liberals all over the country, and M Warrington particularly. Not only has the yoke of 20 years Tory domination in the town been broken, but an evsnt has happened within the last week which fills the cup of happiness to the brim. One of the drawbacks to Liberal organiz ition in War- rington has been the unsatisfactory building in Bewsey-street, which has for so many years been utilised as the party headquarters. As a club, it has long been a most comfortable meeting-place, but for political purposes it has not afforded the best facilities. Now, however, the greatest difficulty in the way has been removed' by the generosity of Mr. Frank A. Roberts, J.P,, who has signalised the great victo y gained at the recent election, and crowned</p>
        <p>A HANDSOME GIFT.</p>
        <p>his connection with local Liberalism by presenting the Club premises, lock, stock and barrel, to the members. This most handsome gift is very heartily appreciated, and will result in the memory of the donor ever being kept green by Warrington Liberals. The Club authorities can now set to work with light hearts and put their house in order. The unpretentiousness of the present building can very easily be changed, and a structure evolved which will be a credit to the party and a lasting monument to the generosity of a long-esteemed Warrington Liberal.</p>
        <p>When the minimum wage VOTING IN" THE for Corporation workmen TowN COUNCIL. was fixed some two months ago after a long debate,the sitting of the Council ended with a dispute about recording the names of those who had voted for 'and against the motion. Councillor Stott and Councillor Lord both demanded that the names should be taken, but they were too late. At a special meeting of the Council held on December 15th the Mayor adverted to this little question and ruled that no member had the right to demand the recoiding of the names of voters in a division either under the Municipal Corporations Act or the Standing Orders of the Council. The most curious feature of the matter is that for years the names of members taking part in an import- ant division have frequently been dictated by the Mayor and written down by the Town Cierk without request from anybody. But when a Councillor asked and demanded that the names should be recorded in this particular instance he was met with a refusal. We are very glad to se that on Tuesday Councillor Stott will propose that the following motion be added to the Stand- ing Orders: "If two members of the Council so request, the names of those voting for and against ..,Ft resolution or amendment, shall be recorded." It is absurd for any difficulty to be raised. No record of the Council proceedings is complete without the names of those who voted " yea " or "nay," and we hope the motion will at once be agreed to. At any rate the Press will take care- ful note of those who are for and against the proposal.</p>
        <p>There is no more gratify- ' THE JuNion log feature of local political LIBERALS. life than the success which is attending the Junior Liberal Association. It has already enlisted the active help of many ardent young Liberals in the progressive cause, and the Association may justly take credit to itself for having played no small part in the promotion of the great triumph of Liberalism in the Borough. Now that the elec- tion excitement has subsided, the Associa- tion is addressing itself with renewed earnest- ness to the educational work which is the main purpose of its existence. The importance of fitting young men to be able to defend intel- ligently their political faith cannot be too strongly emphasized. The junior Liberals of NY arrington will have ample opportunity of becoming educated in the aims and ideals of their party and there is every indication that they will take full advantage of it. At the meeting on Monday the President (Mr. E. Osborne) in a thoughful address sounded the key-note of the Association's endeavour. Pro- - gress, he said, must he the watchword of the Association and truth its shield, and he urged the members ever to defend the cause of their less. fortunate countrymen,</p>
        <p>Royal marriages have no Tim SPANISH longer the significance they ALLIANCE. had in days gone by. At one time the union of the ruling families of two nations was an event of the greatest international importance, being regarded as the token of an. alliance between the nations themselves. Nowadays the personal element in politics no longer plays such a part, and Royal weddings are—within limitations —t he pri- vate attairs of the persons most concerned. But if these thinks have lost much of their politi- cal significance,they stilt retain a social importance which cannot be ignored. The approaching marriage of Princess Ena of Battenberg with King Alfonso will mean that the British Court will in the future have a more or less close communica- tion with that of Spain, and they are bound I mutually to influence each other. The example of the Court is followed more or less closely by British society ; and we may expect Spanish ideas and modes to get more recognition here than they have. How far is this tendency to be welcomed? The Rev. F. B. Meyer cousiders it unfortunate, and speaks of the Spanish Royal Family as one of the least enlightened of Roman Catholic houses. The evidence of history, how- ever, is, generally speaking, in favour of contact between peoples; and, in any case, it must be remembered that British influence on Spain will probably be as powerful as her influence on us, so that we may do something to soften whatever bigotry may exist. On tlie other hand, Spain is a country from which we may have some- thing to learn. Its manners and customs, its language, its people, its literature, are less known to us than those of countries mucla more distant, I yet the people who conquered the Moors, and who produced a Cervantes, must he worth knowing more intimately.</p>
      </div>
