      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>RAILWAY INSTITUTE FOR WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>RAILWAY INSTITUTE FOR WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Recreation for L. and N.-W. Employes.</p>
        <p>Generous provision for the recreation of thek War- rington employes has been made by the London and North Western Railway Company,by placing at their disposal the recently vacated district goods offices. Mr. I. T. Williams, district manager, at once suggested and in company with Mr. W. N. Turnbull, superintendent of the line, engineered a scheme for the formation of an institute to provide the men, who number about 800, with a means for social intercourse and recreation, and also to give them facilities to improve their knowledge of the technical branches of railway work. Strong com- mittees have been formed especially to extend the work in connection with the St. John Ambulance Brigade and the Railway Temperance Union and already these two splendid organizations have been greatly benefltted. The whole of the building has been re-decorated and pro- vision has been:made fora billiard table,bagatelle,air-ritle range, and other games, and in addition there will be a smoke room, lecture room and a reading room with an abundant supply of literature. A happy idea put forth by Mr. Williams, and which has been acted upon, has been ta obtain a collection of specimens, &amp;c., which will indicate the industries and manufactures carried on in the Warrington District—practically the whole of central Lancashire. Generous promises of help in this direction have been received from all the principal manufacturers and this arrangement it is thought will " - prove highly interesting and instructive. The Institute will be conducted on broad lines, entirely free from politics or any contentious subject, and the formation of a strong committee (representative of the heads of the different departments in Warring- ton) is a guarantee for its good management. The movement has received the patronage and support of Sir Frederick Harrison, general manager; Mr. Frank Ree, chief goods manager ; Mr. Turnbull, superintendent of the line ; and the chief officers of the( company, who have shown their interest by intimating their intention to be present at the opening of the premises, which will take place during the course of the present month. One Pleasing feature of the moment is that the committee are showing a desire to assist the objects of the Beauti- ful Warrington Society and in time the building will be covered with creepers and the land surrounding it artistically laid out with shrubs and flowers. By the courtesy of Messrs. Croalleld and Sons a well attended and representative meeting was on Wednesday evening, held in the Assembly Room at Bank Quay. Mr. I. T. Williams presided and explained the details of the scheme. The utmost enthusiasm prevailed and it was evident that the men fully appreciated the efforts which are being put forth on their behalf.</p>
        <p>\I</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>PADGATE.</head>
        <p>PADGATE.</p>
        <p>WESLEYAN BIBLE CLASS.-LISt Sunday afternoon before a good attendance of the members Mr. E. England gave an address on "Immortality." Mr. England treated the subject from History—Nature— Reason—Justice—and Scripture, Messrs. Cotte Hall, London, Plimston and Seed, afterwards spoke on various points arising out of the address.—At the close Mr. England was warmly thanked for his address which had been highly appreciated.</p>
        <p>1</p>
        <p>lat RnQsts they ttleuE after</p>
        <p>* * * spite of Mr. Chamberlain's warning that" cotton ing " the past year has been one of record mos- for that industry. Mr. William Tattersall, in ~ i• ' Cotton Trade Circular" says the year's trading A A.'las been the most profitable on capital in the (); istory of Lancashire" ; and the facts he produces A'aBtif7 his conclusion that the year 1905" will never :pc forgotten by cotton spinners and manufacturers." Yernendous returns were made, and the figures of ,tinety limited spinning companies which publish 'accounts show a profit equal to 19 per cent. per annum liter allowing for full depreciation and interest on can capital. The shipment of, spun yarn and IPtton cloth shows striking inereases,while this growth la,.7sthe demand for our cotton goods goes on, we are !? doing more and more every year to help other o..ations to make the goods for themselves. In 1003 export of textile machinery was valued at 29,894 ; last year it had neon to £5,607,436. .urf these products, Germany bought nearly one million's °ltil from us; yet at the same time eur export of trns and finished cotton goods to Germany have 'atlY increased, tariffs and competition notwith- tand.in„ t, . .</p>
