      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <p>t</p>
        <p>LOCAL NEWS.</p>
        <p>Notice. The Warrington "Examiner" is now published on Friday afternoon, price one penny, and is on sale at the various newsagents at about 4 o'clock. Should any difficulty ba experienced in getting the paper the proprietors will undertake to deliver a copy at any address in Warrington by 5 o'clock on Friday after- noon without extra charge. Address all communica, tions to" Examiner Office," Bewiey-street Warrington Index to Inside Pages. PiGE .</p>
        <p>2. " A Cruel Temptation," serial. Lancashire Congregational Union Meeting. N.S.P.C.C. Prosecution. 3. Women's Chat and Fashions. Our Local Churches. The Licensing Act, 1001. Stockton Heath. 6. Football. "The House of Dread " by Hume Nisbet. '. Mr. King's Victory. Celebrations at Stockton Heath. Children's Corner. Coming Events The first annual concert in connection with the War- rington Secondary School will bg held in the Parr Hall on February 16th, the proceeds of which will be devoted to the school athletic club. The programme will include vocal and instrumental music and the performance of the extravaganza "The Queen of Hearts." (Wm. Yeardley.) The annual ball in connection with the Warrington Market Tenants' Association will be held in the Parr Hall on Thursday evening. Two balls have been arranged under the auspices of the Warrington Women's Liberal Association. The first will be held in the Parr Hall on Wednesday next and the second in the Lion Hotel on February 21st. A Band of Hope festival under the auspices of the Warrington Band of Hope Union will be held in the Parr Hall on Thursday week. The programme will include the coronation of the Queen, the plaiting of the maypole, chorus and action songs, and musical drills. Particulars as to prices &amp;c., will be found in our advertising columns. The programme at the Bold-street popular penny concert to-night will be submitted by Messrs. J. Hill, T. Boothroyd, Whittaker, and E. A. Sanders, and the St. Clement's minstrel troupe. The fifth annual concert in connection with the Warrington Male Voice Choir will beheld in the Parr Hall on Saturday, February 17th. Selections will be !given by the Manchester Orpheus Glee Society. A. meeting of the Warrington Junior Liberal ' Association will be held at Gregory's Cafe on Monday. Brewster Sessions.</p>
        <p>The annual borough licensing seseions will Jae held at the Police Court on Friday next. No announce- ment has been made of any new applications. Petroleum Oil in barrels and drums delivered free.—J. S. GARLAND, 51 to 55, bridge st.,Warring. ton.—Advt. Death of Mr. B. Kenyon. We regret to announce the death which occurred on Wednesday morning of Air. Benjamin Kenyon, licencee of the Green Man Inn, Green-street. The deceased gentleman,who was 01 years of age, had been ill for about a month, the cause of death being heart trouble. He was very well known and respected in the town, having kept the Green Man Inn for upwards Of twelve years. He was a native of Leigh and in his earlier years had an experience of Canadian life. Mr Kenyon was a prominent Freemason, being an S.D. of the Gilbert Greenall Lodge. He was the iChairman of the Warrington and District Wine and Beer Sellers'Association,and showed a great interest in its work. In politics he was a staunch Conservative, although he never took an active part in political affairs.—The Inneral took place at the Cemetery this (Friday) afternoon, when many townspeople attended as a mark of respct. The Freemasons were largely represented.</p>
        <p>The, Labour Bureau. The following are the returns up to to-day(Fray): Registered. during the week, 16 ; making the total, number registered 122 (113 men, 9 women). During the week 7 men have obtained work; now on register. 105 (96 men, 9 women). The following is a list of the different trades: —General labourers, 37; builders, 8 ; wire trade, 6 ; plasterers, 1 ; ironworkers, 6 ; furnace stokers, 1 ; clerks, 2 ; tanners, 5 ; joiners, 1; painters, 22; fitters, 1; horsemen, 2; French polishers, 1 ; printers, 1; fustian cutters, 2 ; charwomen, .7 ; gardeners, 1; rubber mixers. The Bureau is open daily (Saturdays excepted) from 9 to 12 for men and from 2 to 3 for women and every opportunity is given Ito the unemployed to have their names registered. During the week several employers have expressed their approval of the bureau and their willingness to co-operate. Prompt and careful attention is given to. applications for men,and it is hoped that all employers ! will avail themselves of the opportunity of obtaining i the best: class of workmen with the least possible trouble.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>Examinet, Saturday, February 3, 1906.</head>
        <p>The Examinet, Saturday, February 3, 1906.</p>
        <p>Stealing Doormats. At the Borough Police Court on Thursday William Rankin was sent to gaol for six months on charges of stealing two doormats value 20i., from houses in Wintuarlegh-street. Prisoner was seen by Police Constable Sankey offering the mats for sale, and the officer, being suspicious, arrested him.</p>
        <p>UNDER TliE CI.OOIC for Good BOOTS.—Advt. Collision in Buttermarket Street. _</p>
        <p>---------- -- On Saturday afternoon an enclosed milk float, with glass sides, front and back, driven by Mrs. Elizabeth Seed, of Risley, was proceeding \in the direction of home and when opposite Academy-street it collided with another float belonging to Patrick Fitzpatrick, West-street, warrington. Thq pony attached to the enclosed float fell, and the shafts of the conveyance snapped, the lady passengers being badly shaken. They were taken into a shop and after a while were able to continue their journey.</p>
        <p>Penny Popular Concerts. Another enjoyable entertainment was provided on Saturday evening at the penny popular concert in the Bold street Wesleyan schoolroom, when Mr. George W. Weaver presided over a fairly large audience., A' pianoforte solo was excellently given by Miss John- son, and songs by the Misses Plumpton, C. Atherton, and Mason and Mr. H. Plumpton. Several recitations were contributed with effect by Mr. A. Nadin. The humorist was Mr, J. A. Barker, Manchester, who met with great sucpess with his songs and recitals. The accompanist was Mr. G. W. Weaver, jun.</p>
        <p>Up-to-date Fire Places.—J. S. GARLARD, 51 to 55, Bridge et., Warrington.—Advt. Motor-Car Accident</p>
        <p>An exciting incident occurred in Bridge-street on Monday. A London motor-car was proceeding along Mersey-street, and when at the corner of Bridge-street it ran into the shop front of Messrs. Greenhalgh and Company, upholsterers, smashing one of the cellar lights. The driver of the car states that the accident was caused through the steering gear failing to act. One of the occupants was thrown out, but sustained no injury. The amount of the damage to the window was immediately paid, and the car proceeded on its journey._</p>
        <p>Cheap Travelling. Charles Kershaw, last from Liverpool, was charged at the Warrington Police Court on Saturday with travelling on the Cheshire Lines Railway from Liver- pool to Warrington without having paid his fare.— A ticket collector saiclzhe was collecting tickets, when he found the prisoner lying on the floor in a third- class compartment. He said : "I am caught." He handed him over to the foreman.—The foreman stated that the prisoner told him he had travelled from London to Liverpool without a ticket, and was going back in the same way but got in the wrong train.—A fine of 10s. and costs was imposed.</p>
        <p>Warrington Chess Club. A meeting of local gentlisnen interested in the game of chess was held on Thursday evening at the King's Cafe, Bridge-street, when it was decided to form a chess club. Mr. E. L. Fleming was elected President, and Mr. J. It Wood of Friar's Green House Honorary Secretary. A committee was also appointed including these gentlemen along with Messrs. H. Bond, F. A. Dunne, J. Newcombe, and J. Warburton, who will meet to draw up a few necessary rules and arrange for recognised nights for play. During the evening a number of friendly games were indulged in by those present at' the meeting.</p>
        <p>As for ClANEiciliMes HIGH-CLAM MINIMAL WATERS.--Advt. . Gymnasium Ball., • The members of the Borough Gymnasimn held their annual ball on Friday evening, about 150 being present, and it was a • pronounced success. The " Gym " had been very tastefully decmated for the occasion by Mr. Ralph Johnson, and was much admired. Mr. Alf. Starkey's band gave a capital selection of music. Mrs. Atkinson catered with her customary ability, and Mr. Alderman Tinnion ably discharged the duties of M.O. The success of the ball is, in no small measure, duo to the indefatigable efforts of the hon. secretary, Mr. Harry Holloway. Iron Fencing or every description. Lowest prices.—J. S. GARIAND, 51 to 55, Bridge St., Warrington.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Warrington Tobacconists' Affairs. The case of Evans v. Stanway, Limited, a debenture holders' ;action, was before Vice Chancellor Leigh. Clare, at the Lancashire Chancery Court at Manchester on Monday. Mr. Eastwood appeared in support of an application for the appointment of a receiver and manager of the Company, which carried on business as wholesale tobacconists at Warrington. He said the debentures, amounting to about .000, had become due and the appointment he asked for was necessary because of the seizure of the property of the company by the execution creditor. The application, which was not opposed, was granted.</p>
        <p>Animated Pictures at the Parr Hall. The New Century Company have continued their exhibition of animated pictures at the Parr Iran during the pet week and the large audiences have testified to the popularity of the entertainment. The pro- gramme is a varied and highly interesting one and includes many of the most up-to-date examples of animated photography. Views of the Royal visit to India are a striking feature and 'there is also an admirable picture of Sir George White unveiling the war memorial at Liverpool. The humorous items, of which there is an admirable display, are particularly good. There will be an afternoon performance to-day (Saturday) and the falai entertainment will be held this evening. LADIES' BEST GLACE BOOTS AND SHOES, UNDER THE CLOCK.BR/DGE-STREET.—Advt. The 'Apollo Choir. A successful year's work was under review at the annual meeting of the \Varrington Aiiollo Prize Hale Voice Choir which Was held in the Druid's Hall last Friday evening. Mr. Nathan Cook presided over a large attendance. The Secretary (Mr. R. W. Cook) presented the annual report which stated that during the year the choir had been successful at three eisteddfodau, viz., Earlestown, Rhyl and Chester. The following officials were elected :—trainer and conductor, Mr. 11. Berrey treasurer, Mr. George Morrall; secretary, Hr. R. W. Cook; auditors, Messrs, W. Critel;ley, and W. Parker. In the course of a brief speech the chairman spoke in the highest terms of the services of Mr. Berrey which he said had ran- tributed in no small degree to the success of the choir. Besses a' tit' Barn Band.</p>
        <p>. This famous band of Lancashire cotton spinners which has won so many prizes and had such a flatter- ing reception from the French public, has recently made some specially fine Columbia Records for use on all talking machines using gold moulded cylinder i records. Disrecords also are n course of preparation and will be soon announced. The records now avail- able are 201396. " The Cossack March," stirring !I music, bright and lively and a splendid record ; 201397 " Punchinello March." tuneful, swinging march, played with the vim and procksion for which this baud is celebrated; 201400 "Blue Danube Waltz," always a .favorite ; 201398 "Ida and Dot Polka," cornet duet, with superbly executed triple tonguing, full band accompaniment ; and 201399 " Ora Pro Nobis," trombone solo, accompanied by the entire band. These records and others of the pantomime hits and •latest popular successes. are contained in the February supplement of Columbii Records just issued Dealers everywhere have the new records, or a post card for '! Price Book 20 " will bring a copy of the list if sent to the Columbia Phonograpl4 Company, Gen,' 89, Great Eastern Street,,London, }X.</p>
        <p>PUBLIC BEtsIEFIT.—IINDF,R THE CLOCK FOR BEST BOOTS IN LANCASHIRE.-=Advt.</p>
        <p>St. Ann's Congregational Gathering. The annual congregational gathering in connection with St. Ann's Church was held in the echo lroem, Drinett-street, on Monday evening, and was in every way a success. The proceedings opened with tea, I and a highly interesting concert followed. IThe opening item was a pianoforte duet by, the Misses D. Harrison and Elsie Padmore, and later in the evening a pianoforte solo was given by Mastpr'S. Reynolds. Miss E. Clarke rendered a couple of songs, entitled " Dream memories" and " Flight of ages," very successfully, while Mr. C. Savlge was heartily applauded for his singing of " Maid of Malahar." Mr.J. Johnson contributed "Star of Bethlehem," in a pleasing manner, while pro- minent features of the evening, were the songs, " The British Fleet," and " Cavalry,' given by Mr. Thomas. A couple of coon songs, entitled" The old banjo,' and " Shine, shine, moon," by the Misses A. Collin, E. !Butterworth, D. Harrison, M. Jackson, D. Harding, 1 and N. Vincent were creditable performances. Uti well as their action song, " The washer-woman." Other pleasing items were two violin and piano trios by Mr: J. ,Johnson, Master F. Stanley, and Miss M. Johnson. The choir was in attendaime and rendered " Blow, ye gentle breezes," and "Good night,beleved." The chair was occupied by, the Vicar, the Rev. J. Davies. The proceeds were in aid of the parochial funds:</p>
        <p>Cruelty to a Horse. At the Police Court on Monday, Daniel Taylor, 12, Alexandra-street, was summoned for cruelly ill-treat- ing a horse by working it while in an unfit state on January 22 ; and William Dunbobbin, 17. Gas-street, was summoned for causing it tobe worked.—lnspector Gibson, R.S.P.C.A., said that at eleven o'clock on the date named he saw Taylor driving a brown cob attached to a greengrocer's cart. The cob was going practically on three „legs. It had a badly diseased fetlock, and sprained and contracted tendons, and was only fit to be slaughtered. Defendants were ordered to pay the‘costs of the summons. Oil Stoves, Coke Stoves, Lamps, 6:c.—J S. GARLAND, 51, to 53, Bridge St., Warrington.—Advt. Sankey Wire Mills Cricket Club. The annual meeting of this club was held at the Brooklands Hotel on Monday evening. Mr. Frank Taylor presided over a large attendance. The secre- tary's and treasurer's reports which were considered highly satisfactory were adopted. The following officials were elected :—President, Mr. Ernest Houghton; captain of the first eleven, Mr. Frank Taylor ; sub-captain, Mr. H. Clarke ; captain of the second eleven, Mr. R. Hewitt ; sob-captain, Mr. H. Hawthorn ; treasurer, Mr. H. Clarke ; secretaries, Messrs. W. Deckhouse and R. W. Cook ; cornatittke, Messrs, W. 'Parton, N. Cook, S. Partridge, E. Whit- field and P. Bladen. The vice-presidents were heartily thanked for their support and were all re-elected. A splendid list of fixtures has been arranged and the coming season promises to be a very succrssful one. ' Galvanized Booting Sheets, Roofing Felts, &amp;c. Large stock.—J. S. GARLAND, 31 to 55, Bridge St., Warrington.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Proposed Welcome to Mr. J. A Seddon, M.P. ' On Monday evening it is proposed to 's°ire a special welcome at Cairo-street School to Mr. J. A. Seddon, M.P., the new member for the Newton Division and a social has been inaugarated for the purpose at which the workers and friends in the Warrington and sur- rounding districts are expected to be present. There will be a little speechmaking interspersed with music and refreshments and It is anticipated that a pleasant eveninr, will be scent in rejoicing over the victory accomplished. Church Society Lecture.</p>
        <p>In connection with the Warrington Cluirch Society a lecture on the "Liverpool Cathedral" was delivered in St. Clement's Mission, Bank-street, on Thursday M evening by the Rev. Charles Harris, .A. There as a.good attendance, the Rector (Canon Willis) presid- ing. The lecture proved a highly interesting one, and was illustrated with a splendid series of lantern slides. Details of the progress of the scheme up-to-date were given, and it was stated that it was hoped to complete the laying of the foundations by the end of this month. —A hearty vote of thanks was accorded to the Lecturer and Chairman, on the proposition of the Rev. Canon Morley Stevenson, seconded by the Rev. H. A. Lester. Sunday Trading. We understand that the Warrington Tradesmen's Association have approached the Watch Committee with a view to putting a stop to Sunday Trading by enforcing the Lord's Day Observance Act. The Watch Committee had the matter under consideration on Thursday but came to the conclusion that the Lord's Day Observance Act, which dates back to the time of Charles H. is entirely unsuited to the require- ments of the present time. The committee, however, think there should be some general enactment upon the question of Sunday trading. It is at present open to any person after conforming to the requiremen ts of the Act to take proceedings against others who do not comply with it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>Workingmen's Mission, Thewils-street</head>
        <p>Workingmen's Mission, Thewils-street</p>
        <p>The second Band of Hope entertainment of .the season was given in the schoolroom on Tuesday even- ing before a large audience, over which Mr. Nelson presided. The first item was a pianoforte duet by Miss M. Titchett and Miss E. Dale. Solos were rendered by the following :—Mrs. Tanner, Mr.. D. Gittens,a nd Master T. Tanner, and were much enjoyed. Recitations were given by Amelia Cleve- land, Elsie Appleby, Mary Gilbert, Ethel Millington, and Thomas Richards. The dialogue "Miss Sprigg's Work," which was given by Getty Morris, Bessie Foster, and Annie Lawton, was mach appreciated. Other items were: Gramaphone selections by Mr. H. Paden ; dialogues " Dolly's Medicine," by Edith Preston and Hannah Preston ; " Beverages," by Amelia Cleveland, Mary Hart, Polly Fletcher, and Beatrice Johnson; and Boys' favourite games," by Arthur Dennett, Arthur Morris, Albert Hunt, John Schule, C. Forster, J. Long, and J. Hipkiss. The concluding item was a dialogue by members of the, Young 'Ladies' Class entitled " Wanted, a Cook." Mr. F. Atkinson acted as accompanist. PUBLIC BENEFIT, UNDER THE CLOCK FOR BEST BOOTS IN LANCASHIRE.—Advt. " The Queen of Hearts." Most people in the district will be familiar with the huge pictorial poster of " The Queen of Hearts" with Mr. George Robey as "the Queen" Those who have not seen this curiously entitled pantomime ought to lose no time in making a trip to the Royal Court Theatre, Liverpool. ,The inimitable Robey alone is worth the outlay, for there is no more amusing comedian on the stage, and he is at his best in "The ! Queen of Hearts.' He has the house in roars of ' laughter the wholeime he is on, and much of his " business' is nel and striking,. In addition there are Messrs. T. A. Shale and John A. Waillen, who contribute an important share to the gaiety of the piece. The principal boy is Miss Millie Lindon, who struts about in true cavalier style and sings tune- fully. Miss Maud Darling, as the princess is charmingly natural, vivacious and pretty. She is a vast favourite with the audience and deservedly so. Then there are a couple of knock-about corn 3' dians and a troupe of accomplished girl dancers. The pantomime is gorgeously staged and runs most merrily. We predict for it a long stay at the Royal Court yet, but visitors cannot go too soon. They may want to go again. There are nightly performances at 7.30, and matinees at 2 o'clock on Mondays, Thurs- days and Saturdays.</p>
        <p>WEDDING PRESENTS.—Large selection of Useful and Ornamental Articles.—J. S. GIRLAND, 51 to 55, Bridge it., Warrington.—Advt. Work of the Warrington Fire Brigade. Superintendent Turner of the Warrington Fire Brigade has prepared his animal report in which Ile says, the total number of " Calls ".during the year was 27, including three " Test alarms " given by members of the committee. There were only two fires of a serious nature and these occurred beyond the Borough boundary. The attendance at each alarm averaged 19 men, and the minimum attendance was 14. The full complement of firemen turned out on eight occasions, and the attendanOe at the tests and practices was also very good. The total damage done by fires is estimated at ..£17,453, showing an increase of £9,480 as compared with the previous year. The damage in the Borough amounts to a sum which is the least since 1888, viz.: .£B7B, showing a decrease of £430-1; and beyond the boundary is the largest amount. for some dozen years, being £10,580, an increase of £13,850 I have made a quarterly inspection of the appliances at the various public institutions within the borough, and I found them to be in good working condition. The strength of the brigade is 21 officers and men. The appliances consist of :—Two steam fire engines, one manual engine, two fire escapes, ladders, two hose carts, two bandpump' s ten standpipes, keys, and 1,500 yards of canvas hose. The engine has been very, wen horsed by Mr.E. Connor. The " quick-hitching ' harness is working well and consequently a dietinct gain has been obtained in arriving at the neighbonr- I''mod of fires. In conclusion I again be to thank the Chief Constable and the members of the Borough Police Force for the valuable services rendered by them.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>ASK for CUNN=GRA.M'S HIGH-CLASb WIINBRAL WATERS.—Advt.</head>
        <p>ASK for CUNN=GRA.M'S HIGH-CLASb WIINBRAL WATERS.—Advt.</p>
        <p>" A Dream of Christmas."</p>
        <p>The members of the St. Clement's Band of Hope who took pirt in this production are to be congratula- ted on the success of their efforts. Owing to unfore- seen circumstances, the performance had to be held a week later than was originally announced, but unlike the majority of postponed events, nothing was loft to be greatly criticised. The drama itself was supposed to be, in the first scene, a dream, Where Miss Yuletide had a fleeting vision of three shepherds talking about the Saviour's birth, and angels singing " The first Noel.' The second scene illustrated the Christmas party of Miss Yuletide, who, after gracefully receiving her guests, entertained them with a short selection of songs, Scc. The festivities were interrupted by the appearance of Father Christmas. This . kind old gentleman distributed gifts to the guests, but found he had none for the hostess. Miss Yuletide therefore requested him to give her a vision of the coming. year, and he promised to do so. This was fulfilled in the third scene, when twelve girls dressed- as the various months, recited a short description of their characters. The part of Miss Yuletide was well sustained by Gertie Leigh, and that of Father Christmas by Dolly Jones. The shepherds were :—Harry Broadhurst, . George Bancroft, and Toni Molineaux while the following represented tho months: Nancy Cross, Ada Cowap, F.mma Saunders. Alice Bancroft, Cissio Parker, May Cross, Edith Woods, Bella Gill, Annie Guest, Lily Caldwell, Sophia Ward, and Esther Robinson. In addition to the above, the programme was taken part in by Misses Annie Bancroft, Gertie Jones,Jano Cross, and Polly Fairbrotber. Without disparaging the efforts of any of the other, it may be said that the coon songs by Blanche Millington were the " hit " of the evening, and she had to appear no fewer than six times before the audience were sttisfied. The accom- panists were ilfisses L. Molineanx and Nellie Mather. Tim programme concluded with a dialogue entitled " The Fairy's Visit,", by Misses Gertie Leigh, May Cross. Nancy Cross, Emma Saunders, and Master George Bancroft. Great credit is due to Mr.' C. J. Sheath, Misses Bancroft and Molineaux who were mainly responsible for the training of the children.</p>
        <p>CRAWFORD'S 'MAYFAIR" BISCUITS.</p>
        <p>A NEW BISCUIT FOR</p>
        <p>AFTERNOON TEA.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>ALDERMANIC VACANCY.</head>
        <p>THE ALDERMANIC VACANCY.</p>
        <p>Possible Developments.</p>
        <p>The aldermanic vacancy created by the death of Alderman Hutchinson will be filled on Tuesday at the monthly Council meeting. If seniority is observed, Councillor Ellison will succeed to the honour, but there are rumours of a division. Councillor John Roberts is talked of as being a likely Conservative candidate, and the names of Councillors Burton and Duncan. are also mentioned in connection with the vacancy. The Non-Trading members are reported to be opposed to the election of Councillor Ellison.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>PEN KETH.</head>
        <p>PEN KETH.</p>
        <p>Tut Poor t Cfm,PßF,s's CAMP.—On Friday evening last a most successful concert was held in the Wesleyan School, in aid pt the Warrington Poor Children's Summer Camp Fund. There-was a crowded audience, and Mr. James Dodds filled the chair with his custom- ary good humour. An excellent programme titad been arranged and the various items were well received, 1 Messrs. Wallington, Whittle. and Hannanall obtaining encores. The selections submitted were :—Pianoforte solo, Mr. George Weaver ; song, "Because," Miss E. Taylor •, old English song, ' The Leather Bottel," Mr. W. H. Wallington ; violin solo, " Fantasii Pnstorale," Mr. 'Louis Wood; song," Down the Vale," Mrs. J. E. Hannan ;. humorous selections, Mr. NV. W. Whittle ; song, "Songs of Araby," Mr. J. E. Hannan ; 11 humorous selections, Mr. W. W. Whittle ; song, "Ii Bacio," Miss E. Taylor ; violin solo, " Pirates of Penzance," Mr. Louis Wood ; song, " Good company," Mr. 3. E. Hannan ; song, "The jovial Monk," Mr. W. H. Wallington ; duct, " Life's dream is o'er." Mr. and Mrs. Hannan; humorous selections, Mr.W. IV. Whittle. During an interval in the programme brief accounts of the work done by the Summer Camp and the need for greater support were given by Mr. A. H. Lee and Mr. W. H. .1. Jenkins, the organising officials of the scheme. Altogether £5 ss. was realised by the concert and Mr. Dodds promised £lO. A word of praise is due to Miss Alice Aspey for so successfully carrying out all the necessary arrangements.</p>
        <p>LECTURE.—On Wednesday evening there was a good attendance at the meeting of the Mutual Improvement Society to hear a lecture by the Rev. W. Mellor entitled "An hour in Deamland." Mr. James Dodds presided. The lecture proved very interesting and was followed by a disenssion in which several members related dream experiences of their own.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>" A PRETTY KETTLE OF FISH."</head>
        <p>" A PRETTY KETTLE OF FISH."</p>
        <p>Latest Phases of the Struggle for the Tory Leadership. ,</p>
        <p>" This is a pretty kettle of fish," says a Unionist journal,discussing the turmoil among the Opposition, and adds sarcastically that only one statesman of the first ranks believes in Protection, and therefore, of course, the party must have him for leader. Indeed, matters do not appear to be shaping for the better in Conservative circles. _</p>
        <p>The" Birmiagham Post," starting with the usual dis- claimer of any desire on Mr. Chamberlain's part to oust the ex-Premier from the leadership, conveys in plain language the opinion that it is the only hope of the party. It points out that the rank and file, who apparently are so much averse to Mr. Balfour's leader- ship, are not Liberal Unionists, but Conservatives members of Mr. Balfour's own household, as it were. Why this is so (it goes on to say) is not difficult to understand. These members are what might be called the Tory Democracy. They are practical. hard-headed men, with no sophistry in their mental equipment, and strona, healthy, fighting instincts. Such men naturally prefer Mr. Chamberlain's direct and definite handling of Fiscal Reform. Tariff Reform is the only policy before the Empire. The timeslthat have gone had much to recommend . them. But other times demand other men, as well as other manners. Logically, no doubt Mr. Chamberit.in should take up the responsibility of leadership, bxt it is only just and proper that Mr. Balfour should first have an opportunity of stating his views. ALONE TO BLAME FOR THE TORY ROUT.</p>
        <p>There are other views among a section of the Con- servative parties who have lonz memories, and thee are strongly put by Ir. Wilson Noble in the 'Times.' He writes : Surely Mr. Chamberlain has done enough mischief for one election. By capturing the organisations he has made the Unionist party adopt a policy which " stinks in the nostrils of the people." It is he, and he alone, who is responsible for inflicting on the party the greatest defeat it has ever received since the Reform Rill. Mr. Chamberlain is not a Conservative, knows</p>
        <p>nothing ' of Conservative principles, and is not in sympathy with Conservative traditions. What right is there to suppose that Mr. Chamberlain will be to-morrow what he is to-day? This variegated politician has been everything in turn, and nothing long. Should the great Conservative party so far forget its glorious past as to choose such a man as leader, it must, in so doing, inevitably sign its own death warrant. _ _ _</p>
        <p>"A Conservative of Conservatives "points out that If the claims of Chamberlainism are persisted in they must inevitably end in the disruption of the Unionist party. Meanwhile the" Standard "and the" Morning Post" continue to publish columns of letters attacking Mr. Balfour, and -demanding that the member for West Birmingham be made the leader. Mr. Welsford, the defeated Unionist candidate for the Crewe Division, remarks that the ex-Premier has lived in a political balloon, and his personal attraction has been so great that he has insulated many M.P.'s from popular opinion in their own constituencies. "Can l‘fr Balfour lead on the advanced party with the conviction necessary for success, so as to unite it ?" His answer is" No," and he favours Mr. Chamberlain on a joint manifesto signed by the two rivals.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>ANOTHER KITCEIENER WANTED.</head>
        <p>ANOTHER KITCEIENER WANTED.</p>
        <p>"I have all ,ny life been a Conservative," wrote "Farmer John " "and still am and voted as such ; also a great admirer of Mr. Balfour, but it is absolutely certain that unless our party adopts a straight line and sticks to it as a whole we will be many a long day in Opposition." "The Right Bon. Joseph Chamberlain must be made leader of the Opposition, for the same reasons, neither more or less, that Lord Roberts and Kitchener were sent to South Africa." So wrote "Lombard-street." . -</p>
        <p>, " Semper " is also nmong the unfaithful; "I have been a Balfourite ever since he became our recog- nized leader, and a Tory all my life, nigh three-score years, but we want a man of firmness and action now." " A City Unionist " is very outspoken. He says We have got to, break with the caste that has hitherto ruled the party, and choose for our leaders only men who are in touch with the democracy. The Cecils, Brodricks, and Goschens have ruined our party for the next three years. I do hope the Tariff Reformers will stand no nonsense, but will insist on having Mr. Chamberlain as their leader. He ought to be the leader in name as well as in fact.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>LIBERAL CLUB.</head>
        <p>THE LIBERAL CLUB.</p>
        <p>SPLENDID GIFT BY MR. F. A. ROBERTS, LP.</p>
        <p>We have much pleasure in announcing that by a deed of gift Mr. Frank A. Roberts, LP., of Annesdale, Windermere, has handed over the Liberal Club premises, in Bewsey-street, to the members. Last week we announced that Mr. Roberts had paid a sur- prise visit to the Club. He was evidently so pleased at the improvements which have been made -during the last fifteen years, and at the great victory gained by the party at the recent election, that he decided to relieve the Club of embarrassment so far as was possi- ble to him. He has therefore made a gift of the club premises and fixtures to the members, who needless to say, cordially appreciato his generosity. It will now be possible for them to enter upon a're-construction scheme and make the building in all respects the worthy home of Warrington. Liberalism. For years the uremises and many of the fixtures have belonged to Mr. Roberts, and a rent has base paid. It estimated that-his gift is equal to one •of £2,000, and easily transcmds anything in the way of donations to the Liberal cause in Warrington. Prior to becoming the Liberal Club,the building was the home of the late Mr. Peter Rylands, and was, we believe, originally built for Mr. William Beamont, the first Mayor of Warring- ton.</p>
        <p>For the benefitcof those of our readers whose memories do not go very hr backove may mention that Mr. Frank Roberts was formerly the head of the chemical com- pany of Roberts, Dale and Co., which had works in Mersey-street, and St. James'-street. He was also, prior to his removal to Windermere some 15 years ago the chairman of the executive of the Warrington Liberal Association.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>ALLEGED FRAUDULENT BANKRUPT. ---- Former Warrington Tradesman Corn-</head>
        <p>ALLEGED FRAUDULENT BANKRUPT. ---- Former Warrington Tradesman Corn-</p>
        <p>mitta for Trial.</p>
        <p>Edmund Green Barratt, formerly of Warrington, and last from Blackpool was charged oc remand at the War- rington Borough Court on Thursday with having been adjudged a bankrupt and not disclosing certain assets; not delivering up certain property ; concealing certain items ; and making a false statement in his "state- ment of affairs "—all with intent to defraud. Mr. Ry lands (Messrs. Boote and Edgar, Manchester) appeared for the Director of Public Prosecutions, and Mr. T. S. Steel for the prisoner. At the previous hearing of the case Mr. Rylands stated that the prisoner was charged with :—Not dis- closing to the Oaicial Receiver book and other debts due to him amounting to £49 17s. 5d., a piano value £29, a rolled gold watch value £2 10s., a proportion of a residuary share of his grandfather's estate of the value of £l6 3s. 7d. ; and further with not disclosing posses- sion of a seoretaire, hencote and his share in a coal business. The total amount of the prisoner's liabilities only reached £125, and if the properties which he had mentioned had been pnt in there would have been 20s. in the £ for the creditors. The estate, as disclosed by the bankrupt, only amounted to £32, or about 4s. in the, R.</p>
        <p>On Thursday, Mr. Ellis Johnson, deputy official receiver, stated that the book debts not disclosed by the prisoner had up to the present realized £l5. The secretaire. &amp;c., had been sold for £1 55., the residuary share of the grandfather's estate realized £l6 3s. 7d., and the piano had been sold for £lB, though the last- named sum had not yet been realized by the Official Receiver.</p>
        <p>William Barratt, Hindlev, brother of the prisoner, stated that at the time of the bankruptcy his brother had a certain interest in his grandfather's estate over and above mortgage on the estate. Prisoner had also a share of £3O in a business at Hindley. Detective Meacock cave evidence of arrest. When charged prisoner said " Ido not intend to defraud any- one. I mean to pay 20s. in the 2. This is my brother's work." Prisoner, who reserved his defence, was committed for trial to the Liverpool Quarter Sessions, bail being allowed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>WARRINGTON CHILD'S DEATH.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON CHILD'S DEATH.</p>
        <p>Coroner and Disused Clay Pits. MANUFACTURER'S OBLIGATIONS.</p>
        <p>Mr. S. Brighouse, county coroner, held an inquest at Warrington on Thursday evening on the body of Elsie Howard, the four-year-old daughter of George Howard, 246, Knutsford-roall, which was recovered from a dis- used clay pit off Knutsford-road. George Howard stated that his daughter went out on Monday night ostensibly to go to her aunt's. She did not turn up and witness and others instituted a search for her. Nothing was seen of her until about eight o'clock next morning when her body was found Ifloating in some water in a disused clay pit about four hundred yards from his house. The clay-pit was not fenced off and was only about 33 yards away from Ithe end of Oldham-street.</p>
        <p>Mr. Brighouse said that as certain rumours had been freely circulated about the death, of the child he had causjd a post mortem examination of the body to be made.</p>
        <p>Dr. Smith reported upon the result of the post- mortem. The child's body bore no marks of violence and there were no signs of the child having been in any way illused. Death was due to drowning. Detective Barlow stated that the pit in which the girl was drowned belonged to Messrs. Mainwaring and Pemberton, brick-makers and was not fenced off at all. In answer to the Coroner Detective Barlow said that anyone walking past the end of Oldham-street could walk into the pit. Mr. Rogers (foreman of the jury) : The pit is being filled up is it not ? Detective Barlow: I cannot say. The Coroner commented upon the absence of a fence round the pit. The Quarry Act clearly stated that any pit within 50 yards of the highway must be properly and securely fenced. In this case the pit was within 59 yards of the roadway. Mr. Mainwaring stated that an excellent fence had been placed round the field, but it had been completely pulled down by lads whose ages ranged between 14 and 16 years. The land was private and people had no right upon it, but when a man trespassed on the land they could not be expected to keep someone to carry him off. The people bad been ordered off the field time after time, but they would not go, and he received nothing hut insults in trying to keep them off. Mr. Brighouse : Why not put a fence round? Mr. Mainwaring : We have put a fence round the field.</p>
        <p>Mr. Brighouse: The Act says the opening in thelanl must be fenced. Mr. Niaiuwaring : But we fenced the field. Mr. Brighouse : The law says that the opening must be fenced.</p>
        <p>Mr. Mainwarmg : Quite so, but you" would require a fortune to keep putting fences up, as fast as they are pulled down. These people do not bother to keep their children away. Mr. Brighouse : The Act assumes that people will trespass and therefore says you must fence the opening. If there was a proper and secure fence such as the law required this child could not have got into the pit. Mr. Mainwarine : A fence is put up and the child's brother. comes and pulls it down. Mr. Brighouse : You must keep your eves open and prevent him. If I lost a child of mine in this way I would be very upset. Mr. Maiuwaring : I wish all parents thought as much of their children. If they did they would go to the trouble of keeping then' in the house. Mr. Brighouse : That is all very well but children will be children and will run about.</p>
        <p>Addressing Detective Barlow Mr. Brighonse said : Let the Town Clerk take out a summons against these gentlemen under the Quarry Act and see what the magistrates have to say. We cannot have children four years of ago lost for the sake of a manufacturer of bricks.</p>
        <p>The jury returned a verdict of "Found drowned '1 and added that the owners of the pit should either fill up the pit or securely fence it. Mr. Brighouse: Mr. Mainwaring's argument is quite specious, but suppose the law said I should have a gate to my back yard, and I put one up. Then snp- pose someone pulled it down. I should have to put another up, and would have to do so as often as it was pulled down. The fact of someone pulling the gate down would not relieve me of my obligation. There would be an end of all things if Mr. Mainwaring's argu- ment prevailed. Young children like these must be protected against themselves. I don't like the way in which these people have taken the death of this child. If I had been them I would have said to the father "We are sorry indeed that you have lost your child. We sympathise with you and are sorry it has happened, and that the pit was not fenced. We will put a fence up, and see that it does not happen again." Perhaps you will let me tell you, Mr. Howard, that lam sorry you have() st your child.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>COUGHS AND BRONCHITIS. MINISTERS OF THE GOSPEL ADVISE VENO'S LIGHTNING COUGH CURE.</head>
        <p>COUGHS AND BRONCHITIS. MINISTERS OF THE GOSPEL ADVISE VENO'S LIGHTNING COUGH CURE.</p>
        <p>Rev. W. W. TULLOCH, D.D., Bon-ar Bridge, N. 13. writes :—" I have been a martyr to Asthma all my life and lately to chronic winter bronchitis. I have found VENO'S LIGHTNING COUGH CURE a valuable medicine." Rev. W. DACRE, 5, College Villas Road, South Hampstead, London, writes :—" I have a high opinion of VENO'S LIGHTNING COUGII CURE. I have used it with much benefit to myself and have recommended it to others."</p>
        <p>VENO'S LIGHTNING COUGH CLIRE for purity, effective- ness, and all-round potency has not its equal anywhere for coughs, bronchitis, asthma, catarrh, and children's coughs. 91d., Is. Id,, and 2s. 9d., at Chemists every- where.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>TOWN COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>TOWN COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>Business of the Next Meeting.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the %Carrington Town Council will be held at the Town Hall on Tuesday evening at six o'clock.</p>
        <p>ELECTION OF ALDERMAN. I The first business will be the election of an Alderman to Blithe vacancy occasioned by the death of Mr. Alder- man Hutchinson ; the appointment of a representative on the Lone shire Inebriates Acts Board in the place of the late Mr. Alderman Hutchinson. WATCII COMMITTEE. I This committee has decided that the following tenders be accepted :—Messrs. James Smith &amp; Co., Ltd., of Derby, for the supply of Police Ciothing, Buttons, Brushes and sticks, at £179 10s. Messrs. Unsworth Bros. for the supply of Helmets, Caps, Gloves and Armlets, according to specification and schedule of prices. A letter was read from the Local Government Board sanctioning the borrowing of £3,735, being a proportion of the excess expenditure on the new Police Buildings. It was resolved that Mr. H. Haßard be appointed Clerk of Works for the completion of the new Police Premises at a salary of 10s. per week, not, to exceed in the , whole £2O.</p>
        <p>FINANCE AND ESTATE. At meeting of this committee the Borough Collector reported that the Assessment Committee had:passed a re- ... , . -ol</p>
        <p>solution which wouldk have the effect of very largely re- ducing the rateable value of the Borough and also of re- ducing the revenue of the Water Committee by the sum of £3OO. Resolved that the Town Clerk and Borough Treasurer be authorised to interview the Clerk to the Assessment Committee with a view to having the resolution rescinded. The following resolution from the Distress Com- mittee was read and agreed to :—"That the Finance and Estate Committee of the Town Council be requested to advance the moneys required by the Distress Committee until the end of the present financial year." The Borough Treasurer laid before the Committee a statement with reference to overdue accounts for street- making, and the Town Clerk was instructed to recover the money without further delay, and in cases where necessary to appoint a Receiver. LOCAL GOVERNMENT BOARD AND POLICE</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>BUILDINGS</head>
        <p>BUILDINGS</p>
        <p>EXPENDiTURE. At a meeting of the Finance Committee The Town Clerk submitted a letter from the Local Government Board, giving sanction to the borrowing of the sum of £3,735 on account of the excess expenditure on the new Police Buildings. The total excess expenditure was £4,867 13s. 9d., but the Board made the following de- duction :—Loss of credit on old materials .U 65 us. 'temporary housing, £l2l 18s. 2d. ; loss of profit on old materials £2OO ; jobbing to Technical School and temporary drains .£l5; temporary wastshouse, £l4;total £lOl9 9s. 7d, In respect of work superseded in carry- ing out alterations to the house for the Inspector £5O; on 12 chairs for the Borough Court £3l 16s, ; on one armchair for the Borough Court £3 10s. ; on one side. board for Magistrates' retiring room £10; on one , standing desk in Charge Office £18; total £ll3 Ga.; leaving £3,735 18s. 2d, As regards the items making lup the sum of £1,019 9s. 7d., it appears to the Board that this expenditure was due to the retention and use lof the old Armoury for Technical School purposes and they cannot therefore regard it as properly chargeable to the Police Buildings account. As regards the sums deducted in respect of furniture, the Board consider , the articles of furniture in question to have been unduly expensive for a Police Court. The Board further explain that in arriving at the period allowed for repayment of Incurred rt t wt that years etahr se tghreealtoearn;atrhtey h o• f tree had espeadrietgoarred was the 0 curf before the application for the Board's approval of the loan was submitted.and they have reduced by two years , the period which they would otherwise have allowed for the repayment of the loan.</p>
        <p>STREET IMPROVEMENT AND LIGHTING. This committee has resolved that the tender of Mr. Joseph S. Garland for 160 yards of wrought iron hurdles ' at Os. 9d. per yard be accepted ; that the three new I streets near the " Beamont Council School be named " Wilkinson " street, " Garner "Street, and " Duncan " street, respectively ; that in the naming of streets after I individuals such streets shall be selected in the locality with which the names are associated ; that the plans of a new street behind Orford Avenue submitted by Mr. Unsworth be approved, subject to the corner plot being given up to the public on condition that the Corpora- tion pave the same. LONGFORD IMPROVEMENTS. A LOCAL GOVERNMENT BOARD INTERVIEW. At a meeting of the Sanitary Works Committee the Town Clerk submitted a letter which he had received from Colonel Marsh, Chief Inspector for England of the Local Government Bo.rd, requesting that the engineer should interview him in London with rafereirce to the application now before the Board for the borrow- ing of the sum of £1,200 for the extension of the bacteria beds at Longford. It was resolved that the Chairman and Engineer interview Colonel Marsh at the offices of the Local Government Board, and that the Chairman be authorised to make such arrangements as he may think necessary. Ata subsequent meeting it was resolved that the Publics Analyst be requested to take samples monthly of the Sankey Brook water. The Chairman reported the result of the interview which he and the Engineer had with Colonel Marsh, an Inspector of the Local Govern- ment Board, which was considered satisfactory,—The Eogineer was instructed to carry out the recommends- tions of the Lo6al Government Bciard in reference to the vapours from Longford Depot —That the tender of Mr. Edward Isherwood, for carting at Bs. per day • and ltd, per ton, for the carting from tlie Central Depot, Howley, to Longford, was accepted. PAVING AND SEWERAGE COMMITTEE. The committee has resolved that Mr. Councillor Ashcroft be appointed a member in the place of the late Mr. Alderman Hutchinson ; that Mr. Councillor Evans be appointed a member of the Stores iSub-Committee in place of the late Mr. Alderman Hutchinson. The tender of Mr. J. Newton for carting for the Highway Department, and the tender of Messrs. John Buckley &amp; Co., Limited, for carting for the Scavenging Department, for 12 months each:at 7s. 6d. per day was accepted. This committee submits a list of streets to be paved and recommends the acceptance of tenders for various sections of the work from Messrs. Benny and Thompson Mr. J. Johnson, Mr. W. Owen, Mr. T. Stringer, Mr. Thomas Hewitt, and Mr. T. C. Starkey (of Hull). The Borough Surveyor presented a report on behalf of the sub-committee appointed to inspect the streets in the town and stated that there was a total length of unpaved streets of 27,180 feet and of unpaved passages of 49,690 feet. The cost of carrying out the work of paving all these streets and passages was estimated at £76,171 17s. Gd. THE PAINTING OF THE MARKETS. The Markets Committee has decided that Mr. F. Haslam be appointed Clerk of Works to superintend the painting of the markets and that he be paid Id. per Ihour more than the standard rate of wages for painters. That Mr. Councillor Evans has been appointed the deputy chairman and Mr. Councillor Benson a member of this committee. GAS COMMITTEE. At a meeting of the Gas Committee it was resolved that the Engineer be authorised to advertise fo4 tenders for the erection of Tar Extractor of 2,000,000- gallons per diem capacity. PROPOSED PURCHASE OF FIRE APPLIANCES. At a meeting of the Fire Engine Committee the Borough Surveyor reported on the proposed purchase of the combined fire extincteur and escape. It was resolved that the committee visit Liverpool and Waterloo to inspect the apparatus in use there. WATER COMMITTEE. This committee resolved that the Town Clerk com- municate with the Manchester Ship Canal Co. drawing their attention to the leakage of water into the culvert under the Canal at Stocaton Heath swing bridge. THE TRAMWAYS. At a meeting of the Electricity and Tramways Com- mittee an application from the Distress Committee for tramway tokens to be issued to their Inquiry Officer was read. It was resolved that the request be granted. THE PROPOSED NEW STREET. • COUNCILLOR LORD'S QUESTIONS AND MOTHIK. Councillor Lord has given notice to ask the Chairman of the General Purposes Committee I. By what authority was application made to the Local Government Board to acquire property belong- , ing to Messrs. Greenall and Co. and Mr. Griffiths on the north side of Rylands-street ? 2. If such authority was obtained, what was the date of same, and when was it confirmed by the Council ? 3. By what authority did the Town Clerk enter into an agreement with Messrs. It. Garnett and Sons to acquire the necessary property and land of Messrs. Griffiths, Greenall, and Hewitt, and to k take down the same, and within two years to make a street or feet wide over a part of the land so acquired? 4. Has any further agreement or agreements been entered into with any person or firms to open the Sankey-street end of the new street 10 feet in width? If so what are the terms of the agreements, and with whom are they made ? At a later stage of the meeting Councillor Lord will move, that the Town Clerk be instructed to withdraw the application now before the Local Government Board for a provisional order to acquire by arbitration the properties named in the said application belonging to Mr. Griffiths and Messrs. Greenall and Co. OTHER MOTIONS. The following notices of motion have been given By Mr. Councillor Stott: That the following motion shall become a Standing Order of the Council :—lf two members of the Council so request, the names of those voting for and against a resolutioa or amendment shall be recorded. By Mr. Councillor Pemberton : That a committee be appointed forthwith to revise the Standing Orders of the Council, and to recommend such alterations to ' this Council as they deem necessary.</p>
        <p>Nearly 1,000,000 persons are said to be on the verge of starvation in Japan.</p>
      </div>
