      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>NEW COLLIERIES AT ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>For about a year past, the Sutton Manor Colliery Company have been completing arrangements for the sinking of new collieries in the township of Bold, just outside the St. Helens borough boundary. The trial boreholes have enabled the company to decide on the most favourable places for the sinking of shafts, and it is now stated that the officials of the company are engaged pegging out the land for the shafts and the necessary engine-houses and other buildings to be started. Colonel R. Pilkington, the ex-M.P- for Newton Division, is the active head of the company, as well as of the firm of Messrs. IL Evans and Co., who work extensive collieries at Haydock and Slt Helens. The pit shafts will be sunk in the neighbourhood of Clock Face and Micklehead Green, and when successfully 11 accomplished the enterprise will lead to a great expan- I sion on the Bold side of the borough. The Sutton I Manor Company's estate is very extensive, and all the best seams of coal in the district are to be tapped. BRAVE WORK" IN A PARR PIT. _ _ -</p>
        <p>An inquest was held at St. Helens on Saturday on the body of Richard Moss, 46, a miner, who was buried by a fall of roof in the Parr Colliery, belonging to Messrs. R. Evans and Co. Moss had left his working- place with a box of coal, and while proceeding along the haulage road the roof fell in, completely burying him. Some men near heard the fall, and they summoned the officials, and all set to work to remove the debris. They could hear the poor fellow moaning, and at last they uncovered his face and gave him brandy and tea. They were about to continue their efforts to rescue him when another great fall took place, and Moss was buried again. Some of the helpers had to be dragged out of the debris. Two days elapsed before Moss's body was recovered. _ _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. Matthews, His Majesty's Inspector of Mines, said he would like to compliment the men who had tried to rescue this man, They worked very bravely in a very dangerous place. The jury returned a verdict of accidental death, and the Coroner, Mr. Brighouse, said he would like to associate himself with the inspector in what he had said about those who rendered such brave service in trying to rescue this man. He had had some experience of these cases now, and he could quite picture the scene. They were deserving of highest praise. The foreman of the jury said they were all agreed on that. Mr. Peace, who iepresented the Colliery Company, said the firm were proud of the men who had taken part in this work. Some of them were there for 27 hours, continually at work, and were actually themselves /ragged out of the fall. They could not help admiring the British pluck of the miners, who from day to day worked under such dangerous conditions and were alway prepared to sacrifice their lives on behalf of their fellow- workmen.</p>
        <p>What makes us poor? What 'keeps us poor? 'Waste largely waste. 'Waste of effort; Waste of energy, • Waste of labour, Waste of time, Waste of money. To fight this constant waste about the house there is not 9ne thing to compare with Fels-Naptha soap be- cause it saves just that which is so easily and so often wasted—effort, energy,</p>
        <p>work, time, money. With Fels-Naptha soap no hot water, no copper fire, no steaming suds smell through the house. All this and more or your money ,returned,</p>
        <p>Use Fels-Naptha as stated on wrapper. Fels-Naphta 39 Wilson street London EC</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>I RUGBY FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>I RUGBY FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>THE appearance of that unique little Diary and J- Companion "Issued by GEO. G. BUSSBY &amp; , for the Use of Cricketers (which may be obtained from Sports Dealers, Booksellers, or Railway Bookstalls for 6d9, reminds us of the coming season, in anticipation of which GEO. G. BUSSEY CO., have prepared a' TEO prd " stock of Bats. We are told that the Umber they hold for B.t making alone exceeds 1,000 tone. •• Glorious Cricket ,follows the British Flag everywhere."</p>
