      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>NORTHERN UNION LEAGUE.</head>
        <p>NORTHERN UNION LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>RESULTS OF SATURDAY'S MATCHES.</p>
        <p>G. T. P. Runcorn 0 2 6 *Normanton 0 1 3 Warrington *Brought'n R'gers 2 4 16 Hunslet *Keighley *Oldham *Leeds *Wakefield Trinity 1 1 5 Wigan *Halifax *Millom *Barrow *Pontefract *Widnes *Batley 3 3 15 Bramley *Hall 1 2 8 Dewsbury 0 0 0 Played on the ground of the club marked *.</p>
        <p>Warrington Win at Salford. The return match was played between Warrington and Salford at Weaste on Saturday, and resulted in a capital win for Warrington by 8 points to 6 points. They thus avenged the one-point defeat administered by Salford a month ago. There was one change from the selected Warrington team, Widdeson being obliged to stand down owing to injury. Brooks came in at centre, and Kenyon went on the wing, so that the team was not weakened in any way. Salford played their selected fifteen. Fine weather prevailed, but there was only a small crowd of about 3,000. Teams and officials: Warrington : Berry, full back ; Kenyon, Brooks, Taylor, and Harris, threequarters ; Hockenhall and Lloyd, backs • Preston. Boardman, Shugars,Thomas, Belton, Naylor, Heath and Harmer' forwards. ' Salford :—.J. Cochrane, full back ;W. M'Whirter, I. Lewis, J. Lomas (capt), and J. E. Davies threequarters, D. Preston and W. D.John half backs ; J. Rhapps, D. Rees, E. J. Thomas, A. Buckler, J. Spencer, S. Warwick W. Brown and S. Holbrook, fornards. The home team started operations in very dashing style, and a smart piece of combination between Preston John and Lomas nearly brought about Warrington's 'downfall in the first minute. The centre ran brilliantly land was going over the Warrington line when Taylor I got him by the feet and pulled him down a yard short. This narrow escape did Warrington little good, for within five minutes Lomas took a pass finely with one hand and dashing between Taylor and Harris he gave Berry no chance to prevent the try. The scorer failed at goal. Salford were now on good terms with them- selves and for a short time they looked like severely defeating the visitors, so smart were their methods. Warrington made a plucky rally, and Harris twice shak- ing Davies off kicked well into the home quarter. The second time Thomas secured the ball before Cock- rane and was only collared on the verge of the line. Some determined play by 'Warrington was now seen,and the reds were penned in their quarter. Lloyd secured from a scrum and gave Hockenhall a timely pass. The latter dodged the home halves immediately and then with a brilliant corkscrew run he went over the line with a try. Preston failed at goal, but there was a new interest in the game now that the teams were level. Warrington showed a bold form, and a fast run by Kenyon compelled Cockrane to kick out of play a yard from the line. Salford were dangerous with a combined effort among the forwards which Berry checked, and half time came with the score, 3 points each. On restarting Warrington resumed the aggessive. The forwards changed their tactics and some clever wheeling and rushing gave Cockrane some very anxious moments. After about ten minutes play, Heath got the ball at his toes and dribbling fully thirty yards he scored near the posts, Hockenhall adding a goal. This reverse roused the homesters and chiefly through Lomas they attacked. Warrington defended resolutely, but eventually Lomas received from Preston and rushed over at the corner. He himself took the goal kick but failed to equalise. From now onwares, Warrington had rather the best of matters and Kenyon nearly got in on the left, while Harris made a couple of good marks. Final • Warrington 1 goal 2 tries (8 points) Salford 2 tries (i; points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>Original Cocoa, and a Speciality. E p psgs</head>
        <p>The Original Cocoa, and a Speciality. E p psgs</p>
        <p>Distinguished from all others by its invigorating nutritious qualities and delicious flavour. It contains all the substance of the choicest Nibs, and main- tains its leading Position as _ _</p>
        <p>COCOA</p>
        <p>the best form of COCOS for every-day use.</p>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, February 3, 1906.</p>
        <p>plainly—a dark lace, distorted by murder, with black eyes glaring like the countless eyes around him. .. • He had evidently come prepared for his hellish crime, for as I looked he picked up a mattock and spade and began to dig a grave. He worked with feverish speed, unconscious of the watchful eyes, and after a time had the hole deep enough for his purpose. Chained with horror I looked on and saw him bury his victim, cover the spot carefully, and arrange the bushes so that the fresh marks cotfld not be seen. After this he went off carrying his implements, and as he went the demoniac eyes followed him and I was left alone. I suppose I must have turned in my sleep, for after this a blank came. Then I was once again in the wood, but this time there was a change in the scene. Several of the old trees had been cut down and the grounds looked more deserted. The vases and statues were broken, the summer-house almost a ruin, and the big house shut up. These traces of time shewed that many years had passed since I last looked on the picture.. • It was twilight this time, a summer's evening, and the wood was sombre with shadow. The pathways were almost obliterated with weeds and bramble. The touch of Neglect was upon all within view. Decay had commenced her fell work on the handicraft of man. Nature was asserting her savage and imperious rights. I felt that I had been brought to this scene of desolation and gloom for a purpose. This purpose was to see once again the murderer of that long-ago buried victim. f was right, for presently I saw him dragging his wearied limbs through the tangle.</p>
        <p>I knew him, altered as he was by years of debauchery, disease, and unconscious remorse. 7 knew him by his eyes, bloodshot and filmy. although they had become; by his face, blotched and bloated as it now was; by his convulsively- clenching hands, gout-enlarged at the joints as they were. But by more than these signs I recog- nised him, for there, trooping after and round him, like the attendants of a Nero, were those devilish companions, the half-developed watchers with the flaming eves. He was old. whites-haired. decrepit and tramp-like in the shabbiness or his attire. The wickedness which that midnight crime phased had now become the expression of the man. Passion, induk eace, cruelty and sensuality had obliterated the hurnan and left only the beast upon that evil countena ice. He was now an object to pity and avoid. But his spectre train had increased enormously since that night, and I could see their features more plainly, even although they were perpetually shifting and changing. ?There were tiger-like faces with yellow, flaming eye-balls, wolf-heads with hungry glares, pig-snouts with small, crafty orbs, and other unclean monstrosities that were all the more gruesome for a certain human likeness added to their beastliness. I coeld see them clustering round him like flies in Egypt about a piece of putrifying meat; filmy, blending and loathsome. lie had come to the scene of his first great crime with the burden of either crimes upon him, and the legions which those deeds attracted were with him. They could not leave him now. I saw him pause when he reached the ruined summer-house and look round him with hopeless despair. Then his gaze centred on the spot beneath where the bones of his victim lay and a shudder passed over him. With what :night have been a groan—could that weird silence have been broken he sat down on the edge of an overturned fragment of stone.</p>
        <p>There was a pause, during which he appeared to be fixing his mind upon some desperate project. As he sat thus, with the look of a maniac, the tiger- faces with the flaming eyes clustered nearest to him, while the other spectres slunk back. Round and above him the gloomy wood seemed flashing with yellow fireflies, while he stared madly on that hidden grave. All at once he drew from his ragged vest pocket a small bottle with a red label on it on which was printed "Poison!" With nervous haste he pulled out the glass stopper, placed the neck of the bottle to his blackened lips, and next instant was writhing on the ground in violent convulsions. This struggles were over in a few seconds, and what had been the wreck of a man lay passive; but the crowd of witnesses still remained. The flaming eyes whirled and danced over the supine corpse.</p>
        <p>I was still watching and the dayliggt still lingered, when from the body I saw something like what the man had been grow up filmy as the others were and hover there for a time.</p>
        <p>It seemed as if this new appearance was held by invisible cords to the body, and that it was making frantic efforts to be free. In this struggle it was assisted by those round it. At last the links broke, and even as I watched the foul crew drifted away, and again the wood became empty except for that dead suicide and myself. The atmosphere had been chill as a grave vault while. those presences were near me. Now, how- ever, that they were gone the summer warmth returned to the wood and the dry soil gave out the heat of the season. This shill was the same that X had found so depressing in the house always. A aildness which, like the breath of winter, absorbed the vitality from the sun rays and made them cold as death. I had observed this peculiarity before. Were those influences the cause?</p>
        <p>After this my dream changed rapidly. I seemed to be transported from place to place without volition or effort on my part. I passed through dens of infamy and crime, and into public-houses where men and women were drinking furiously. Some of these places were dimly lit with candles, some brilliantly illumined with daring gas. .But all were thronged with those watchful, gloating, demoniac eyes and unfinished developments of hideousness. They hovered over the human wrecks and vice-sodden, watching, for ever watching, their downward march to perdition. Not the least dreadful portion of my dream was the unbroken silence that reigned while the action was progressing. It, was like a grim tragedy being performed in dumb show—the silence of chaos while the passions of hell raged noiselessly amid a frigid space. Presently I was drawn from the haunts of vice and drunkenness and brought back to the first scene. Again years had elapsed and the wood was in its present condition, while the house bad been built and occupied for some time. I stood in the room where I was at that samnent sleeping, and saw on a bed a man in thy, last stages of delirium tremens. As with the suicide those countless eyes were filling the space and waiting for him to make his exit and join their hopeless ranks. It was a blood-curdling spectacle, even although his shrieks and blasphemy were stilled by that frozen atmos- phere. The actions and agonised contortions of the haunted madman were all too expressive with- out sounds, those cruel, lambent eyes too horrible for endurance. With a moan of unutterable terror 1 broke the accursed spell and leaped from my bed, awake, bathed in icy perspiration and shuddering as if with ague. I glared about me wildly, a chilly breathing laved my body as if from a host of bloodless spirits. Round me on every side blazed lurid spots like the phosphorescent eyes of wild beasts. They were in pairs, set close together, yet they crossed and recrossed, advanced and retreated in a bewildering confusion. With a cry of affright I rushed towards the table where the matches were placed and struck a light. As I did so, the eyes were blanked out, while milky shadows, like smoke wreathes, drifted front the circle of light. There was no more sleep for me that night. Dressing myself I sat with the gas blazing until morning dawned, then I went out to seek for a more wholesome habitation.</p>
        <p>I have written this in my new abode, for that was my last night in this house of ghosts. Naturally I took some trouble to find out the history of that large deserted mansion, and at last I did so.</p>
        <p>_ It was told me that the second daughter of a former owner had disappeared and was never heard of afterwards. The legend was that she had stole from the house at night, leaving a note for her father behind her, and that her name was no longer mentioned. What her after, fate was no one inquired, nor was it ever known. Many years afterwards, the decomposed body of an old man, wretchedly dressed, was discovered in the wood, and beside him an empty bottle marked "Prussic Acid." The same informant told me that the first tenant of the house had been a dreadful drunkard, and died there of delirium tremens, fifteen months , after lie entered. . -</p>
        <p>I don't suppose my story has been very con- vincing, nor do 1 wish it to prejudice anyone, otherwise dealers in old furniture might suffer as well as owners of old houses. But I was satisfied with my experiences and much relieved when able lo leave the chill shadow of that house of dread. _</p>
        <p>{Tiu En.]</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>(Copyright.)</head>
        <p>(Copyright.)</p>
        <p>THE HOUSE OF DREAD.</p>
        <p>BY HUME NISBET,</p>
        <p>Author of "Bail Up," "The Bushranger's Sweet- heart," "The Revenge of Valerie," "A Yard of Ribbon," Ste.</p>
        <p>After living in a flat for two years I found the rooms unendurably crammed. It had been exceedingly cosy at the start, for I had purchased just sufficient to fit and make it comfortable. For a time, also, by putting up shelves here and there, I managed to stow things away, as one does on. board ship, so as to leave room to move about.</p>
        <p>But, alas! if it is, impossible for a stone to rest without gathering moss, so it is for a human being to pause long without accumulating. The hour arrived when my one longing was for more space. My property had ceased to be a pleasure and had grown to be a burden on my soul, as property generally becomes to its possessor.</p>
        <p>Enjoying - . i fiat-life as I dtd, t may seem strange that I did not rather sacrifice my superfluities and put some restraint upon my acquisitive inclinations than relinquish my comfortable quarters. It appears easy to have a periodic weeding out and clearing up, to those gifted with the organs of order and method. So it would have been with me had I been so gifted, but I am not. Now and again, when the litter became too oppressive, I made an effort to select and tidy up. It was hopeless; every article lying helter-skelter had been bought for a purpose, either for the immediate or the distant future, and I could not possibly dispense with any of them. There were notes, sketches, plots, books of reference, and articles which forced me to retain them; therefore it generally ended in my making up another brown- paper parcel to place amongst the other dust- covered packages. A fire only could have done the task effectively, leaving me like an uninsured merchant with no responsibilities and about as shipwrecked in hopes. My charwoman did her utmost to help me, honest woman that she was; but after her visitations, although I had more breathing room in my flat I was left in despair likewise, since she had swept" away all that I required most strictly. How blessed a man would be, if like Walt Whitman he could content himself with only the 'Bible and Shakspere. It is in the hoarding, as well as in the making of books, where most of our troubles come in.</p>
        <p>These regretful reflections I make in respect to what followed, when my insensate craving was satisfied. I quitted that happy flat and obtained the space I had longed for. Accursed space and fatal romantic madness which drew me into that awful experience. For weeks I went louse-hunting without success. Numerous suitable residences I found and nearly secured; that is, when I had considered the matter settled with the agent, it was discovered that some- one else had been before me with the landlord or vice-versii. These spirit-harassing experiences attend all house-hunters.</p>
        <p>At last, when almost desperate, I fell upon a residence that charmed my artistic eyes with its surroundings and suited in size. I£ was the only one left unlet in the district I had decided upon; therefor it was a case of Hobson's choice with me. Probably, however, I should have fixed upon it even if there had been a choice, for, as I have said, the outlook was delightful to a poet, a painter, or a novelist. To a level-headed paterfamilias, how- ever, the case might have been different. _</p>
        <p>There was a 'delicious and leaf-shaded wood behind with a wide-spreading park beyond, such as seldom can be found within walking distance of London tram-cars. Portions of a huge old- fashioned mansion could be seen through one partizg of the branches, while the garden fences were covered with a tangle of virginia creepers. As a picture, this rear-prospect from my balcony and windows required no composition. Somewhat sombre, as lofty and thickly-branched trees always are, there was yet a feeling of lavish and untrained nature about it that appealed strongly to me. Here surely I should be able to rest and thinlle without outside distraction. When these trees were clothed in their summer garb, how delightful to listen to the whispering of rustling leaves, the melodious piping of the birds. It was still early spring, so that the branches were bare, yet they lay behind each other like a tracery of close lacework. It was a fine picture of rugged trunks, shapely limbs, and delicate limo even under the bleak sky of March. A thrill of satisfaction passed over me. 1 noticed what appeared to be a winding stream glistening between ivy-covered banks. This added to the charms, yet as the water lay still and rather close to my back paling I inquired about it. The reply eased my anxious mind. There had been a large pond in the park which the owner was draining away. In the summer it would be perfectly dry. Were the drains all right I' Yes. The present tenant had lived in the house for ' seven years and had nothing to complain on that j score.</p>
        <p>Did the chimneys go all right? "Yes," was the reply of the seven years' occupant. "Was it a dry house ? "</p>
        <p>For reply I Was shewn the cellar which was free from any damp, and stretched under the house. It was a splendid cellar—roomy, strongly built, and with nothing to find fault about. I could not think of any other point to raise, therefore I concluded the bargain and prepared to move in. _</p>
        <p>For the next fortnight we were all busy arranging the furniture, putting up shelves and placing the books in order, and during this fortnight, having plenty to do, I was fairly content. I say, fairly content, for although I slept badly during this time—suffered from wretched dreams when I did sleep, and found a strange restlessness in me when I was awake—still, I put these unusual sensations down to the change. I had become, to some extent, a creature of habit, it would therefore take some time to get used to my new surroundings. At length my house was in order and I could begin to enjoy it and work. My writing-table was placed where I could see best. The gasfitter and painters had quitted the premises. There was nothing to disturb me except the unceasing shower of printed bills which poured through the letter-box, and which no mortal can avoid let him be ever so virtuous. I ought to have settled into work, for I had taken a long rest; like- wise, I had a subject all planned out. But work I could not, although I stro4 franti- cally to do so. The strange unrest increased hour by hour, intensified by a brooding feeling of hope- lessness that pressed upon my heart like a night- mare.</p>
        <p>I could neither read nor write. I prowled over the house like a caged animal, and with what I suppose must be the feelings of a trapped beast. Heavy sighs and groans broke from my pent breast without giving me any relief. I had never in my life suffered such despondency and desolation—such appalling loneliness and absolute hunger for the society of my kind. The desire to howl in my despair was on me and increasing every hour. Now this was the more singular, because all my former life I had been a lover of solitude, and cared little for company. Indeed, my few friends regarded me as a confirmed recluse, and for months at a stretch I had never crossed my own front door. To see a friend for half-an-hour at rare intervals had satisfied me up to this time,Thut now all seemed changed. This house weighed upon me like a burden of dread without giving me the satisfaction of a reason..</p>
        <p>_ It was lightsorne, airy and cool; the ceilings were lofty and the street highly respectable. Could it be this air of respectability that chilled my Bohemian spirits ? I tried firmness and reason, also perseverance, but all failed; the depressing influence was too powerful for tuy philosophy. The only surcease I had was when I rushed out- side the iron gate and left the street behind me. Then I found peace and grew more hopeful and confident, as a coward does, who has been afraid of the night, when daylight comes. Away from this house I argued with myself about my insensate folly, cursing my weakness and sloth in wasting so much valuable stime, and vowing that I would return and conquer the idle feeling. Then I would hasten back and enter bravely enough, to be seized upon once again by the wretched influence which rendered me as motiveless and inert as an attack of malaria might d 9. • Three months passed in this hopeless struggle, and during that time I had done absolutely nothing, except peregrinating the streets, moving unhappily through the rooms, or lying limply in my chair, filled with a dread, all the more horrible because it was so vague. I had watched, with hopeless eyes, the trees putting forth their buds, the buds spring into tender green spots, and finally expand to broad green leaves. These were now rustling and whisnerine all day lone and sounding like rain-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>ro not give rise to Acidity, – INDIGESTION, &amp;C. 110 it OATS. APalatable, Nutritious and Economical Dish.-Lancet.</head>
        <p>ro not give rise to Acidity, - INDIGESTION, &amp;C. 110 it OATS. APalatable, Nutritious and Economical Dish.-Lancet.</p>
        <p>drops in the dark. When ihe moon rose it shone between the ebony wood, weird Ind sombrely beautiful. That wood repulsed me curiously, as a graveyard , ' might have done. I dared not look upon it after nightfall, although why, I could not define. Once or twice as T gazed into it from my back '• garden by daylight, and when the sun gilded it I admired it for its lavish and untrained condition. It had been long neglected, for the big house had • ; been shut up for years. Yet it bore the traces f ' former wealth and splendour, for among the dense undergrowth and tangled weeds lay broken pilasters, vases, and statues of stone and marble. At one part I saw the remnants of a Corinthian summer- house, with fluted pillars, flowered capitals, act. carved carved freizes. These ornamental ruins were nearly , . smothered in vegetation, yet when the sun pens- I trated to the shadows I could note sufficient to tell me what they had been once upon a time. There was a glass folding door which led from ray sitting-room into the gardens and shewed me the wood behind. This prospect by day was pleasant enough, but when darkness c,aine and the room was lighted that glass door haunted me with a morbid fear that I could not overcome. ! It attracted my eyes from my book, in spite of all my efforts. Every minute I looked towards it expectantly, for what I could not have said. It felt as if someone was matching me outside, and that I might at any moment see something horsible. That door got on my nerves so dreadfully that at last I dare not sit in the room alone; even to shut off the gas and rush upstairs to my bedroonx we an experience which made me shudder and bathe... me with a cold sweat. * Only three visitors had called upon Inc during this interval. When It asked them how they liked my , house, they replied almost in the same words: I"I don't think I like it. It seems very dreary and depressing. There is a kind of moated grange feeling about it that I cannot account for. It gives me the hump." My own sensations exactly, as I had felt after the first hour, but this had become intensified to something much worse than mere dreariness or depression. I By this time I had grown almost desperate. My obligations rendered it strictly necessary that I should get to work, yet I was as incapable as ever to do so. I resolved, therefore, to go on a fortnight's tour. I also made up my I mind that if I came back no better I would give I op the house without further efforts to settle dos._ This would be excessively foolish and expensive, I knew, but I was trusting to the change of scene ' curing me of my morbid fancies. I travelled to Scotland, and after the first day was convinced that the fault must have been mine. My faculties cleared, and the depression fell from tme like an unhooked cloak. Then after enjoying I my holiday I returned once again, active and fit foe ; duty. Alas! the moment I t entered m front door all my activity and joy departed, wl le the gloom settled upon me as densely as ever. "Enough,' I said. "This decides the matter. I shall give up this accursed house no matter what it costs me." However, I did not find the difficulties about getting rid of my bargain that I had anticipated. The landlord gave his consent readily enough to me sub-letting it when I informed him that it did not suit me, and two days after the board was up I found a tenant to take it off my hands. I had now only about two and a-half months to occupy it till the next quarter-day. It would take me this time to look out for another place and prepare for moving. I felt that I could face the gloom for that time. For the next few days I chanced to be occupied outside a good deal, and had even began to regret my haste in giving up a house that seemed so desirable, and which was so quickly snapped up. But the influences which were driving me out did not give me much time for regret. The very first day that I passed indoors the depression became so heavy that I could hardly endure it. I went to bed early, lay tossing for hours, and at last fell asleep to suffer from the usual nightmare. During those past three months one aeries of dreams had haunted me. But although terrifying enough at the time lof their enacting they had always been formless to my waking senses. , This night, however, the dreams were vivid enough to impress Me with a keen recollection when I woke. Those dreams remain with me yet , and will as long as I live. I The scene was the wood, not as it now was, but as it must have appeared in the distant past. In , some parts it was denser ; that is, older and larger :trees grew where now was clear space, yet I knew it was the same wood and the same house which now stood deserted.. Well-cared-for walks intersected the grounds. The vases and statues were in their places, while the Corinthian summer-house gleamed whitely amid the leafage. I It seemed to be late, for the moon was high in the heavens, while from the house, portions of which I could see, there were no lights. IBefore me stood the figure of a young man dressed in eighteenth century costume. He stood lin front of the summer-house, and although a shaft 'of moonlight touched his head and shoulders I could not see his face; as the hat shaded it. I He was waiting for someone, and appeared to be limpatient or angry, for although he did not move from his position yet lie clenched and unclenched his hands in an ugly fashion. II had an instinctive repulsion as I looked at this . waiting figure, but what terrified me more was that the space round him seemed filled with watchers. I saw glaring eyes on every side of him ; hideous, half-formed faces and indefinite figures pressing round .hiin and watching him with lambent, baleful 'eyes that flickered and shifted incessantly. It was a wood crammed with malignant demons. I was horror.h eho sawa female form, with a large t s e cloak covering her, glide along the walk from the direction' and join the man. As she approached she flung back her hood. Then the moon-gleam sheared up a face, exquisite in its pale and youthful loveliness. Instinctively I chained w Presently of ,tf ht I guessed why she had met him at this last hour. It was a planned elopement about to be consum- mated. I saw them embrace for a moment, while the crowd of formless watchers drew closer. If the the silence was awful: not even a leaf appeared to couple said anything, I could not hear. Indeed, rustle. Then I saw a change in the position of the couple. ;The was struggling in his arms while he clutched her throat with both hands. He was strangling her while those fearsome, glaring eyes kept switch with unholy glee. In a little while her struggles ceased and she lay at his feet a limp, dead mass. In the struggle his hat had fallen off, so that. now I could observe him</p>
        <p>pare 'P. adbury for economy in use, for price, mid for quality. Cadbury's is better value, , and lit's the nicest cocoa also.</p>
