        <head>WIT AND HUMOUR</head>
        <p>WIT AND HUMOUR</p>
        <p>THINGS really most necessary to mend—Evtt tvays. THE man who is scared at ghosts is afraid of nothing. Ir is hard to please the man who never knows what he wants. _</p>
        <p>WHEN does a ship share the same fate as coal? When she is scuttled.</p>
        <p>NATATORY.—" Can you swim ? " " Where did you learn ? " "In the water." " Youß neighbour appears to have failed a good many times? " "Just twenty-four times. The next one will be his silver bankruptcy." ROWNE DE BOUTE.—You wouldn't marry Miss Roxey for her money, would you, Upson? Upson ' Downes : How else can I get it? "I AM summoned to another climb," said the bell-boy as the indicator announced a call from the top floor. WHEN a washerman changes her place clf resi- dence, one may ask "where she hangs out now," without using slang. "Jacx, do you love your littie baby brother?" "What's the use? He wouldn't know it if I did."</p>
        <p>NED "I don't think it right that children should go to school for so little. Father ought to have to pay more than he does." Fred " Why." Ned: " 'Cos then he wouldn't be'abla to afford to send us."</p>
        <p>IVI.AmatA: "Ethel, what do you mean by shout- ing in that disgraceful fashion? See how quiet Willie is," Ethel : "Of course he's quiet ; that's our amo. He's papa coming home late, and I'm</p>
        <p>MAREE.—A lot of us girls have established a secret society, Jack. Jack: What are the objects of it? Mabel : Why, we meet together and tell secrets, of course. "Now, really, what was the most astonishing thing you saw in Paris, Mr„.. Spicer'!" asked Miss Gusher, and 'without a Matent's hesitation Seth answered, "M,,' hotel bill." EMPLOYER : How did you break that vase? Office Boy: I had it in my hand when I heard your bell ring and dropped it, because you told me yesterday to drop everything and answer your bell whenever you rang. "Wu, Tommy is arithmetic easy for you this year ? " Tommy: Yes'm." "Is it because you har a new teacher?" "No, ma'am; it's 'cause I ain't got no 'rithmetic."</p>
        <p>Mns.— Qtrwas : Your husband reminds me of somebody. Mrs. Neighbours: He reminds me of his mother every time the cooking doesn't suit him.</p>
        <p>GREENE (sadly): " That's. too bad. My wife has gone and put my handkerchief in the wash, and I am positive that I tied a knot in it td remind me of something." Flan telegraph messenger: "I don't like de- livering messages in there." Second boy: " Why " First boy: "All the doors are fixed so's yer can't slam 'ern." TEST OF GOODNESS.—You cannot really te'l whether a man is a conscientious Christian or not till you have been present when he puts the wrong end of his ]pen-holder in his mouth. Houss OWNER: "How (hoes your furnace work this weather ? " Tenant: "The exercise of raking it keeps me warm enough, but the other members of the family complain." DEPOSITOR: "Is. the cashier here?" Bank Manager: ".No, he's gone away." Depositor. Ah, gone for a rest, I presume?" Bank Manager sadly: N-o, to avoid arrest. "WHAT'S the matter? You look SO cross," asked a mother of her little son. "Well, mother, you told me always to count ten before I spoke when I was angry, and I've counted 150, and I'm just as angry as ever." "You had better not go boating with Ada," said Tommy to his sister's .fiunce." "Why not, Tommy?" "'Cause I heard her say she intended to throw you overboard sow." "JOHNNY," he whispered to her little brother, "did your sister get a note from me last night? It was written on pink paper." "I think she most have got it," said Johnny, "'cause when she came down to breakfast this morning her hair was done up in pink curl-papers." ROUNDABOUT ,HINT.—" I hear that you're going to move, neighbour?" "Stuff and nonsense. Nothing of the kind. Who told you so ? " "Your landlord."</p>
        <p>Too Mum HARMONY.—Wiggins: "I understand you've just been away on a three 'months' trip with Wigglestein. Did you enjoy your company?" Waggles: "Well, no, I can't really say I (lid." NViggins : "Why not?" NVaggles "Why, the fact is, he always agreed with everything I said." FOR PHILATELISTS.—Apropos of the Duke of Coburg as a philatelist, an amusing story is told of how, on the occasion of his showing his collection of stamps to an American lady, she exclaimed: "Why, this rushes creation. It's a regular stampede." TEACHER'S PROPOSAL: "You may tell us, Tommy, some of the ways in which the element of fire confers a benefit on the human race." Tommy (who knows something of his father's business methods) : "When the amount of the insurance exceeds the value of the stock on hand." _</p>
        <p>. - - "I BELIEVE we mothers cannot be too careful. Whenever there is any difficulty between my hus- band and me, I send the children outdoors imme- diately." "Very prudent; but, on the othar</p>
        <p>nana, is it gooa !or tne enuaren to be obliged to be on the streets from morning till night ? " HEAD of Household (year 1900): "Alfred, dear, your biscuits are very good this morning." Young Husband (colouring with ”pleasure) : "I am glad to hear you say so, love. Head of Household: "Still, they are not quite as good as papa used to make."</p>
        <p>HARRY DOIINTOWN to country sweetheart: "Miss Ifilkyweigh, do you play and sing When the Cows are in the Corn?" Miss Milkyweigh : 'Lord bless you, no. I got the dogs aul chase 'eta out.</p>
        <p>AN innocent misunderstanding oa sometimes very useful in helping over a hard place. said the teacher, "you may spell kitten.' " " K•double-i.t-e-n," said Mabel. ", Kitten' has two i's then, has it?" "Yes, ma'am, our kitten has."</p>
        <p>HIS HOM—The man vim° reads other people's literature and forgets to return it happened to find a friend busy with paste and scissors: "Ha, ha !" said the caller, cheerfully. "So you are going to keep a scrap-book, are you?" "Yes," was the unsmiling reply. "I think manage to keep it Nobody ever wants to borrow. scrap-books you know." TUE late Dr. Cook, of Haddington, while officiating as prison chaplain, on one occasion observed among the prisoners an old woman who had been a frequent inmate. "Here again, Mary the doctor said. To this Mary replied: "Awed. sir, whaur could I be better? I get my meat ad I get my claes, an' I bee the benefit o' your minis- tratione</p>
        <p>Two Iribhowl were discussing the comparative usefulness of the sun and moon. One of them swore that the sun gave the stronger light. Said the other, "But the moon is more sensible.' "How do yees make that out!" "Oh, it's aisy." "Let's hear yees prove it " Bedad, the moon shines in the night, when we mid° it, while the sun comes out in broad daylight, when even a on eyed man could see widout it." TEACHER (giving directions for standing) : "Stand with your heels together, toes turned out, making an angle of forty degroes." This was fol- lowed by a look of bewilderment on one boy's face. Teacher: "Well, Tom, do you know what I mean? Do you know what a degree is ? " Pupil: "Yes, sir." Teacher: "What is it?" Pupil: "Sixty- nine and one-fourth miles.</p>
        <p>THE FARMER: "Why don't you work ?" The Tramp: " D'yer t'nk everybody in the World is crazy? I never worked in my life, hut I don't owe anyone a penny, while I s'spose you've bin workin' since yer was able tor drive a cow, an' I'll bet there's a mor'gage on yer farm that keeps yer a slavin' ter pay the int'rest on!" , WHAT HE NEEDED.—The late Father Healy, of Bray, was once about to enter a carriage at Kil limy Station, when, observing the compartment was nearly filled by ministers, he hesitated. "Oh, come in, Father Healy," said Lord Plunkett, the Protestant Archbishop of Dublin; "come in want to ask you a question. A gentleman of your cloth has come over to us, and I want to know what we should give him." "I think," replied Father Healy quietly, "that I should give hina the pledge."</p>
