      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>HAVE YOU INDIGESTION.</head>
        <p>HAVE YOU INDIGESTION.</p>
        <p>ARE YOU WEAK AND THIN. SEAWEED IS YOUR SALVATION.</p>
        <p>Mr. HENRY DAVIES, 22, James Street, Wrexham, writes, Feb. 27th, 1904 :—"i beg to adds word of praise in favour of VENO'S SEAWEED TONIC as I have received the greatest of benefit. I was in a very weak state and suffered frightfully from indigestion, and also got very thin and weak, but thanks to your SEAM BED TONIC., I am quite well and strong again and able to work." VENO'S SEAWEED TONIC is guaranteed to permanently cure all ailments arising from a diseased condition of the stomach, liver, kidneys and blood. It strengthens the body and nerves, cures indigestion, wind, sluggish liver, weak and painful back, dizziness, headache,kidney troubles, drops, and female weakness, and is especially good for chronic constipation. Price Is. 13. d. and 2s. 9d. L at chemists dverywhere.</p>
        <p>My Dear Children,— ' hope you will all remember the new com- petition I announced last week for girls, and I hope that many of you will try and win one of the beautiful prizes that I have offered.</p>
        <p>Next week I shall announce the toys' com- petition, so you must all be on the look-out for it in next week's paper. Many letters, essays, 'and poems have reached me this week. I print a few below, and have pleasure in awarding a prize to Gladys M. Kendall for her contribution, which will be concluded next week. I have told you all that I will always award prizes if I receive good essays or pieces of poetry. I suppose by this time you .have all settled to your studies again. I am afraid you had no snowballing or skating, as we have had neither snow nor sharp frosts during the past holidays. I was particularly pleased to receive letters from two of my very early members—namely, Thomas Oram and G. B. Richmond. The news they gave me was very interesting, and I hope they will try for some of. my com- petitions. I am very pleased to welcome Ernest Harrold as a new member, but his essay arrived too late; so he must try again. I have sent off this week six of my post- card albums to members who have sent me in a list of twenty-four new names for our Children's Corner League. We now have 900 members; so I have hopes, that in a very short time we shall have 1,000. Many thanks to Hannah Martin for her letter, but there was no prize offered for the answer to puzzle _that she sent me. _</p>
        <p>With all good wishes from • UNCLE NED.</p>
        <p>Dear Uncle Ned,—l thought I should like to send you a short poem. The title is " Kittenbury Football Match."</p>
        <p>"It's dreadfully chilly," said Pussy the timid, To all her companions one day. " But that doesn't matter," They merrily shouted; " We're off to the meadows to play." And, my! how they gambolled! I don't think I ever have seen Such a gambol as that. There is plenty of noise With the girls and the boys, But they're silent compared with a cat. And, pussy the timid,— Where could he be hiding? Why, muffled in wrappers, alas! He sat on a seat in the dining-room window, And watched the great game through the glass. From your little niece, GERTRUDE HANOVER.</p>
        <p>. My Dear Uncle ed,-4 suppose you have been thinking that I had forgotten I was a member of your League, because I have not written to you for a long time • but it is not so. We have commenced school again this week, after a fortnight's holiday; and, as it is the election on Monday, we have had a holiday given to us. The puzzles YOU sent me to do came rather late, and 1 had no time to. solve them before the ap- pointed time. I and my two sisters gladly received the blotters which you sent for us, and have used them several times since we got them; and I myself thank you for the sample bottle of Mason's \Vine Essence, which I have not yet used.. I have spent a very joyful Christmas, and have attended several Christmas parties. Hoping you have had a jolly time.—From .your loving niece, MARY PLINSTON.</p>
        <p>A CONTRIBUTION FROM GLADYS M. KENDALL. GRANDMA'S STORY. , "A story, my dears? What children you are for stories, to be sure! Well, I don't think I have any left; but, if you will go _ -nrne ch,</p>
        <p>and get some chestnuts from cook, I will think of one, and then we will go and roast them before the fire in the hall." So said grandma, on New Year's Eve. On this day we were going to have a large family party, but we had received a telegram in the morning from our aunt, saying that all were laid up with colds, and could not possibly go out in such bad weather. So we made the best of this disappointment, and were going to have quiet games by ourselves. Yet again we were disappointed, for mother was ill with neuralgia, and father had gone to see an old friend, who was dying, eight miles away, and would not be back until next morning. These are the reasons we wanted a story to pass the time. a Well, dears, you have got a lot of chest- Ttuts, so let me begin,' said grandma.</p>
        <p>AN ADVENTURE WITH A MAN-EATER (Concluded.) •</p>
        <p>The " sowar " on watch had fallen asleep, and the tiger had pounced upon him and carried him off to the jungle. We fired, our rifles in the direction the brute. had taken, not with any hope of hitting, b,ut trusting that the sound, of firearms would make the beast drop his victim. We followed him a short distance, and then, seeing how useles.i it was to continue, in the darkness, we re- turned to camp. Early next. morning we found traces of the poor " 56war " .close to the c:imp, At one place we found his belt, and in another his turban. .We could not rind the body, and the tiger had evidently dragged it into the recesses of the jum..Tle. Soon after, some of the villagers arrived. bringing a white calf. for a bait. Guided by them, we made our way to a place 'about a mile away, close by 'a stream, where they said that they had seen the tiger's tracks, showing that he came there .to drink. He was not to be expected until. evening; so, after reconnoitering the ground, and select- ing in a suitable tree a place to build a " meechaum," we returned to the village. In the evening We returned to the stream, and the first thing we did was to -build the " meechaum " in the tree. We did not in- tend to shoot the tiger from the tree, but made it only as a place in which to pass the night, until we could " stalk " the tiger to the spot. • The pr white calf was brought up, and fastened to a tree a short distance from the water. We took up our position in the tree, and shortly after thidnight we heard, the calf moaning piteously, and we knew then that the man-eater was in the neighbourhood. We descended, and took up our rifles. After a few minutes we saw the tiger stealthily approaching, and, when he was near enough, we fired. The huge animal staggered and roared, but he was Mortally wounded, and, after a very few seconds, he fell a lifeless heap. Never before or since have I seen such a huge monster. We had his skin made. into a beautiful rug, which many have admired. The villagers,' needless to say, were over- joyed to see that their enemy was destroyed.</p>
