      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>MR. KING'S VICTORY.</head>
        <p>MR. KING'S VICTORY.</p>
        <p>REJOICINGS AT A STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>Torch-light Procession.</p>
        <p>In no district in the Knutsford Parliamentary Divison has the splendid victory achieved by Mr. A. J. King given greater satifaction than in Stockton Heath. This growing suburb has long been regarded as Tory to the backbone, and, from a Liberal point of view as hopeless, and the recent triumph of progressive principles was what many hoped to see but few dared to expect. For the first time in a hundred years the Liberals of the district have the satisfaction of being represented by a supporter of their programme. Such an achievement was not to be passed over lightly and Saturday evening was given over to 'general rejoicings. The proceedings opened with the entertainment of the workers lin the Providence schoolroom where a substantial tea awaited them. The subsequent celebrations were of a more public character, a torch-light procession being held. This was timed to leave Elleamere-road at seven o'clock and some considerable time -before that hour a large crowd assembled near the swing bridge. The Brick- street Mission band was the first to put in an appear- ance, the next arrivals bejng torch-bearers—young and enthusiastic Liberals whd cheered continuously. Mr. A. J. King was accompanied by Mrs. King and Mr. and Mrs. Thistlethwaite and drove to the scene in an open carriage. Immediately upon his arrival a rush was made for the vehicle and the horse was quickly unharnessed, the work of drawing the carriage being undertaken by between twenty and thirty young men. Everything in readiness,tbe procession moved off amidst hearty cheers to the tune of "See the conquering hero comes," I vigorously played by the band. The route to Victoria- square was thickly lined with spectators who accorded Mr. King a mixed reception cheering or hooting accord- ing to their political beliefs, though the cheers were the more heartily given and the cries of derision were largely drowned. Prior to Saturday one prominent Liberal worker bad received several anonymous and veiled threats of violence but these were generally dis- regarded though a strict watch was kept for any sign of active opposition. At Victoria Square an egg of com- parative antiquity was thrown by a youth but it passed harmlessly over the heads of the processionists. More would probably have followed but the thrower noticed a muscular Liberal looking in his direction and beat a hasty retreat. This was the only active display of party feeling the remainder of the opposition—though very little was shown—being confined to good humoured bantigh From Victoria Square the journey was continued along Walton-road to the Stag Inn and then along Ellesmere-road to Stafford-road where " Auld Lang Syne "was sung prior to Mr. King leaving for Manchester, the greatest enthusiasm being mani- fested.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>SPEECH-MAKING.</head>
        <p>THE SPEECH-MAKING.</p>
        <p>At the conclusion of the tea, there was a little speech-making of a congratulatory order. Mr. W. H. Sherburn, chairman of the boil Liberal Executive Committee, proposed a resolution congratu- lating Mr. King upon his victory. Their Tory friends were taking their defeat rather badly, and the late Memberfor the division in a message to the Press had attributed his defeat to some Ananias who had been wandering about the constituency. Taking into con- sideration certain leaflets which had been issued, in which Mr. Egerton stated that he was a Free-Trader and a free-fooder, in spite of the fact that he had some months previously stated that Mr. Balfour was too slow for him, and that he preferred to back Mr. Chamberlain as being the faster horse, they could easily see where the Ananias was. (Hear, hear, and laughter.) He heartily congratulated Mr. King upon his success, and hoped he would live long to represent the division (Cheers). Mr. C. Jackson *Ames seconded the resolution and in doing so paid a high tribute to the excellence of the Liberal organization in the division, and also praised the efforts of lady workers, which, he said, he believed had been no small factor in bringing about Mr. King's victory. ( Hear, hear. The Rev. H. Donnie° surported. If the election had proved one thing more than another, he said, it had had shown that Mr. Chamberlain's proposals were economically false, morally wrong and politically dis- astrous. (Cheers.) Whereas free trade' education and the licensing laws bad played an important part in the Liberal victories throughout the country he believed their successes were primarily due to the fact that people were taking a more intelligent interest in politics and were beginning to think for themselves. (Hear, hear,) He appealed to the Liberals of Stockton Heath to keep their organization in good working order so that at next election they would be again able to return Mr. Ring. (Cheers.) The resolution was enthusiastically carried. Mr. King briefly replied and expressed his great indebtedness to the workers who had supported him so nobly during the fight. When he last appeared in that room he dare hardly hope for victory and even that night he was a little incredulous that they had achieved in Knutsford a victory which was one of the greatest in the country. (Cheers.) He appealed to them ,to keep their organization in good order as in the future he would not have so many opportunities of visiting them as he had during the past year but be would endeavour to keep in touch with them and would,periodically give them a-report of the work in Parliment. He trusted that in the future they would never have reason to regret the great honour which they had conferred upon him. (Cheers.) Mr. Thisigethwaite, who acted as Mr. King's election agent, said be did not think any man had been returned to the House of Commons who was actuated by more sincere motives,or who held higher ideals than Mr. King. (Cheers.) His own part in the 41ection had been very small. They had heard of little differences and friction amongst their ranks but he had not seen anything of such things. The election had been a perfect revelation to him in regard to the large number of enthusiastic voluntary workers who had come forward in every dig- trict, and who had done their duty really nobly. It had been a surprise to him to find the number of workers who had entered into the fight with no thought of reward, but the reward which they bad reaped—that of victory—and nowhere did that apply more than in Stockton Heath. (Chew.). Mr. J. Longland said he would like to emphasise what had been said about Mr. King. (Hear, hear.) He had met people over and over again who had expressed their gratification at the admirable candidats they had got. (Cheers.) People had been impressed more and more by every speech which Mr. King had made with the honesty of his purpose and the sincerity of his convic- tions, (Cheers.) During the recent election he badnot spent much time in Stockton Heath, having worked in Daresbury and Preston Brook where they met with the kindest receptions. That was particularly lib in regard to Preston Brook where they polled within four of the total electorate, and where it had been a real pleasure to work. (Cheers). This concluded the speech-making and an adjournment was made to Ellesmere-road from which the procession started.</p>
        <p>--En rOute Mr. King delivered two speeches, one in Victoria Square and the other at Lower Walton, his remarks being freely punctuated with cheers. At Victoria Square he said: Ladies and gentlemen I con- gratulate you upon this great demonstration which I take to be an expression of your satisfaction at having won this Division for the cause of Liberalism and Free Trade. (Cheers.) Cheshire has done splendidly. (Cheers and a Voice : "We have cleared them out.") There may be here some of our political opponents and I only hope that now the contest is over nothing but the best feelings will prevail—(hear, hear)—and that I may feel that it is my proud privilege to represent every elector in the division irrespective of political views. cCheers). Ido not ask any of you to waver in your political faith—we are as good Liberals and Free Traders as ever we were—but at the same time let us think of those who are beaten and let us not emphasise their defeat unnecessarily. (Cheers). I feel I have taken over very great responsibilities and I only trust that I may have the strength and wisdom to fulfil those responsibilities in a way which you will approve. (Cheers). Speaking at Walton Mr. King said he did not think any county in the country had done so well as Cheshire and gone so solidly for the Liberal candidates. (Cheers) He hoped and believed the great Liberal majority in the Ilouse of Commons would justify the confidence which the country had placed in the party, and would accom- plish some useful ameliorative legislation which would be for the good of the whole community. (Cheers).</p>
        <p>COMM ERCI AL FAIL, RlG.—According to Kemp's- Mercantile Gazette, the total number of Comirereial Failures reeorded in linglandand Wales during dm week ending Friday, January 26th, was 159, Bankruptcy Proceedings published in tie London Gazette, 94; Deeds of Arrangement registered, 65. rite respective numbers in tie corresponding week of last year were : Bankruptcies, 103 ; Deeds of Arrangement 70—Total 174; being a decrease of 19. The tobal number of Commeroial Failures reoorded durieg the 4 week. of the present year is 623 ; the tol!al lumber recorded in the corresponding 4 weeks of last year was 665, show- ing a dooms* of 42.</p>
      </div>
