      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>MR. R. CAMERON, M.P., INTERVIEWED. A FORECAST OF NEW LEGISLATION. The Position of the Labour Party.</head>
        <p>MR. R. CAMERON, M.P., INTERVIEWED. A FORECAST OF NEW LEGISLATION. The Position of the Labour Party.</p>
        <p>Mr. Robert Cameron. M.P. is one of those "grand old men " of whom the Liberal party and indeed the country have every reason to feel proud. His has been a long life of usefulness to the community in various capacities, and although he has attained his eightieth birthday he is still remarkably active in the public service. He has just emerged from an arduous election campaign with flying colours and once more goes to represent the Durham mining constituency of Houghton-le-Spring in the House of Commons. Mr. Cameron has gained a warm place in the hearts of many Warringtonians who have from time to time listened with pleasure and profit to his eloquent and powerful speeches and sermons and have been moved to profound admiration for his unswerving fidelity to conviction, often in the face of trying circumstances. During the past week Mr. Cameron has been staying. with Mr. James Dodds, a very old friend at Penketh House, and an " Examiner " reporter had the privilege of a short interview with him the other day. Mr. Cameron readily chatted on the political situation, and his opinions will doubtless prove of interest to our readers. The conversation at first naturally turned upon Mr. Cameron's own election experiences. His first Parliamentary fight was in 1892 when \he unsuccess- fully opposed Sir Howard Vincent at Sheffield. Nothing daunted, however, Mr. Cameron came forward as a c/ndidate for the Houghton- le-Spring division, with which he had bag ° been identified, in 1895 and obtained a majority of 881. In 1900, in spite of the fact that he was a strong opponent of the war he was one of the very few Liberals who increased their majorities, the electors returning him by 1948. The recent contest, said Mr. Cameron, came suddenly. It was felt that there was no call for it and the Liberals expected a walk over. "But perhaps " remarked Mr. Cameron " the Tories thought I had better be taken away from helping others." The result was that they received a lesson, Mr. Cameron increasing his majority to 5,790. the largest ever obtained in the county of Durham and certainly one of the largest in England. Mr. Cameron is free to admit that he enjoyed an advantage which no other candidate for the clivisiou could possess, in having frequently lectured to the miners on geology, travela,and science during the past thirty years. He was also instrumental in establish- ing the science and art classes which have become the most successful in England. Some of the miners have obtained as many as eleven certificates, and not a few have turned out rather remarkable men. Owing to the excellent instruction received at the classes a desire for greater knowledge has been created, and some of the former miners are now preachers and doctors. Mr. Cameron, of course, undertook this work before he had the remotest idea of entering Parliament, and it naturally made him a host of friends. During the campaign just concluded Mr. Cameron held about twelve large meetings, all highly enthusiastic and all unanimous. His huge majority has given the ytmost satisfaction, and one of the Northern papeu‘ has aptly likened it to a great steam roller in its crushing effects on Toryism. Messages of congratulation have poured in from all quarters, and one of the earliest to arrive came from Ottawa. Turning to general politics Mr. Cameron expressed his delight at the great wave of Liberalism which has swept through the land. "The election" he said 'l is been so extraordinary in its character, that. one finds it difficult to consider how the House be re-arranged. Of course, the great fact is a clear majority over all parties of 10-1 The government ought to do great things with that majority." "And what, in your opinion,is the conclusion to be drawn from the election results? " asked the inter- viewer. _</p>
        <p>Mr. Cameron was:emphatic in his reply." The results" he said " indicate very definitely that the late Govern- ment was found out and despised by all classes of the community. Ten years of class legislation and very little that was really for the people, added to art extravagant and wasteful war and Increased expendi- ture, created general disgust throughout the country. It strikes me as being a remarkable feature that in spite of the fact that three fourths of the Press were in favour of the Government, and notwithstanding the expected help from the brewing interest, the people's eyes have been opened and they have judged for them- selves as I don't remember them to have done before in all my experience of politics." "In what light do you look upon the fiscal question? " • " I am convinced that that issue was an after- thought of Mr. Chamberlain's. Finding how the bye-elections were going everywhere on his return from South Africa, he thought he would raise a cry that would divert attention from the war and the education question. His failure is the most signal of all. This election settles the fiscal question, but not in Mr. Chamberlain's way." As Mr. Cameron happily remarked at the declara- tion of the pull in his own constituency, he does not fear" the Emperor of Birmingham" any longer, for Mr. Chamberlain is now more like Robinson Crusoe on lfis solitary island, with a fierce sea raging around Lim.</p>
        <p>Asked how he viewed the rise of the Labour mo—e- ment, Mr. Cameron said : " The strength of the Labour Party for the first time in the House is a new feature in English history. There is no doubt that the Labour men will modify many ques- tions and help on measures that affect the working men. J greatly rejoice at the increase of their numbers. I told them fifteen years ago when fighting in Sheffield, that until they had a hundred representatives in Parliament their real wants would never be cared for. I look upon the division among themselves as a small matter. Of course, for the last ten years I have always been in the same lobby with Ken. Hardie." As to the relations between the Liberal party and. the Labour men, Mr. Cameron had no hesitation in saying that he thoug'n the wisest policy of the Liberal i party n friture was to heartily welcome the working- men into Parliament. Mr. Cameron next turned his attention to the work Which confronts the Government. " The Liberals, with all their majority " he remarked "have a hard task before them. They have so much to undo before they begin really to create or to bring out their own programme. I hope, It )wever, for great measures for the poor : housing, amelioration of slum life, a solu- tion of the unemployed question, and real amendments in a national direction of the Education Act. I also hope for the restoration of the magistrates' power, as before, on all licensing questions." " On what basis do you think the education question ehould be settled ?" " I hope, at least, that the lines of settlement will be upon "no sectarianism," and above all freedom from religious tests of the school master arid tha Ashok, staff. They must become national servants, as they are paid by the nation." Mr. Cameron believes that the trade union law,which has aroused such universal indignation among the working classes, is almost certain to be altered name- I diately. The Tories, he said, made a great mistake in not amending it before they relinquished office. They were very mortified afterwards. The coal tax which affects Mr. Cameron's constitu- ency very considerably is, he thinks, sure to be abolished. That tax was another mistake of the Tories.</p>
        <p>"What do you think will be the policy of the Government on the land gnestien ? " asked the</p>
        <p>• •</p>
        <p>the traditions of his family in his veins and in his I brain. It ;is a new departure to bring in a man of the calibre of Mr. Lloyd-George, but the most remarkable of all 'facts that con- cern the workingman is that John Burns is a Cabinet Minister. The public now have a right to expect very much from the Cabinet, but it is to be hoped that while they are steering through the initial difficulties, the people will show that p.tience which the condition of things demands." It has been a source of great disappointment to Mr. Cameron, that owing to the very adverse circumstances in the House he, in common with other Liberal members,has been able to accomplish little during the. past ten years. He, however, introduced the Burials Bill which is now the law of the land and which he hopes will settle the old bitter controversies between' Church and Dissent over the graves of the dead. "It was painful work " he went on "to be in that House for ten years, because most of one's votes and speeches were worthless in face of. the crushing ' majority we had alwasa to fight. Now the House of Commons will be like la new world. Anything that one does will ' tell,' and most sincerely do I hope it will be for the good of the mass of the people, and not of the favoured classes as it has been during the last ten miserable years." During the election Mr. Cameron has been present , at meetings in the north, south, east and west, and never before has he seen such unanimity and enthu- siasm. The mass of the people everywhere were awake and took a passionate interest in the issues. It is a noteworthy circumstance that three of the four candidates whom Mr. Cameron assisted were returned with great majorities. In concluding an interesting conversation, Mr. Cameron expressed his gratifiCation at the great 11 Liberal triumph in Warrington. He has visited ' Warrington a few times lately, and he was exeeedingly delighted to find that in the election it was not behind other towns. "I am indeed glad " said Mr. Cameron " that you have joined in with the flowing tide."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>BISHOPS IN PARLIAMENT.</head>
        <p>BISHOPS IN PARLIAMENT.</p>
        <p>Their Attitude on Great Questions</p>
        <p>The attitude of the Bishops of the Church of England upon important questons introduced into Parliament formed the subject of a lecture delivered by the Rev. Dr. Karfoot, M.A., of Leigh, at a meeting of the Cairo- street Unitarian Young People's Society, held in the schools on Wednesday evening. The Rev. C. Harvey Cook presided over a fair-attendance. Dr. K A AFOOT at the outset explained that from Hie earliest time' the Bishops had had a place amongst the' legislators of the country but when a new Bishopric was created for Manchester it was seen that no longer ought every Bishop to be included in the House of Lords simply because he was a bishop and it was arranged that the number Should be confined to 24. Sixty years ago therefore we saw the principle adopted' that a man should not sit in the House of Lords simply bemuse he was a bishop but as Nonconformists they desired to see the 21 reduced to none. (Hear, hear). A number of objections could be raised to their presence. One was on the ground of religious equality. Was it right that a clergyman of one particular chu'rch should be admitted to the House of Lords while the clergyman of another church was kept out ? (Hear, hear.) It was distinctly unfair that it should be so, but he would ask them to judge the Bishops by the results of their efforts as legislators. In 1807 an important measure was brought before the country for the educa- tion of the children, called the Parochial Schools Bill, in which magistrates were given the power of saying where schools were required, and of directing where those schools should be built at the expense of the country. Although that Bill passed the House of Commons, so strong was the opposition of the Bishops on the ground that it would interfere with the clergy that the House of Lords rejected it. The consequence was that years went by before steps were taken to see that the children of the count y ware properly educated. In 1810 the criminal laws were such that four hundred crimes were punishable by death. The House of Commons sent bills to the Upper House time after time but they were rejected. When at last a Bill was passed, the Bishops by 7to none opposed it. It was natural to have expected that Christian ministers would have been anxious to bring about reforms instead of rejecting them. (Hear, hear.) In 1829 when the Roman Catholic Emancipation Bill was intro- duced, the Bishops by 19 to 10 were against it. Twenty-one Bishops voted against the Reform Bills of 1831, while only two supported those measures. Ulti- mately the vote changed to 15 against and 12 for. Up I to 1834 f Nonconformists were excluded from the universities on account of their religious beliefs. Three times when a Bill to alter this was placed before the House of Lords the bishops voted 2 for, 22 against ; for, 3 against ; and none for and three against, thus once more proving themselves too conservative. Prior to 1839 there was no education department for England and it was thought that year that popular schools should be introduced. The Bishops were once more iu opposition on the ground that an education department would interfere with the privileges which the clergy held with regard to education. When the education measure was passed the Archbishop of Canterbury and the Bishops even went to the extent of sending an address to Queen Victoria asking her to cancel the Bill. The Bishops were equally opposed to the measure for removing Jewish diabilities. The Bishops professed to be the friends of the poor people but he was sorry to say they by their acts did not show themselves in that light as by 27 votes to 9 they opposed Cobden's proposals. Mr. I.rfoot proceeded to show how the Bishops had opposed the abolition of the Church rates and the Deceased NVlfe's Sister's Bill. He woceeded to deal with the attitude of the Bishops in regard to the Education Act lei 1902. In that Act the Church of England was dealt with liberally but the Bishops wanted more. They wanted to place the cost of the maintenance of the schools on the rates and their action called from the Bishop of Hereford the expression that it was a game of grab. They even went so far as to add to the Bill that the schools should be maintained out of the public rates provided it should not be any additional charge on any public fund. This addition was struck out when the Bill returned to the House of Commons. Judged by the results he thought the Bishops should leave the House of Lords. (Hear, hear.) They had no business there and the sooner the country was rid of them the better it would he for the country. (Applause.) Dr. Karfoot was heartily thanked for his lecture on the motion of Mr. Pemberton seconded by Mr. Ms.deley.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>DRAUGHTS.</head>
        <p>DRAUGHTS.</p>
        <p>WARRIIiGTON V. LIVERPOOL Sr. JAM es.—The return match between Warrington and the Liverpool Club was played last Saturday at Bibby's Restaurant, Market- street, Warrington, and resulted in a complete reversal of the previous match, Warrington coming out His winners on this occasion by a majority of four games. This is a victory the Warrinkton players may well feel proud of, as the St. James' Club is one of the strongest in the county, and only two eeasons ago held the Lanca- shire Championship. Score :-.• WARRINGTON. ST. JAMES'. Wins. Draws, Wins. 2 F. Dunne 1 W. Tomkinson ...... 1 A. E. Fer 1 E. J. Powell 0 W Cummins 1 J. Lightfoot 0 1 Percy Dunne 1 W. Pilling o 0 J. Hardman 1 W. Smeilie 1 J. H. Parker 0 0 G. Clare I W. Carson 1 0 E. Norbury 0 F. Kenny ' 2 J. A. Fleming 1 J. Rowe .l T. Jacks 0</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>WARRINGTON DRAUGHTS LEAGUE.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON DRAUGHTS LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>1 WARRINGTON D. &amp; C.C. V. MONKS HATA. ANT) CO.— Played on Tuesday evening at Bibby's Restaurant. Score :- 1 WARRINGTON. Wins. Draws. Wins. 1 1 F. Dunne I 1 W. Rigby 2 E. P. Dunne 0 H. Powell 0 1 J. Hamblett 0 G. Clare 1 2 T. Hardman 2 J. Connolly 1* B. Norbury .- ...... 0 W. Talbot, sen 0 10</p>
        <p>MONKS HALL.</p>
