      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, February 3, 1906.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, February 3, 1906.</p>
        <p>to be tranny. We shill, no doubt, differ in opinions as we do in features. No two minds, we are told, think exactly alike. But whilst we shall discover errors in the creed of one another yet we shall not magnify those differences. It is the essence of the Liberal creed that it not only tolerates but encourages diversity of individual opinion ; and this characteristic of our party will never, we trust, be lost. Man has a right to think all things, speak all things, write] all things—but not to impose his opinions. We shall reason with one another, believing with Macaulay that" men are never so likely to settle a question rightly as when they discuss it freely." And, now gentlemen, in conclusion, as we view the present political situation we can say, as Cicero pro- claimed in the Forum, the season of liberty has now come. What we have hitherto suffered has been owing to a kind of fatality, which we have borne as well as we could; but if any such case should happen again it must be owing to ourselves. The affair is now reduced to the last extremity ; the struggle is for freedom—the proper end and business of the British people. Lot us strive after a true vision of Empire—an Empire bound together by claims of brotherhood and not by the sordid lust of gain. Our aspirations may not make us, individually, richer—if we count riches according to the number of our menials—and we may be misunder- stood—of what matter ?—but we shall have joy in knowing that by our endeavours we have helped to make some lives happy and that the name of Empire is beloved of the people. Taking progress as our watch- word and truth our shield, we will make war, not upon the helpless poor, but upon the unjust strong, and by doing so hasten on the day when Liberty shall be the password of all nations and equity and justice shall be their aim. Mr. J. HAWTRORN said Mr. Osborne had referred to the late Government's" departed greatness." He rather thought it should have been" departed slothfulness," or something of that sort. (Laughter). He believed tile Labour movement had been the result of dissatisfaction with the social conditions of to-day, and'he hoped and believed the new Progressive Government would leave behind it a record of useful work in that direction. (Hear, hear). One of the greatest problems at the heart of the social question was the more equal distribution of wealth—the removing of the disparity between lavish wealth on the one hand, and extreme poverty on the other. (Applause). Mr. H. GRAY said the Liberals had had a grand cause to fight for, and the era just entered upon would make its mark on English history. He did not agree with Mr. Hawthorn upon the question of the equalization of wealth. He believed that was one of the errors of socialism. He did not think it was by any means the greatest factor in our social life, and there was no doubt that the mere seeking for material gain to the exclusion of all else, was eating into the heart'of the nation. By all means let them have equality of opportunity by a proper system of education, and let everyone be made to feel that he was part of the community, and that it was his duty to strive to bring about the betterment of that community. (Hear, hear.) Mr. OSBORNE thought Mr. Gray had misinterpreted Mr. Hawthorn's meaning. Mr. Gray had an exaggera- ted and unfair idea of what Socialism meant.</p>
        <p>Mr. H. W. GUEST said the country was in for a period of calm after the General Election, and they could utilize that time to educate themselves on political questions. They could study the actions of the Govern- ment, and, if necessary, criticize the action of their own party. He proposed a vote of thanks to Mr. Osborne for his exvellent and interesting address. Mr. RATHBONE seconded., Messrs. Hutu and H. BLEASE spoke in support;Fand it was carried with acclamation.</p>
        <p>Mr.OssoftNe briefly returned thanks. In answer to Mr. Gray, he said he could not briefly define Socialism, and promised to address the Association on that question.</p>
        <p>THE UNEMPLOYED. What the Warrington Distress Committee is Doing. A meeting of the Warrington Distress Committee formed under the Unemployed Workmen Act, 1905 was held at the Town Hall on Wednesday evening under the presidency of Alderman Tinnion. There were also present r—Mrs. Harrison, Mrs. Clay, Mrs. Wilkin- son Miss Broadbent, the Revs. Dr. Mercer, J. Yonge, and W. Bracecamp, Councillors Stott, Wilkinson,Wright Wilson, Barton and Skellasd, Messrs. S. Davies, A. J. Jolley, C. Haley, A. T. Hallaway (deputy town clerk), and 3. Fargher (clerk and inquiry officer).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>WARRINGTON JUNIOR LIBERAL ASSOCIATION.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON JUNIOR LIBERAL ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>THE POLITICAL OUTLOOK.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Warrington Junior Liberal Associa- tion was held at Gregory's Cafe on Monday when Mr. E. Osborne, the president of the Association, presided over an excellent attendance. The meeting was called to hear the Chairman's presidential address. Mr. OSBORNE, who was heartily received said : I cannot easily describe to you the sense of pleasure and pride, and I must add of great responsibility as well, with which I address you this evening. You base been pleased to confer upon me a great honour, in electing me your President, and I tender to you my grateful and warm acknowledgements. I sincerely hope that our meetings together from time to time and our continued activities in search of political knowledge may conduce to much good, and that they may be helpful not only to ourselves but also to those with whom we have occasion to be daily associated. To-night, I am here to speak upon the present political situation and to offer, if I may, some observations thereon together with what I consider to be the policy of the members of this Association.</p>
        <p>In the first place, the respect due to departed great- ness makes it incumbent upon me to refer to the decease of the last Tory Government. Probably, no General Election has ever been, carried on with more spirit and determination than has the present, and from the returns to hand—and they are practically complete—there is reason to believe that most of the principal con- stituencies have followed the splendid lead of Man- chester, thereby giving a strength to the Progressive forces hitherto unknown. We, in Warrington, together with the electors in other parts of the country have , manifested, in no undecided manner, our disapprobation of the last Tory Government. And I should like to take this, the first opportunity, of conveying to the members of this Association the thanks of Mr. Arthur H. Crosfield for their arduqus and energetic labours on his behalf.</p>
        <p>In Mr. Crosfield we have a representative pledged to a Progressive policy, and I will now only add that I am sure I am voicing the feelings of the members in wish- ing him success in his efforts, and health and strength to carry out his desires. The late administrators if not entirely obliterated, have, at any rate, received such a blow that their programme and ideals will have to be considerably altered before they dare again appeal for the support of the people. Why all this resentment ? To reason upon the impolicy of the late disastrous war is foreign to our purpose, but we cannot help think- ing that, in accomplishing the overthrow of an enemy, in some respects more generous than some of our repre- sentatives in South Africa have proved themselves to be, we have not only defeated the object for which it was commenced, but we have laid ourselves open to attack because we have sacrificed, to some extent, that policy of freedom which we have striven long to estab- lish, and have in a great measure maintained. Emanating from Birmingham, gloomy and desponding reports, as to the degeneracy of BRITISH TRADE</p>
        <p>and traders have been widely circulated by certain disciples of a discontented aristocracy. All the while trade and commerce have flourished. But whilst that is so the benefits attendant upon them have not increased to the same extent and in the same ratio. And why? The employer of labour, in order to compete with other markets has to deduct from the wages of his workpeople what he pays in exorbitant taxation. The cry of the workpeople is beard. Reform is certainly needed—not Protection from the foreigner—but a reform levelled against unjust profits of landowners in the form of royalties; unfair charges for the conveyance of merthandize in unduly high railway rates ; and oppres- sive taxes caused by an unwarranted expenditure on% wanton foreign policy. The remedy is to be found not so much in foreign but in local areas. If the parties concerned will divest themselves of selfish unearned increment, a cure will soon be found to remedy the ills our trade suffers from without resorting to methods of continual strife and unpleasant relationships with con- tinental nations, _ _ _</p>
        <p>Now that the Liberals have been returned to power with such an unprecedented majority we trust that they will at once begin to set their house in order, to study the true interests of the people and to revise the old and absolete land laws. Land tenure will have to be seriously dealt with in the near future and nothing short of a complete reform in the administration of the country's affairs will suffice. Equity and justice—two main planks in the Liberal platform—must be meted out to rich and poor alike, and a desire shewn to assist in a practical manner, those who are incapaciated,either by age or loss of employment, to regain in some measure the happiness and comfort which they as citizens of a mighty Empire are fully entitled to. The public mind is at present chiefly concerned with the desire to know what results may be expected now that such great power has been placed in the hands of the GOT ernment. We do not pretend to prophesy but we foresee changes which are necessary to make this nation a happy and a contented people. We sincerely hope—and we believe—that the Liberal Party will come up to our expectations and that measures will be intro- duced which will be benefical and equitable to the whole community. We rejoice to know that Labour has, at last, been recognized to have a place and play a part in the management of the country's affairs, and, we trust, by an earnest endeavour to work together for the common good, the progressive forces will long rule the destiny of the Empire. Fair representation of the people is necessary, and to infuse into the House of Commons, men familiar with the needs and wants of the great gpass of the population of the kingdom, is absolutely Wssential to a just and consistent constitution. The Labour question is a growing and neccessitous problem. The relations of CAPITAL AND LABOUR are steadily evolving and are gradually reaching a stage when careful and earnest consideration are required to attain an equitable understanding. From earliest times there has been a desire on the part of some to live by the efforts of others. From owner and slave, lord and serf, master and servant the relations have gradually evolved until we reach another stage—that of employer and employed. Each has its own advantages and responsibilities. The commanding power of the employer has hitherto been great, and, doubtless in some instances, it has been tyrannical. We hope, now that the employed has realised his strength, that reasonable negotiation and a desire for justice will tend towards a better understanding between the different sections of society. Whilst bread is essential for existence, yet" man doth not live by bread alone," but has ideals and inclinations which, from lack of oppor- tunities, have been allowed "To-die ;—to sleep To sleep !—Perchance to dream ; " Now; the awakening has come! and sorae speak I timidly and with bated breath as to the possible results of such a large representation of the people. We confess we are not alarmed, believing that 1 the artisans will act with justice towards their political antagonists, and knowing that there is no desire on the part of the overwhelming multitude, in the name of Labour, to establish a new despotism of the many over the few, but to convert the love for aristocracy, which has hitherto been almost a religion, into a love for equality and freedom. Dryden said : You say a long descended race, And wealth, and dignity, and power, and place, Make gentlemen ; and that your high degree Is much disparaged to be matched with me. Know this, my lord, nobility of blood Is but a glittering and fallacious good ; The nobleman is he whose noble mind Is fill'd with inborn wealth, Unborrow'd from his kind. _ _</p>
        <p>• What are the members of the Junior Liberal Associa- tion going to do ? I well remember when Mr. Harold Gray—who has worked so indefatigably to form this Association, and to whose energy and ability we are, as a body, so deeply indebted—came to see me to ask me for my support in the movement, I hesitated a little, because I felt that perhaps young men who had not, as yet, any decided opinions with regard to politics might. be dubious in joining even a progressive party and being dabbed Liberal before we really knew what was meant by the term and its associations. lie assured me that the members were, so far as he knew, desirous of meet- ing together for purely educational purposes and that they were desirous of studying great social as well as political questions. in fact, that it was knowledge and not party tactics that they were.aimingat.l consented then If I were asked why I am a Liberal I should say that I believed a Liberal to be a man who did not refuse to his adversary the equity and iustice he demanded for himself. That a Liberal believed in progress and reform —and in not sticking to some doctrine merely because it was old,and had age only to back it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>TORY POLICY</head>
        <p>TORY POLICY</p>
        <p>we are told, is this—"fora long time to resist a good me tsure in principle, and when it beJomes too late to resist it in principle, and it has become necessary to acknowledge the principle on which it is founded, then to cripple it by taking from it a number of the attri- butes that are necessary in order to give it full, practical and substantial effect.' I believe that is quite true, as we have only to think of the Unemployed Act as a fair sample of Tory equivocation. Carlyle once said" he could not attempt to conserve Conservatism, believing it to be a sham, a curse of God, and doomed to destruc- tion—as all lies were." What is our mission? Already the Association has given an example as to its merits and from the syllabus now in hand we shall observe a continuation of those efforts. We,l take it,are determined not to be a nominal. association but an active and intelligent organization By lectures on various political problems, and confer- ences with one and another, we shall strive to be conversant with and capable of defending the great progressive principles. Public speaking is to be encouraged. A Library is to be established and reading circles formed. In fact what we want is to become students of social and political problems, taking politics is a serious business we, will endeavour I hope to read both sides of a ques- tion so that we may not become bigoted. We shall not be tied down to party for party purposes, alone. If some say to us "in our way or not at all.—You stall eat your porridge with our spoon or have no food." I that we will have nothing to do with, believing it</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>FINDING WORK FOR ;rll UNEMPLOYED.</head>
        <p>FINDING WORK FOR ;rll UNEMPLOYED.</p>
        <p>- The Cif-UR:SIAN said the various departments of the corntnibtee had now got to work and he thought the results were quite as favourable as most of them expected, under the somewhat fettered condition in which the Law allowed them to net. The total number of names registered was 114-105 men 9 women. Work had already been obtained for 13 men, leaving on the register 911 men and 9 women. The names of all unemployed painters who were registered had been submitted to the Borough Surveyor's department with a view to finding them work in the painting of the markets, preference of course, being given to married men. The names on the register included those of 15 men betweemthe ages of fifty and sixty, and five of the age of sixty and upwards.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>'TILE FINANCE AND GENERAL PURPOSES CONIMMEE.</head>
        <p>'TILE FINANCE AND GENERAL PURPOSES CONIMMEE.</p>
        <p>This Committee resolved that Mr. Councillor Wilkin- son be appointed chairman, and Mr. A. J. Jolley, the deputy chairman for the ensuing year ; that the Com- mittee meet at 6.30 p.m., on a day to be hereafter fixed; that the Finance and Estate Committee of the Town Council be requested to advance the monies required by the Distress Committee until the end of the present financial year ; that a summary of the persons registered at the Labour Bureau be printed and sent out weekly to the various employers of labour in the Borough, and that a certificate of registration be issued to unemployed parsons • that the Electricity and Tramways Committee be asked to allow the Inquiry Officer to use the tram cars on the same terms as Corporation servants. Mr. JOLLEY moved the confirmation of the minutes. He said it was thought that the certificate of registra- tion would facilitate the securing of employment by some persons. The Rev. Dr. MERCER seconded and the minutes were approved. INQUIRY AND LABOUR COMMITTEE. At a meeting of this committee it was decided that Mr. A. .1. Jolley be appointed Chairman and the Rev. John Yonge the Deputy-Chairman for the ensuing year; ddidaltensquirit was decided that the meetings of this Committee be held at 6.30 p.m. on a day to be hereafter fixed. The Committee I considered the applications received for the position Officer. After i v candidates that Mr. James Fargher be appointed Clerk and Inquiry Officer at wages of 30s. per week, and that he commence eriev ci nog f Clerk r k his duties on the 27th, instant. It was resolved that the form of notice of the opening of the Labour Bureau now submitted be approved, and that small bills be printed and distributed throughout the borough. Mr. JOLLEY moved the confirmation of the minutes. He said he did not think it desirable to express any opinion as to the success or otherwise of the Labour bureau at this stage. They ought to give it a full and fair trial and he hoped something useful might result. Probably the committee would meet again in the course of a week to go into the applications received andhea- from the officer a complete statement as to the work he had done. About fifty of the cases registered had, he understood, been investigated. Councillor Wittoirr seconded. The Rev. W. BRACECAMP inquired whether the bills announcing the opening of the bureau had been circu- lated. Several men had made application to him and said they had never seen the bills and did not know where to find the bureau. Mr. HALL AWAY said 500 bills had been distributed in the borough. The Itev. W. BRACECAMP said it was important that the bills should go into the poorer parts of the town where there were many men needing work. Several members of the committee expressed the opinion that the bill was too mall and that many people would not be able to read it.</p>
        <p>Rev. Dr. MERCER: Mr. Bracecamp and myself are perha s both afflicted with a class of people who suffer from chronic bad eyesight when work is concerned. (Laughter). A suggestion wls made that the bills should be affixed to the doors of places of worship and it was stated that posters would probably be placed on the hoardings at an early date. Mr. DAVIES asked if any temporary relief had been given to tho applicants for work. Had. the Committee any money at their disposal? Mr. HALEY inquired if the places found for some applicants had been provided by the Corporation or by private employers. The CHAIRMAN : I understand that they were found by outsiders practically, but through the agency of this committee. Mr. Jor LEY, in replying to the discussion, said the work was in its purelyexperiruental stage, and the Com- mittee were to some extent feeling their way. Dr. Mercer touched one blot very emphatically in his observations. It was a trouble he (Mr. Jolley) was fully cognisant of, and he thought some members of the Committee realized it also. Perhaps one feature ought to be a comforting consideration. If some members were inclined to be disappointed at what they considered the fewness of the applicants, it was perhaps an evidence of the good state of trade in the town, for which they ought to be thankful. The Distress Committee was not allowed to give pecuniary relief, and had no funds. They might help people by endeavouring to find them work, or in certain instances providing work and helpiir, them to remove to other places where they might 12 employed. They had, however, no authority to give actual pecuniary assistance ; that was a matter for the Guardians. The minutes were approved.</p>
