      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>round, and Isherwood's strained tendon is not expected to be sound any sooner. Dickinson may be on the "A" team any wk. His knee is reported strong again. --- Findlow, a young half-back from Broughton has been signed on by Warrington, and Lambeth, the Salford St. Bart's half who made such a good impres- sion on Saturday, is also expected to signed on shortly</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>Rugby Football.</head>
        <p>Rugby Football.</p>
        <p>[By " Ovalist."] 1 After experiencing the hardest of luck for several ' weeks in being very narrowly defeated,Stockton Heath Warrington effected quite a mild sensation on on Saturday won handsomely. Their opponents were Saturday by beatingthe Salford men on their native Bolton who had to admit defeat by 20 points to 9 after heath. The "native Heath "by the way, scored a hard fought game. To-day the Heathens first team against hieold club,but that is another story. Readers go to Preston to meet the Grasshoppers second fifteen, of these notes will recollect that I said last week that and there is every possibility of their record being Warrington could win, if they tried. On improved. The team is Langly, full back; Bouchier, Saturday they tried to some purpo'se and; Bell, Ferguson and B Bradshaw, three-quarter hacks ; Salford had to take a back seat. As the " Reds '.I Davenport and P. Hazlehurst, half-backs ; Parker, won at Warrington somewhat luckily by a solitary , Cook, Daintith, K. Gibbs, Fawcett, Skelton, Smith point, this counter-stroke by the cup-holders is parti- ; and Owen, forwards. The second team entertain cularly gratifying. Perhaps they have taken it into Manchester University "B" and have a hard game their heads that it is time they lifted the club out of before them. the groove into which it has fallen. They have shown us that points can be obtained even away from home, ? and the bulk of the remaining League engagements ought certainly to be won without much difficulty.</p>
        <p>To say that Saturday's result was a surprise to Salford, as well as Warrington people, is to put it mildly. After drawing at home with Broughton Rangers they did not anticipate being taken down by Warrington—a team which has appeared somewhat of a spent force this season. But after the first ten minutes, the homosters were overplayed to a sufficient extent to give Warrington the victory, and the Reds' supporters had to swallow down hard lumps of humiliation.</p>
        <p>At the commencement, I thought Warrington were going to be " wiped off the map," so energetically did the Reds begin. They handled the ball with surpris- ing dexterity, and Lomas twice made the Warrington defence look very poor in the first five minutes. Once ho was brought down a stride from the line, but the second time he romped over. At this point, it looked about a million to one on Salford. They were getting possession and tossing the ball about in a dangerous. fashion, and I quite thought Warrington's chance had gone when the home team scored first. The Warring- ton men rallied finely, however, and playing closer up, they checked Salford's tendencies to juggling. By and by they forced their way into the home quarter, where Thomas and Harris both came near to scoring. The equaliser came suddenly, however, from Hocken- hall, who showed his best form in a wonderful zig-zag run round the pack, through the home backs, and over the line in good position. It Was ' such a smart piece of work that the home supporters cheered. Preston should have kicked a goal, but he failed here, and again with an easy penalty shot. ' The interval arrived with the teams on level terms.</p>
        <p>There was an air of confidence about the Warring- ton men on resuming. They knew they had nothing to fear, bar Lomas, and their prospects of at least ono point were exceedingly bright. A change of policy was at once made manifest. Finding that they were not heeling out often enough,the Warrington forwards went in for wheeling and rushing every now and then. These tactics were well carried out, and proved very I successful. The home halves were qtrite thrown off their play, and Cochrane at full back had a very lively time. F-om one of these sudden rushes Heath, the old Salford forward, came away with the ball at his toes. He had fully 25 yards to go, but he dribbled on speedily and actually steered the ball past 1 the full back and over the line for a grand try. It was a bitter pill to be administered by the hand of an ' old " nal," but the Salford folk were the first to admit that Heath was a rare good forward. Of this there can be no doubt, for he has been conspicuously useful in every match recently.</p>
        <p>Hockenhall added a goal from Heath's try, and the Salford men found themselves five points in arrears. They set to with a will, and there was a battle royal until the close. Warrington were compelled to fall back before the fierce rushes of Lomas and his men,but their tackling was better at this period of the game than any. The cup-holders were warming to ho work and the game looked very like a cup-tie. How- ever Salford would not be denied, and Lomas taking a pass from D. Preston ran like a lion through the Warrington backs and grounded at the corner. There was a want of decision about Harris and Taylor at this point or the try should have been saved. They made the fatal mistake of waiting for Lomas to pass. This raised the home score to within a couple of points of Warrington's total and Lomas took great pains with the goal-kick. The angle was difficult, and the ball failed to cross the bar, though perilously near. This appeared to be the limit to Salford's endeavour. They cracked up afterwards and Warrington were near adding to their score more than once in the closing stages.</p>
        <p>On the home side, the one outstanding figure was "Jimmy "Lomas, who played with great dash and cleverness. Without him the Reds must have been out- classed, for the rest of the threequarter line was never in the hunt with the Warrington four. 1 Preston and John again gave a smart display behind a good pack of " heelers," and their passing was quicker and better-judged than that of Hockenhall and Lloyd. The latter pair were in good form, and the " inter- national" seems to have benefited by his rest. Taylor was the best of the threequarters and but for some poor passes he would have probably made a hcle in the home defence. No man could have worked harder, The forwards, though beaten at " heeling," were more dangerous in the loose than the Reds, and they never slackened. Berry had a severe afternoon's work from which he emerged with distinct credit and numerous bruises. His field- ing and kicking were excellent and in other respects he compared very favourably with Cochrane. He has the " makings " of a good full-hack.</p>
        <p>ft is just possible that after to-day's matches Broughton Rangers may head the league. They easily upset Hunslet on Saturday, and with Leigh, Halifax and Hunslet all away again they stand a big chance of going to the top. Once there and they will need a lot of moving, especially seeing that Wilson is tit again.</p>
        <p>The League table up-to-date is as follows : Foe. Agst. Per- P. W. L. D. P. G. T. P. G. T. P. centage llonsiet 2l 17 4 03146 50 242 12 23 93 80-95 Leigh Bro'ton R2l 16 4 13334 76 296 18 20 96 76.57 Halifax__ 23 15 2 63629 41 181 16 13 71 7826 Oldham 25 19 5 13931 68 266 19 14 SO 7800 andlield l9 12 5 22621 38 156 14 19 85 68.42 Rrincorn 20 13 6 12730 33 165 10 18 74 6730 Bradford 2l 13 6 22833 58 240 21 25 117 6666 Hull K.R 24 15 7 23226 39 169 29 26 136 66.66 Keighley 2l 14 7 02830 50 210 19 26 116 6666 Leeds gan Salford 22 12 S 22331 52 218 22 18 98 5909 St. Helens 19 11 8 02227 43 183 21 27 123 5789 Wak'fleld T 22 10 8 42431 32 158 24 34 150 51'54 ISwinton 2l 10 10 12122 29 131 19 23 122 50'00 Warrington 25 12 12 12528 35 161 21 32 138 5000 Pontefract 20 811 11725 28 134 24 30 138 42.50 Hull 23 913 119 29 32 154 23 35 151 4130 York " 8 13 1 17 26 20 112 26 36 160 38.63 Bramley Barrow l9 513 11111 19 79 37 63 263 2894 Dewsbury 24 617 11313 22 92 29 43 187 27•081 Castleford l3 3 9 1 711 6 40 35 51 223 26921 Batley 2l 414 31123 12 82 30 31 153 26'19 Widnes l7 312 2 8 411 41 17 37 145 23521 RochdaleH 22 215 5 912 21 93 24 59 225 2045 Milton] l3 210 1 512 10 54 29 51 211 19•23 I Brighouseß 19 215 2 612 12 60 32 61 247 15181 Morecambe 16 213 1 510 20 SO 26 49 199 15621 Normanton 17 114 2 4 5 4 22 28 46 191 1116 The points are two for a win and one for a draw. The percentage is calculated upon the basis of the total points possible.</p>
        <p>Warrington should record the first of three more successive victories to-day, with Millom as visitors. The following Saturday they return the visit at Mill= and the week after that are due at Barrow. By then Warrington ought to have got in such winning form that they should have got a good chance at Bradford, on the last Saturday in this month. Then, ho ! for the Cup-ties</p>
        <p>The team for to-day contains a couple of alterations from the one at Salford. Hockenhall has had to stand down again owing to bruises and his place is taken h.sz Lees, of Chadderton, who played such a good game for the "A " team Last Saturday. Jones replaces Brooks at centre, and the team will be : Berry, full back ; Kenyon, Jones, Taylor and Harris, three- quarters ; Lees and Lloyd, half backs; Boardman, Preston, Thomas, Shugars, Naylor, Heath, Belton and Harmer, forwards.</p>
        <p>The injured list still contains some well-known names. Fish has been seen on crutches this week, a visit to Allison's having disclosed the fact that there was a small bone broken in his leg. This will pro- ably keep him out of the team until the cup-ties come</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>Hockey.</head>
        <p>Hockey.</p>
        <p>The Latchford men were rudely surprised on Saturday when they entertained the Widnes Club. The latter succumbed to Latchford earlier in the season at Widnes by four goals to none, but on Saturday they took ample revenge by scoring six goals to Latchford' one. Latch- ford were not at full strength but they never expected a licking. Widnes however, played a very dashing game and fairly earned the honours, even if the spore was a little flattering to them. Knight got Latchford's only goal from a penalty bully.</p>
        <p>Stockton Heath accomplished a smart performance on Saturday by beating South Manchester away by 3 goals to 1. The backs and centre-half were in capital form, while the scorers were S. Baxter (2) and Kirk- connel (1).</p>
        <p>To-day, the Stockton Ist team match is cancelled,but the 2nd eleven are at home to Alderley Edge. Team : Kertland, goal ; Hawtin and Silcock, backs ; Guest, Jones, and J. Houghton, half-backs ; Baxter, L. Pier- point, Howard, Cliffe, and C. S. Woods, forwards. Bully-off 2.30; referee, Mr. N. W. Bellamy.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>BEWSEY ROAD WESLEYAN CHURCH.</head>
        <p>BEWSEY ROAD WESLEYAN CHURCH.</p>
        <p>Foreign Missionary Anniversary.</p>
        <p>The foreign missionary anniversary in connection with this church was celebrated on Sunday, the offer- tories being on behalf of the Wesleyan Methodist Missionary Society which is doing a splendid work in distant lands. There were good congregations. The preacher at the morning service was the Rev. A. Cecil Parker and in the evening theßev. Sidney Pitt occupied the rostrum. On Tuesday evening the annual public meeting was held, and was fairly well attended. Mr. W. Hickman presided, and the principal speaker was the Rev. NV. Bridle, a returned missionary from China. A report on the work of the Missionary Society during the past year, was read by the Rev. Win. Mellor. The total income was £169,536, an increase of £3,300. The expenditure, however, was £B,OOO in excess of the income. The Liverpool district, of which Warrington forms a part, subscribed £8,315, and the amount raised by the Warrington circuit was £261 Bs. 7d., an increase o 21.1185. The Bewsey-road Church had been making pro- gress in its foreign missionary contributions during the past three years and the amount handed over last year was £n Is. Gd. an increase of £2 135.1 d. That again was an increase upon the proceeding year of £5 17s. 2d. If the financial report of the Society was somewhat dis- appointing, the record of work done was eminently satisfactory and was a cause of devout thankfulness to Almighty God. No greater opportunity had ever been preseuted for making Christ's name a power among the people in China than was at present afforded, while in South Africa the work was a pre-eminent success. All the work abroad called with louder and more urgent voice for help. From every missionary district came cries for additional men and new and better equipment. Their policy at present must be to meet the more urgent and necessitous claims and to have faith in God. The Rev. S. Pitt briefly commended the work of the Society to the increased support of the congregation. The Chairman expressed his sympathy with the missionary cause as being one which had brought bless- ings to the downcast and had purified and uplifted human life.</p>
        <p>1 The Rev. W. Bridie delivered a highly interesting address on the work in China. He emphasised the fact that China was not an uncivilized country. Some people seemed to have the idea tkat China was some- thing like the coast of Africa or some islands of the sea, but as a matter of fact they had in China one of the most ancient civilizations the world had ever seen. They did not go to China to reform its government, so far as theory was concerned, because they had in China the most perfect Government theoretically that the world had ever seen. There was also much that was beautiful in Chinese family life. The great systems of philosophy has, however, proved ineffectual to save China. The Ration had been humiliated and was still in a position of humiliation, although possessing so many natural advantages. The Chinese race was one which bade well for the future. The speaker said he challenged the statement that Christianity had not been very suceessful, at least ,in its beginnings,in China, especially when they realized the enormous difficulties in the hearts of the people. They shuddered as to what the future of China was going to be. Was China going to come under the influence of the agnosticism and militarism of Japan, or was it going to take the more blessed and peaceful way of the Church of Christ ? To-day there were in China 150,000 Christians and there was no ques- tioning the reality of the faith of those converts. They were hard to win but when once they were won they stood firm. The Chinese were destined to play a great part in the history of the world and now was the time for the Churches to grasp their opportunity. During the evening missionary hymns were sung, Mr. B. Wadsworth presiding at the organ.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
