      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-02-03_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>BRUTAL RUNCORN SAILORS.</head>
        <p>BRUTAL RUNCORN SAILORS.</p>
        <p>At Runoorn on Thursday before Messrs. Lnison and Littler, two sailors named John Russell Couch, of the schooner William Henry, of Poole, and Alfred Norton, andoaft clay. 0, when he He spent of the schooner G. T. S., of Poole, were charged with assaulting Peter Gic, a seaman on the schooner John Farley, of Cornwall. The prosecutor whose face was in a shocking condition, said he was a Chilian. He hadm s returning df raoymevePnaitrnor g iwhni ai tßhsuhnai Runcorn, he was attacked by the cargorn, pw‘cVroaiesdoneeners and another,man. Norton struck him over the left eye with a knife, and Conch also struck him.— Robert Moore,, a Ship canal constable, said when he come on the si•cne Gic was lying' over the canal wall in Percival-lane, stunned and helpless. Norton was beating him unmercifully, and was dashing his face on the wall. Couch was standing alongside. Gic's face was like raw beef.—The prisoners were remanded for further evidence to be obtained.</p>
      </div>
