        <p>Hotel-keeper and his Friends. A curious case was heard on Wednesday at Prod- sham Petty Sessions. A month ago Charles Sampson, proprietor of the Belle Monte Hotel, applied for an occasional licence, for extension of an hour, in order th entertain his friends. The magistrates' clerk told him he did not require such a licence. On the night of the party, after closing hours, Sergeant Breeze and Constable Rowlands visited the hotel, where-there anheeda Mr. ssoatmtnpdssonof rteovldelrtyh.emOtnhatthetiiriegapeinrsinognasdmhaitdt supper and were his friends. He admitted that the people had paid for supper, but were now being entertained by him. As a result sixteen persons were charged with being on the premises after hours. Mr. Cherton. for the defence, described the prosecution as iniquitous. The magistrates said there had evidently been a misunderstanding, and fined each defendant is. and costs, suggesting that the licensee should pay the fines.</p>
