        <p>BOARD OF AGRICULTURE AND FISHERIES. PROPOSED EXCHANGE OF LANDS. THE BOARD OF AGRICULTURE AND FISHERIES HEREBY GIVE NOTICE that the Trustees of the Hardsbaw Estates belonging to the Hardsbaw East and Hardsha w West Monthly Meetings of the Society of Friends and the London and North-Western Railway Company have applied, under the provisions of the Incloture Acts, 1845 to 1899, for the Exchange of certain lands, in which the said Trustees are the persons interested, situate in the County Borough Of St. Helens in the County of Lancaster containing Seventy - three square yards and being part of No. 301 on the ordnance map (edition 1893) for certain lands, in which the said Railway Company are the persons interested, situate in the said County Borough containing Sixty and a half square yards and being part of No. 311 on the said ordnance man subject to tbe reservation of minerals contained in the Railway Clauses Consolidation Act 1845. Any person entitled to any Estate In or to any chaige upon the said lands, or any part thereof, who dissents from the proposed Exchange must vi vs notice of such dissent in writing to the Boara of Agricnitore and Fisheries, on or before the third day of March next. 1,43 T. H. ELLIOTT, Secretary. 3, St. James's Square, London,, S. W. 25th January, 1906.</p>
        <p>COUNTY BOROUGH OF WARRINGTON.— WILD BIRDS PROTECTION. NOTICE IS HEREBY GIVEN that the folio wing is a copy of an Order ma.le by the Secretary of State on the 30th day of May, 1904 with reference to the Protection of Wild Birds in the County Borough of Warrington, viz : WILD BIRD (RNOLAND). THE WILD BIRDS PROTECTION. (COUNTY BOROUGH OF WARRIRGTON) ORDER 1904. DATED 30TH MAY, 1904. In pursuance of the powers conferred on me by the Wild Birds Protection Acts 1880 to 1904, and noon application by the County Borough of Warrington, I hereby;make the fol- lowing Order -AU TITLE. I.—This Order may be cited as "The Wild Birds'Protection (County Borough of Warrington) Order 1934." BIRDS. Additions to the Schedule of the ket of 1830. H.—The provisions of the Wild Birds Protection Act,lBBo, shall apply within the County Borough of Warrington to the following species of Wild Birds in the same manner as if those species were included In the Schedule to the Act : Blackbird, Cirl Bunting. Corn Bunting, Reed or Black- headed Bunting, Buzzard, Chiffchaff, Tree Creeper, Dipper' or Water Ouzel, Peregrine Faloon, Pied Fly- catcher, SpottedlFlyeatcher,Goldcrest, Hedge Sparrow. Heron, Hobby, Kestrel, Linnet, House Martin, Sand Martin, Kerlin, Nuthatch, Osprey, Meadow Pipit. Rock Pipit, Tree Pipit, Raven, Lesser Redpole. Red- start, Robin, Stonechat, Swallow, Swift, Song Thrush, Longtailed Tit, Greg Wagtail, Yellow Wagtail. El tick- cap Warbler. Garden Warbler, Grasshopper Warbler, Reed Warbler, Sedge Warbler, Willow Warbler, Wood Warbler. Wheat ear, Whinchat, Whitethroat, Lesser Whitethroat, Wren, Wryneek. ALL BIRDS PROTECTED DURING THE WHOLE OF THE YEAR WITHIN A CERTAIN AREA. HE—During that period of the year to which the pro- tection afforded by the Wild Birds Protection Act, 1883, does not apply, the killing or taking of Wild Birds is pro- hibited in the River Mersey within the County Borough ot Warrington. This area Is shown by blue hatched lines on a map deposited with the Town Clerk. sealed with the Horne Office:Seal, and dated the dun May, 19)4. EGGS. CERTAIN EGGS PROTECTED THROUGHOUT THE BOROUGH. IV.—The taking or destroying of the eggs of the follow- ing species of Wild Birds is prohibited in the County Borough of Warrington : Bittern. Clrl Bunting. Corn Bunting. Reed or Black- headed Bunting, Buzzard. Tree Creeper, Dipper or Water Ouzel. Peregrine Falcon, Pied Flycatcher. Spotted Flycatcher, Gold Crest, Goldfinch, Black- headed Gull. Havetinoh, Heron, Hobby, Kestrel, King- fisher, Linnet, Mallard, Merlin, Nightjar, Nuthatch, Osprey, Owl, Meadow Pipit, Rock Pipit Tree Pipit, Golden Plover, Raven, Lesser Redpole, Redstart, Sheldrake, Skylark, Snipe, Stonechat. Teal, Tern, Longtail Tit, Grey Wagtail, Yellow Wagtail, Blackcap Warbler, Garden Warbler, Grasshopper Warbler, Reed Warbler, Sedge Warbler, Willow Warbler, Wood Warbler. Wheatear, Whinchat. Whitethroat, Lesser Whitethroat, Widgeon, Woodcock, Woodlark. Wood- pecker. Wren. Wryneek, and (after Hay lit in each year) Lapwing, Peewit or Green Plover. Given under my hand at Whitehall, this 30th day of May, 1904. A. AKERS DOUGLAS, One of His Majesty's Principal Secretaries of State. J. LYON WHITTLE. Town Clerk. Town Hall, Warrington. 2nd F ebbruary, 1906.</p>
