        <p>_ A 0 N F L DISCOVERY.—This is the age of research and experiment, when all nai,ure, so to speak, is ransacked by the aJlentinc for the comfort and happiness of man. Fcience has indeed m•de giant strides during the past century, and among the -by no means least importat t—discoveries in medicine cones that of THHUAPION This preparation is unquestionably one of the most genuine and reliable Patent Me iiclnes ever Introduced, and has we understand, been used in the Continental Hospitals by ' [Mord, Rostan, Jobert. Volpeau, Maisonnenve the well- known Chassaignae, and indeed by all who are regarded as Authorities in such mattors, including the celebrated Lallemand, and Bator, by whom it was some time since uniformly adopted.and that it is worthy the attention of those who require such a reuredv we think there is no doubt. From the time of Aristotle downwards a potent agent in the removal of these diseases has (like the famed philosopher's stone) been the object of search of some hopeful generous minds ; and far beyond the mere power—if such could ever have been discovered—of transmuting the baser metals into gold is surely the discovery of a remedy so potent as to re- plenish the falling energies in the one case. and in the other so effectually, speedily and safely to expel horn the system the poisons of acquired or inherited disease In all their potent forms as to leave no taint or trace behind. Such is the new French Remedy THBRAPION, which may certainly rank with, if not take precedence of, many of the discoveries of our day, about which no little ostentation end noise bays been made, and the extensive and ever-increasing demand that has been created for this medicine wherever introduced appears to prove that it is destined CO cast into oblivion all those questionable remedies that were formerly the sole reliance of medical men. Therapion may be obtained of the principal chemists and merchants,throughout the world.— " Diamond Fields Adverti•zer."—Rimberle