        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT. I clean. Detachable collars are being provided with I many of the new white lace, chiffon or silk blouses. They are built for the most part of narrow black velvet aged on either side with a tiny frill of black Chantilly The Spanish Royal Marriage.—Princess Ena of lace, the top frill being softened by an under-pleating Battenberg, whose approaching marriage with the King of white kilted lesse. of Spain is attracting ac much attention, was born at A Millinery Device.—With the new floral toques— Balmoral in October, 1887—Queen Victoria's Jubilee violets and roses being se far the favourite blooms— year—and the official announcement recording the event, milliners are providing little tortoiseshell pins, by stated that it was the first instance of a member of the means of which the hair is caught here and there to Royal Family being born in Scotland since the year these floral erections. What with pinning our hair to 1600. The christening also took place at Balmoral, a our headgear, and adding little bunches of curls both to month later, the little Princess taking the second name our hats and our heads, the removal of a hat or toque, from the Empress Eugenie, who was one of the sponsors. I save in the privacy of our apartment, is now impossible. The Empress has alw..ys taken the greatest interest in I MADGE. the welfare of her godchild, upon whom she has bestowed many rare and valuable gifts. Naturally she is much absorbed in the arrangements for the Princess's marriage with the King of Spain, for the ex-Empress is herself half-Spanish. She was born in Granada in 1826, the daughter of a Spanish Duke, whilst her mother— whose maiden name was Miss Kirkpatrick—was a Scots- woman. Like her Godmother, Princess Ena is called upon to share a Throne, an event which everyone will hope will prove of happy omen to Spain, and her future Queen. Princess Ena's Conversion.—Princess Ena's baptism was according to the simple rites of the Scottish Presbyterian Church, bac she will necessarily embrace Roman Catholicism before her marriage with the King of Spain, and for which change she will be prepared by Dr. Bourne, Archbishop of Westminster. A similar corKersion was once contemplated by her mother Princess Beatrice, when there was every likelihood that she would marry the late Prince Imperial whose death in Zululand in 1879 was the crowning sorrow of his widowed mother. Instead of marrying a Frenchman, Princess Beatrice married a German, who, with the aaine desire to fight our battles, went to Ashanti, and there fell a victim to pestilence. That was in 1896, Ivilhert Princess Ena was scarcely old enough to realise -toe Poignancy of grief. but her mother has never ceased mourn her husband, and has his privately printed diary,n almost daily, in her hands. 11113 Royal Palaces of Spain.—Few Sovereigns can boast the possession of so many Royal Palaces, as the 3rnlitig, King of Spain, and his bride will have the choice 9f Many beautiful homes. The Palacis Real in Madrid ls an immense building, and is decorated in the most costly and magnificent style, but the climate of Madrid iS not an agreeable one, being very cold in the winter, and spring, and extremely hot in the summer. The Palace of El Pardo, which is about seven miles from the capital is also a magnificent bulling, and contains many Priceless treasures, among its works of art. Still more attractive is the summer Palace of Aranjnez, about thirty miles from Madrid, and it was here that the King Spent most of his days when a boy, in the open air. Miramir is the residence_ of the Qneen Mother, and the highly fashionable San 6chastian is the Osborne of Spain. There is also the Castle of Seville one of the most ancient of the royal palaces of the Kings of Spain whose body guard are still dressed in mail armour, and armed with halberds just the same as for many centuries Past. Their Majesties' State Engagements.—The King and 'Queen have several engagements in London during February, of which the most important will be the two Pro-Easter Courts at Buckingham Palace, and the State (Miming of Parliament. Sa many changes have taken Place at the elections, and the fact that it is a new Parliament will give an added interest to these pro- ceedings, but a State opening of Parliament—so rare during the reign of our late Queen—has somewhat lost its novelty, and there is not the rush of peers and peeresses to witness the ceremony as was the case in 1902. There will also be a very small royal gathering on the forthcoming occasion, owing to th 3 fact that so many members of the Royal Familyare abroad,and there will be no procession from Marlborongh House, the Prince and Princess of Woles being still abroad. The Duke and Duchess of Connaught and Princess Patricia are in South Africa, Princess Louise Duchess of Argyll is making a long stay in Egypt, and Prince Arthur of Connaught, and Princess Louise of Schleswig-Holstein, are both on a visit to.the East. For the time being,the Princess Roy3l and Princess Victoria are their Majesties' chief support, in the immediate Royal circle. Women Voters at the General Election.—Some five • or six women have succeeded in voting at the General Election by season of their names appearing by mistake on the register. Probably no more will be heard of these cases, because one vote more or less makes little differ- ence, but if the majority was a narrow one, and a i scrutiny was ordered, there s no doubt that these votes would be disallowed. As the law stands they are illegal, and though the advocates for giving the Parliamentary vote to women have made some stir at the General Elec- tion, women's franchise is not likely to be one of the reforms attempted by the new Government. The Courts.—With the dates fixed for the pre-Easter Courts, leading modistes are becoming busy, for what- ever may be their custom with regard to ordinary toilettes, few women order their Court gowns late or hurriedly. The old regulations still prevail—feathers and train being obligatory, but as regards the cut of the decolletage, and style of the sleeve, the rules are less i rigid than n the Victorian days. A leading light in the modistlo world is in favour of the train carried out in lace, or some gauzy kind of fabric mounted on clouds of chiffon, and decrees that it shall match, and not form a contrast to, the gown with which it is worn It would seem as though velvet brocade, and heavy silks, will be little in evidence at the early Courts. An Old Fashion Revived.—The old-world black velvet braclet is promised a great vogue this spring, an item likely to rejoice the heart of the girl or woman who lacks a supply of jewellery. Quaint old cameos, minia- tures, or buckles, are being used as clasps, but, failing tkese, a dainty little brooch—minus its pin, or pendant, may be stitched to the velvet, in such a manner as to hide the fastenings. Unquestionably the velvet bracelet has the effect of making the wrist look slighter,and the hand whiter, moreover it sets off to the very best advantage, any jewels mounted upon it. Neck Frilling.—A return to the lesse "neck frilling" of some fifteen years ago, seems not improbable, many of the new cashmere, voile,pr other light-weight gowns far spring wear being finisad off round the neck with a narrow ruching of chiffon, kilted frills of lesse, or closely gauffered lace. We all know how becoming this line of white about the throat is, moreover these neck frillings tend to keep the lining and edge of the collar</p>
        <p>'AN ILLUSTRATED TOUR THROUGH MESSRS. GARTONS AGRICULTURAL EXPERIMENT GROUNDS." Such is the title of a beautifully got up publication by this well-known firm of agricultural seed experts and growers. The many phases of seed breeding from the time that the crosses are actually made through the various stages of sowing, selecting, harvesting, thresh- ing, &amp;c., are all fully dealt with by beautiful reproduc- tions from genuine photographs taken at Messrs. Gartons' grounds. It is a long time since we have seen anything so interesting to agriculturists and Messrs. Gartons are to be complimented upon the production of this beautiful and interesting work.</p>
