        <p>THE EXAMINEE,. FEBRUARY 3rd 1906.</p>
        <p>NOTES AND NOTIONS. 1 , We hear that there are revelations of a most serious, nature in the report by Mr. Garside, C.E., on the Waterworks undertaking.. * We are to have waste- paper baskets in Bridge-street and Sankey-street shortly. Beautiful Arrington " is materialising—very slowly. * * * Stockton Heath ia going to bask in Warrington's reflected glow. In other words terms have been agreed upon for the gas-lighting of the village by the Corporation. * * * The Queen's Unemployed Fund has already benefited to the tune of £6,000 from the sale of" The Queen's Carol." A further £l,OOO is anticipated, from the same source. We are glad that the movement for the establish- ment of branch savings banks in Warrington is pro- gressing, and that a new branch will be opened in Green-street, Bank Quay, next Friday afternoon. * * * It behoves Free Traders not to relax their efforts, but to continue to guard the cause th-v have rescue&amp; while on the Free Trade Government lies the responsi- bility of making Free Trade principles the inspiration of their policy as a whole.:" Spectator." Winter bathing is evidently popular in Warrington. The Bathkeeper informed the Town Hall, Park and Baths Committee at its last meeting that the income from the baths for the past four weeks was £32 Is. 10d., and for the corresponding period last year £22 17s. •6d. ; increase, £o4s. 4d. * * * "General Buller, 35, Horsernarket-street, Warring- ton," was the address given by William Duffey who was charged at Chester on Wednesday with travelling from Manchester to Chester without Shaving paid his fare. The magistrates, however, failed to see any resemblance which identified the prisoner with the . ' distinguished soldier and the usual fine was imposed. * * * The "Christian World " states that there are in the new Parliament (exclusive of Scottish Presbyterians), 176 Nonconformists, of whom 73 have won seats from Conservatives. In the last Parliament there were only 69 Nonconformists, and the present representa- tion of the Free Churches in .Parliament is the largest in their history. * * * The Labour Party has been charged with receiving generous contribations towards the expanses of the election campain from Mr. Carnegie. The millionaire, however, has stated that there is not a word of truth in the statement. What will the Labour Party be charged with next, we wonder ? * * * It seems that Sir John Brunner was offered an unopposed return by the Conservatives if he would promise to abstain from helping other Cheshire Liberals. This was a compliment to Sir John's influ- ence, but it was also a bribe which he promptly refused. We are glad to hear that his injured leg is progressing favOurably, though it will be some time [ before he can use ,it properly. . * A * Mr. Alan de Tatton Egerton has issued a farewell 1 ' letter to the electors of the Knutsford division. He announces that it is not his intention to seek re-election. For the" deluded voters " who believed " misleading - Posters and leaflets " he prays "a restoration to a il_raller frame of mind." To those who voted against ° r..._.rie trnionist cause he extends his pity.--It still hurts "'deatly. Poor Alan! '** * • . 10,0-Csat'rlYietWiyimborne's dinner party of March 2nd will , speaking, an adjourned meeting of the V, isanisers of the Liberal victory. It was at Lady e rraborne's hospitable table that the successful plan of . -arl?Paign was finally arranged and the fate of the 1 L'alonist Free Traders irrevocably sealed before the . .I._dislolritifin took place. A typically mr!naging.Churchill, ..ifdaY Wimborne is now the most influential woman , 'n the political world, and if her nephew, Mr. Winston Churchill, fulfills the promise of his youth, she will • Yet live to be aunt to a Prime Minister. - * 5 * The Bishop of Liverpool, at a meeting of the • tenefices' Augmentation Fund supporters, laid e ; mphasis on the fact that clergymen with so-called 41 fat livings " were oftentimes little better off than their brethren who were less highly placed, so many were the demands made upon their finances. His Lnrcl''lliP rather startled his hearers when he added, " I myself had more money to spend when I was a .2rate with £l5O a yea/. than I have to-day." The ii'lshop's remarks may have been intended to console the lean iterate, but we imagine the latter would be ~•'' quite willing to risk a" fat living ."if he could get e • * A * The reconstruction of the Conservative party is t' easing much squabbling. The leadership of Mr. i Balfour s questioned, and the Tariff Reformers are determined to give him his marching orders, and to " Put Mr. Chamberlain per ein his stead. We are not • surprised ;in fact, we should not be surprised at , anything now that Mr. Chamberlain's supporters have started to educate the Conservatives as to where their duty lies. Mr. Balfour has been a tool, for a long time, humbugand now that he cannot humbug the people any longer .. , another must be tried. Wo Sall see what we shall ee. ** 5 * a • There is a noble earl, says the "Sketch," Who 'entertains a good deal at his country house, but who ,eannot quite suppress his private opinions even to 'satisfy his guests. As everybody knows, the modern Ivanlan loves to have lunch fully dressed, with her it on. The earl cannot reconcile himself to this aabit, and the other day he gave a hint to his hatted t - Ists that they will not forget in a hurry. When 7 trooped into lunch wearing all their headgear he teed himself, vanished for a moment, and directly • -r the butler and footmen came in to do the wait- lag with their tall hats on. History does not record What the ladies said when my lord was not listening. * * * While strenuous efforts are being made by the Beautiful Warrington Society to render the town more attractive by the planting of trees, other people, apparently with a less regard for nature, have been busy uith destructive weapons. At osse time Wilders- Pool-causeway boasted of a number of trees but the last of these has disappeared by the cutting down of the three giants which for years have stood boldly near the Wilderspool Brewery. The Town Hall, Parks and Baths Committee of the Corporation have at -last i determined to prevent, if possible, any further depre- dations and have resolved that no trees in any of the public streeta shall in future be cut down without the consent of this Committee. •* * * Those unhappy beings, the ex-members of Parlia- ment, who never expected to be defeated, will (says the London correspondent of the "Dispatch ") fetel acutely the wrench from old ties and associatioaa by one simple, valedictory act at Westminster. This is the emptying of their lockers and the handing over of their keys to the Sergeant-at-Arms. It will be a Poignant moment for some old members, this yielding 11P of the last bit of their Parliamentary territory, and clearing out of their accumulation of political docu- ment% notes of old speeches, speeches delivered and never to be delivered, treasured scram of paper that rimy, alas, are useless, dead leaves of the past. Borne of the Conservative members who knew that their seats wet, " shaky " took the precaution to empty their lockers at the close of last session, knowing that °the dissolution would come upon them, as Mr. Balfour said, "like a thief in the night." But the. majority, serene and confident in their electoral marrity, left 1 accumulations of a long Parliamentary life in their zers, and now will have to endure the last pang of ' " dissolution."</p>
        <p>Of the thirteen members of the Fabian Society who have stood as candidates at the General Election, no less than seven have been successful. * * * Mr. J. Kier Hardie, M.P. says that " the case for Labour Representation is so unanswerable that if it were to be opposed on its merits its opponents would be simply nowhere." * * ' Mr. John Burns' attire seems to be troubling a lot lof people just now. If he dared to walk the street in .a frock coat and silk hat what a.- many" I told you so's " would be heard. But " John "has more under his bowler hat than mere sentiment. * * * It would be a very fitting and graceful act for the Warrington Liberals to recognise Mr. Frank Roberts' generous gift of the Liberal Club by inviting him to a complimentary dinner and presenting him with an address of thanks. * * * The President of the Local Government Board, Mr. John Burns, has refused to support the proposal of the Guardians of Lambeth to purchase an estate of 567 acres for the purpose of establishing a farm colony and sanatorium, as the experiment, he thinks, would not merit the large expenditure which the scheme would involve. * * * Debate and public speaking are essentials in the training of young men. We recommend all those who are anxious to help, and to be helped in progressive ideals to communicate with the Hon. Secretaries of the Junior Liberal Association. Meetings are held weekly, and members can be enrolled at any time. * * * "If Mr. Chamberlain were to suddenly resume his Championship of Home Rule, or once again boldly advocate the restoration of the Boer republics in South Africa—two things he most likely will do—he would carry every one of the seven Birmingham seats enthu- siastically with him. The Birmingham people have not the least sentiment of opposition towards Home Rule in Ireland or Boer Rule in the Transvaal. The poople of Birmingham most certainly believe in Home Rule for Birmingham."—" Labour Leader." * * * * * * The Wariington Police Buildings are neither com- pleted nor paid for yet. A loan of £3,735 has just been sanctioned by the Local Government Board for excess expenditure. This sum is, however, .£1,032 less than the Corporation asked for, numerous deductions having been made by the .tsoard. Such little items as £3l for 12 chairs in the Court,' £lO for a side board, and £lB for a standing desk are described as" unduly expensive for a Police Court," and struck out. The Local Government Board is gradually getting some idea of the palatial character of Warrington's Court of Justice. Ratepayers have been sadly aware of it for too long a time already.</p>
