        <p>~..... - • 1/ , 5f.:!.:4%' ' ----=t--.7 --4-11 I:4_ /• v —.7, %, 1 `VA. irigigigt . °)..:4. •:,..?:„.fi'7.-,::: • °:5..•;, • -.••••.:-..., - .-' "•-• ~, • ' ,•• • -''' ' ... f .'; ;....,. ~ °'. ' ' V ...:- I.tv- ........er,r,...". , ~.._. '- i .16 t L1ER,,,R.,::;::::;:7f;7!--:_:;,•,:::''. - a i 44::,..:...- ; • 4-,11.... , i . ''' ~,,-.- .f, 1; ~..., ! :',...r4. '''...:.1.::14.;5. &amp;'-:-;','Ar.-1, -4,1•414,.,„,"'""...mi...-.:-----4*-- ----- _.'---:-,' et- .4 • -- - 4‘. -,,;,... i:4I)A4-I=-7------- I Nd , Iri , • • AN,. ~"*001g.;:'•;'...".• ~. I,olsk UnCie 1 , .. y • --,- i.=;,t, v....13y _ ____ ~.......\- , - ~', rtl, °'" • P '. 1 ' ,k%;\,.\ :000-4 ;. iik 4.1' '` ' 1 / "Shall I tell you a story about a certain New Year's Eve that happened to me when I was about your age, Cissy?" "Oh! yes, please do," we all exclaimed o 711.--1 frysli in chorus. W 111111. PooV'PY i "Well," began grandma, "it was just such F a night as this—snow and frost, and bitter cold. I had been sent to my maiden aunt, because all my sisters were ill with measles. There was nothing that I disliked more than to be sent to this aunt, for she was what you would call crusty; but I dared not say it, for in my time, if children spoke against any of our relatives, we were punished. ° "My aunt had been more cross than ever the day previous to New Year's Eve. She had made me nearly mad with her worry- cs!tfl 'rip op lou lsntu I 2upics `sAcm 2111 nor the other. At last I could bear it no longer, and I threw a book straight at her head, and said I hated her, and I never wished to see her again. How I have re- pented those words since! At this she went in a dreadful passion, and said things I do not care to repeat to you, though I remember that terrible day as clearly as if it was yesterday. "When I went to bed I vowed to myself I would run away and hide in a cave, which ran a long way underground, and had several little caverns where you could sleep. This cave I decided to stay in for one night, and go away at daybreak. I had enough money to pay for my journey home. I got out my bag and packed all I wanted, and hid it under my bed, ready to start. "At last, when aunt told me to go and tidy for tea, I quickly got my coat and hat, and locked the door. I took two blankets from my bed—one to lay on, and the other ' to cover, myself with. I took the bag from under the bed, and put some biscuits in it. I opened the window, and listened if the coast was clear. I let my bag down first. and then I climbed down myself; but it was more difficult than I imagined, for my coat kept catching in the ivy with which the well was covered. At last I reached the ground, and was just starting to run, when I heard a step, and k) I crouched down, and waited, and waited. At last it died away, and I ran as fast as my legs would let me, for I was fearful of the consequences if I was caught. "At last I reached the cave. I had. been careful to take a small lantern, which I had got from the stable-boy, and I found it ‘,ery, useful, as the cave was very dark. As I lit it, I remembered that I had left my light burning. Suppose the wind should blow the light against the curtain and set it on fire? The thought filled me with horror and dread. I thought it must be time, to prepare for the night ; so I spread the blanket on the ground, and tried to get some sleep." (To be Concluded.)</p>
